insert into concept (slug, name, area_slug, kind, language, framework, brief, lesson, content_status) values
  ('bytes-vs-characters', 'Bytes vs characters', 'text-encodings', 'foundation', null, null, 'A byte is a unit of storage. A character is a unit of text. In UTF-8 one character takes one to four bytes.', null, 'reviewed'),
  ('explicit-charsets', 'Explicit charsets', 'text-encodings', 'foundation', null, null, 'Decoding bytes needs a named encoding. Relying on a platform default breaks when the tool runs elsewhere.', '{"how_it_works":"Decoding needs an encoding. If code does not name one, the language picks a default, and defaults differ: the\noperating system''s setting, the user''s locale, or a fixed value that changed between versions. Java used the\nplatform encoding until Java 18, so the same program decoded files as Windows-1252 on many Windows machines and\nas UTF-8 on Linux. Since Java 18 the default is UTF-8, but older code and other languages still vary.\n\nThe safe habit is to name the encoding every time bytes become text or text becomes bytes, usually UTF-8. Then\nthe program gives the same answer on a laptop, a server and a CI runner.\n\nSometimes the encoding has to be discovered. Protocols state it: HTTP in the Content-Type header, XML in its\ndeclaration, email in its headers. Plain files usually say nothing. A byte order mark at the start is a strong\nhint: EF BB BF for UTF-8, FF FE or FE FF for UTF-16. Without one a program can only guess, which is why formats\nsuch as JSON simply require UTF-8.\n\nWhen a UTF-8 file does start with a byte order mark, decide what to do with it. Most readers should skip it,\nsince it is not part of the text.","examples":["A CSV saved by Excel as CSV UTF-8 starts with EF BB BF. A script that does not skip it reads the first column name as an invisible character followed by name, and lookups by that name fail.","A program that shows é correctly on a developer''s Mac shows Ã© on a Windows build server, because it decoded with the default encoding instead of UTF-8.","A web page served with the wrong charset in its Content-Type header shows mojibake, although the file itself is fine."],"misconceptions":["A text file records its own encoding. Most do not, so the reader must know or guess.","The default encoding is always UTF-8. It depends on the language, its version and the machine.","A byte order mark is harmless. Many tools treat it as part of the first line."],"elsewhere":["Database connections have their own encoding, and a mismatch corrupts names on the way in.","Command-line tools read the locale to decide how to decode, so the same command can count differently on two machines.","APIs that accept text should say which encoding they expect, and reject or convert anything else."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('decoding-across-chunks', 'Decoding across chunk boundaries', 'text-encodings', 'foundation', null, null, 'A chunk of bytes can end in the middle of a character, so a decoder keeps the leftover bytes for the next chunk.', '{"how_it_works":"A streaming program reads bytes in chunks, and chunk boundaries fall wherever the buffer happens to fill. In\nUTF-8 a character can take up to four bytes, so a chunk can end after the first byte of é or after two bytes of\nan emoji.\n\nIf each chunk is decoded on its own, the decoder sees an unfinished sequence at the end of one chunk and stray\ncontinuation bytes at the start of the next. It reports both as invalid, usually replacing each with the\nreplacement character U+FFFD, so one real character becomes two wrong ones. The bug depends on where the boundary\nfalls: the same file can count correctly with one buffer size and wrongly with another, and small test files that\nfit in one chunk never show it.\n\nThe fix is a decoder that keeps state between chunks. It decodes every complete character in a chunk and holds\nback the bytes of an unfinished one, then puts them in front of the next chunk. At the very end of the input, any\nbytes still held back are a truncated character, which is invalid. Languages provide such decoders, as a reader\nthat wraps a byte stream or as a decoder object that accepts partial input.\n\nByte-level counts such as bytes and newlines need no decoding at all, because in UTF-8 a newline byte never\nappears inside a multi-byte character.","examples":["A tool counts characters correctly on a short test file but reports a few extra on a large Chinese text, where three-byte characters often straddle the 4096-byte boundary.","A chat server that decodes each network packet separately sometimes shows a broken symbol in the middle of a message with emoji, whenever a packet boundary splits one.","A log shipper that cuts files into fixed-size pieces for upload must cut on line boundaries, or each piece can no longer be decoded on its own."],"misconceptions":["Decoding chunk by chunk is the same as decoding the whole file. Only when no character crosses a boundary.","A bigger buffer fixes it. It only makes boundaries rarer, so the bug hides better.","Counting bytes and lines needs decoding too. In UTF-8 newline bytes can be counted on the raw bytes."],"elsewhere":["Anything that carries text over a stream, such as chat, WebSockets or server-sent events, needs a decoder that keeps state.","Compression and encryption streams have the same shape: state is carried from one block to the next.","Editors that open huge files in pieces must align the pieces to character boundaries."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('streaming-constant-memory', 'Streaming in constant memory', 'streams-io', 'foundation', null, null, 'Read input in fixed-size chunks and keep running totals, so memory does not grow with the input.', '{"how_it_works":"A program that reads a whole file into memory before working on it needs as much memory as the file is big.\nThat is fine for a 2 KB file and fatal for a 20 GB log on a machine with 8 GB of memory: the program slows to a\ncrawl as the system swaps, or the operating system stops it.\n\nA streaming program keeps a fixed-size buffer, perhaps 64 KB, fills it with the next part of the input, updates\na few running totals and reuses the same buffer for the next part. Its memory depends on the buffer and the\ntotals, not on the input, so it is the same for 2 KB and for 20 GB. The time still grows with the input, since\nevery byte is read once, but the memory does not.\n\nNot every question can be answered this way. Counting bytes, lines and words, finding the longest line or adding\nup a column needs only a few numbers carried from one chunk to the next. Counting distinct words, sorting lines or\nfinding the median needs memory that grows with the input, because the answer depends on everything seen so far.\nThe skill is to ask, before writing code, what state the answer has to carry.\n\nStreaming also lets a program produce output before its input has finished arriving, which is what makes pipes\nbetween tools work.","examples":["Counting the lines of a 20 GB web server log with a streaming counter takes a few megabytes of memory and finishes as fast as the disk can be read. Loading it first fails on most laptops.","A video player decodes a few seconds ahead and throws away what it has shown, so a two-hour film never sits in memory at once.","Database backups are written and read as streams, so the size of the database is not limited by the memory of the machine that makes the backup."],"misconceptions":["Reading line by line is always streaming. It is, until the code collects the lines into a list.","A bigger buffer is always faster. Beyond some tens of kilobytes the disk is the limit, and more buffer only costs memory.","Streaming is only for huge files. The same code handles small files and pipes, so there is no reason to write two versions."],"elsewhere":["HTTP servers stream request and response bodies, so a large upload does not need to fit in memory.","grep, gzip, checksum programs and the merge phase of sort all stream.","Data pipelines are designed around what state they must keep per record."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('stdin-vs-file', 'Standard input vs file', 'streams-io', 'foundation', null, null, 'A file and standard input can be treated as the same kind of byte stream, so one loop serves both.', '{"how_it_works":"Unix tools are built to be joined. A tool given a file name opens and reads that file; a tool given no name reads\nstandard input, which is whatever the shell connects to it: the keyboard, a file redirected into it, or the output\nof another program through a pipe. Many tools also accept a single dash as a name that means standard input.\n\nA file and standard input are both byte streams, read in the same way under the same read contract. So a tool\nshould open the right source and hand it to one shared counting loop, not keep one code path for files and\nanother for pipes.\n\nSome things are true only of files. A file has a size that can be asked for without reading it, and a position\nthat can be moved back. A pipe has neither: its size is unknown until the writer finishes, and data once read is\ngone. A tool that takes a shortcut that only works on files, such as asking for the size instead of counting\nbytes, breaks the moment it is used in a pipeline.\n\nThe output side mirrors this: a tool writes its results to standard output, so the next tool in a pipeline, or a\nfile, can receive them.","examples":["Piping a compressed log through a decompressor into wc counts its lines without ever writing the 20 GB of uncompressed text to disk.","wc prints the file name next to its counts, but reading a pipe there is no name to print, so it prints the counts alone.","A program that rewinds its input to read it twice works on files and fails, or hangs, on a pipe."],"misconceptions":["Standard input is the keyboard. It is whatever the shell connects to the program.","A pipe is a temporary file. It is a buffer in the operating system, with no size and no rewind.","Supporting pipes needs a separate code path. One loop over a byte stream serves both."],"elsewhere":["Compilers, formatters and build tools read standard input so editors and scripts can pipe code through them.","Containers and services write logs to standard output so the platform can collect the stream.","Network sockets behave like pipes: no size and no rewind."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('stream-read-contract', 'The read contract', 'streams-io', 'foundation', null, null, 'A read can return fewer bytes than the buffer holds. The returned count says how many are valid, and -1 means the end.', '{"how_it_works":"When a program asks the operating system to read into a buffer, it says how many bytes it wants at most. The\nread returns how many it actually delivered, which can be anything from one to the size of the buffer. Only\nthose bytes are valid. The rest of the buffer still holds whatever was there before, often bytes from the\nprevious read.\n\nA short read does not mean the input has ended. A file usually fills the buffer until its last part, but a pipe\ndelivers whatever the writing program has produced so far, and a network socket whatever packets have arrived.\nOnly a special result says the end has been reached: zero bytes in C and most operating systems, or minus one\nfrom an InputStream in Java.\n\nSo a correct read loop follows three rules. Use the returned count, never the buffer size, for everything done\nwith the data. Keep reading after a short read. Stop only on the end marker. A loop that breaks the first rule\ncounts leftover bytes again. A loop that breaks the second stops early on pipes and networks while appearing to\nwork on files, which is why the bug often survives testing.","examples":["A 10 KB file read with a 4 KB buffer usually comes back as 4 KB, 4 KB and 2 KB, then the end marker. A program that always adds 4 KB reports 12 KB.","When a slow program writes into a pipe a line at a time, each read may return one short line, although much more is coming.","Network code that expects one read to return one whole message works on a fast local network and fails when a message is split across packets."],"misconceptions":["A read fills the buffer unless the input is over. It can return fewer bytes at any time.","Getting fewer bytes than asked for means the end. Only the end marker means the end.","The bytes beyond the count are zero. They are left over from earlier reads, so counting them adds real-looking data."],"elsewhere":["Sockets follow the same contract, which is why network protocols need message framing.","Every language wraps it, for example InputStream in Java, the read call in C and streams in Node.","Writes have a matching contract: a write can accept fewer bytes than offered, and the rest must be written again."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('line-endings', 'Lines and line endings', 'text-encodings', 'foundation', null, null, 'A line is the text up to and including a newline byte, so tools count lines by counting newlines. A last line with no newline after it is not counted. Files written on Windows end each line with a carriage return and a newline, and the carriage return is an extra byte that line counting ignores but byte counting sees.', '{"how_it_works":"A file has no lines, only bytes. A line is a convention: in Unix, the text up to a newline byte, 0A. POSIX\ndefines a line as characters followed by a newline, so a file whose last line has no newline ends with an\nincomplete line. wc counts newline bytes, so for such a file it reports one line fewer than an editor shows.\n\nWindows ends each line with two bytes, a carriage return 0D and then a newline 0A, a habit from typewriters and\nteleprinters, where returning the carriage and feeding the paper were separate actions. Classic Mac systems used\na carriage return alone. Most modern tools accept both, but not all.\n\nThe carriage return causes quiet bugs. Counting lines by newline still works, but the carriage return stays at\nthe end of each line''s text, so a comparison with yes fails because the value is really yes followed by an\ninvisible character. It is also a byte, and whitespace for word counting. Git can convert line endings on\ncheckout, which changes a file''s byte count between Windows and Linux machines.\n\nSplitting a whole text at its newlines also produces an empty last piece when the file ends with a newline, and\ncode must not treat that piece as a real line.","examples":["A CSV exported on Windows and read on Linux keeps a carriage return at the end of every last column, so a filter for the value active matches nothing.","An editor shows 10 lines and wc -l reports 9, because the last line has no newline. Each is right by its own definition.","A test that checks the byte count of a fixture passes on Linux and fails on a Windows CI runner, because Git converted the line endings on checkout."],"misconceptions":["A line ends however the operating system ends it. Files move between systems, so a tool must handle both endings.","The number of lines is the number of newlines plus one. Not for a file that ends with a newline, and not for an empty one.","The carriage return is harmless because it is invisible. It changes comparisons, lengths and byte counts."],"elsewhere":["HTTP, SMTP and many other protocols require a carriage return and a newline at the end of every header line.","Git''s autocrlf setting and attributes file exist to control this conversion.","Diff tools show a whole file as changed when only its line endings changed."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('word-state-machine', 'Counting words with a state machine', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A word counter walks the text once and remembers one thing: whether it is inside a word. A word starts when a non-space character follows a space or the start of input, so runs of spaces never make empty words. The remembered state has to survive from one chunk of input to the next.', '{"how_it_works":"What is a word? wc''s answer is simple and strict: a word is a run of characters that are not whitespace.\nPunctuation is part of the word, so don''t, e-mail and 3.14 are each one word, and a dash with spaces around it is\na word of its own. Spell checkers and search engines use the more complex word boundary rules of Unicode; wc does\nnot.\n\nThat definition fits a tiny state machine. The counter remembers one thing: whether it is inside a word. Reading\na non-space while outside a word starts a word, so the count goes up and the state becomes inside. Reading\nwhitespace makes the state outside. Runs of spaces therefore never create empty words, and leading or trailing\nspaces do not matter.\n\nThe state has to survive chunk boundaries. If a word straddles two chunks and the counter forgets that it was\ninside a word, the second half counts as a new word. Carrying one flag from one chunk to the next fixes it, and\nthat flag is the whole state.\n\nThe hard part is deciding what whitespace is. Space, tab, newline, carriage return, form feed and vertical tab\nare the classic set. Unicode adds others, such as the no-break space U+00A0, the em space and the ideographic\nspace used in Chinese and Japanese text. Which of them count depends on the tool, its version and the locale, so\nthe same file can have different word counts on two machines.","examples":["The phrase rock-and-roll is one word to wc and three to a person or a search engine.","Text pasted from a web page often has no-break spaces between words, and a counter that does not treat them as whitespace reports fewer words than the author expects.","Japanese text usually has no spaces between words, so wc counts whole sentences as single words."],"misconceptions":["Counting words means counting spaces and adding one. Double spaces, leading spaces and empty files break that.","A word is what a dictionary calls a word. For wc it is any run of non-whitespace.","Whitespace means the space bar. Tabs, newlines and several Unicode characters count too, depending on the locale."],"elsewhere":["Lexers in compilers use the same idea with more states: inside a number, a string or a comment.","Editors and search engines use the Unicode word boundary rules to select and index words.","Protocol parsers carry a small state across network packets in exactly this way."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('differential-testing', 'Testing against a reference tool', 'process-testing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Run your tool and a trusted reference on the same inputs and compare the outputs. Your own expected values only test what you already believe, while the reference exposes cases you did not think of, such as odd whitespace or line endings. Pin the reference version and settings, since they can change its answers.', '{"how_it_works":"Unit tests compare your program with expected values that you wrote. That checks the cases you thought of,\nagainst your understanding of the right answer. If your understanding is wrong, the code and the test are wrong\ntogether, and the test passes.\n\nDifferential testing compares your program with another implementation on the same input, often a trusted\nreference tool. When the outputs differ, one of the two is wrong, and finding out which teaches you something you\ndid not know, such as how the reference treats a no-break space or a file without a final newline.\n\nIt works best with many and varied inputs: an empty file, one without a final newline, Windows line endings,\naccented text, emoji, invalid bytes, a file larger than the buffer, and piped input as well as named files.\nGenerating random inputs and comparing automatically, a form of fuzzing, finds cases nobody would write by hand.\n\nThe reference must be pinned. Different versions and builds of wc disagree on some inputs, and the locale changes\nthe answers, so record which version and settings produced the expected output.","examples":["The real wc in Git Bash reports 58070 words for the challenge''s file when run from a script and 58164 in its terminal, because the locale differs, so a test that compares against it must pin the locale first.","Compiler teams compile the same programs with two compilers and compare the results to find miscompilations.","When a service is rewritten, running the old and new versions side by side on real traffic and comparing their responses catches differences before users do."],"misconceptions":["If my unit tests pass, my tool matches the original. Only for the cases and answers you wrote down.","The reference is always right. It has bugs and version differences too, so a difference is a question, not a verdict.","Comparing a couple of files is enough. The bugs live in the unusual inputs."],"elsewhere":["Browser engines, databases and JSON parsers are tested against each other and against shared test suites.","Large refactorings often rely on comparing old and new outputs.","Data migrations compare row counts and checksums between the old store and the new one."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('site-directory-layout', 'Directory layout as the data model', 'state-storage', 'foundation', null, null, 'A static site generator uses folder conventions as its data model: sources, templates and output each live in a fixed place, so a file''s location decides what it is and where it appears on the site.', '{"how_it_works":"Many tools avoid configuration by agreeing on where things live. A static site generator typically has a folder of content written in Markdown, a folder of themes or templates that decide how pages look, a folder of static files such as images and stylesheets copied as they are, and an output folder where the finished HTML is written. The structure itself carries meaning: a page at content/blog/first.md becomes the address blog/first on the site, without any list of pages to maintain.\n\nThis is called convention over configuration. It makes a new project quick to start, makes every project look the same to anyone who has used the tool, and lets commands such as new page or build work without being told where anything is.\n\nThe most important convention is that sources and output are separate. Output is derived data: it can always be produced again from the sources. Keeping it in its own folder means it can be deleted and rebuilt at any time, excluded from version control, and never mistaken for content. When output is written among the sources, the next build may read its own previous output as new content, people may edit generated files by hand and lose the edits on the next build, and generated files end up committed by accident.","examples":["Hugo and Jekyll both define a fixed directory structure, and a page''s path in the content folder becomes its address.","A project that wrote generated HTML next to its Markdown ended up publishing pages built from earlier generated pages.","Build tools for many languages keep sources in one folder and compiled output in another that is ignored by version control."],"misconceptions":["Folder layout is just tidiness. In these tools it is the data model that decides what each file is.","Generated files are part of the project''s sources. They are derived and should be rebuildable at any moment.","Conventions limit what you can build. They remove decisions you would otherwise make for every project."],"elsewhere":["Web frameworks use folder conventions for routes, so a file''s path becomes a URL.","Language build tools separate source folders from build output folders.","Version control ignore files exist largely to keep derived output out of the repository."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('cli-subcommands', 'Command-line arguments and exit codes', 'process-testing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A command-line tool reads its options, subcommands and file names from its arguments, and rejects ones it does not understand. It writes results to standard output and problems to the error stream, and exits with a non-zero status on failure, so scripts can tell success from failure.', '{"how_it_works":"A command-line tool has a contract with its caller, which is often another program rather than a person. The\ncontract has three channels.\n\nStandard output carries the result and nothing else, so it can be piped into the next tool or saved to a file.\nStandard error carries messages for the person: warnings, usage help and errors. Keeping the two apart means an\nerror message never ends up inside a file of results.\n\nThe exit status, a small number returned when the program ends, says whether it worked. Zero means success and\nanything else means failure, and some tools use different values for different problems. Shells, scripts and CI\nsystems check it, so a step that fails but exits with zero lets a broken build pass.\n\nArguments complete the contract. Options such as -c and -l choose what to do and can usually be combined, the\nremaining words are file names, and an unknown option is an error with a usage message, not something to ignore.\nWhen several files are given and one cannot be read, a well-behaved tool reports that file on standard error,\nstill processes the others, and exits with a non-zero status at the end.","examples":["A deploy script runs the tests and checks their exit status. If the tests fail but the command exits with zero, broken code ships.","A tool that prints its errors on standard output corrupts every report that saves its output to a file.","GNU wc given three files, one of them missing, prints counts for the two that exist and a total, writes an error about the third on standard error, and exits with status 1."],"misconceptions":["Exit codes are for people to read. They are mainly for other programs.","Printing an error is enough. Without a non-zero exit status a script cannot tell that anything went wrong.","Results and error messages can share one stream. Mixing them breaks pipelines."],"elsewhere":["CI systems mark a step as failed purely from its exit status.","HTTP status codes play the same role for web APIs that exit statuses play for commands.","Container platforms decide whether to restart a service from how its process exited."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('untrusted-paths', 'Untrusted names in file paths', 'streams-io', 'foundation', null, null, 'A name that ends up inside a file path, from a command line or a web request, can contain .. and climb out of the folder you meant. Resolve the full path, normalise it, and check that it still sits inside the base folder before you read or write.', '{"how_it_works":"Whenever a name from outside, such as a URL path, a file name in an upload or a command-line argument, becomes part of a file path, an attacker can choose it. The classic attack is directory traversal: the segment .. means ''the parent folder'', so a request for /../../etc/passwd joined naively to a web root climbs out of it and reads a system file.\n\nThe safe approach has three parts. First decode the path the way the server will finally interpret it, because attackers hide dots and slashes in percent-encoding such as %2e%2e%2f. Then resolve it against the root folder and normalise it, so that every . and .. is removed and you have one absolute path. Finally check that this absolute path is still inside the root, comparing whole path segments: a root of /srv/www must not accept /srv/www-private, even though the text starts the same way.\n\nRejecting strings that contain .. is not enough, because encodings, backslashes on Windows, and symbolic links that point outside the root all get around simple text checks. Resolving and then checking containment is what holds. The same rule applies to any code that writes files, where traversal can overwrite configuration or code.","examples":["A photo service lets users download their files by name, and a request for ../../config/secrets.env returns the server''s credentials.","A filter that removes ../ from paths is beaten by ....// which becomes ../ after the filter runs.","An archive extractor writes a file named ../../.bashrc outside the target folder, a bug class called Zip Slip."],"misconceptions":["People think browsers and curl will never send .. in a path. They normalise it by default, but an attacker''s own client sends whatever bytes it likes.","People think checking that the resolved path starts with the root text is enough. A sibling folder with a longer name passes that check.","People think this only matters for reading. Writing to an attacker-chosen path is usually worse."],"elsewhere":["File upload handlers, template loaders and backup restore tools all join untrusted names to folders.","Path traversal has its own entry in the OWASP and CWE lists of common weaknesses.","Containers and chroot jails limit the damage, but they are a second line of defence, not a replacement for the check."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('markup-to-html', 'Converting Markdown to HTML', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Markdown is a lightweight markup language that converts to HTML. Doing it reliably is parsing: block structure first, then inline marks, following a precise specification such as CommonMark.', '{"how_it_works":"Markdown lets people write formatted text with plain characters: a line starting with a hash is a heading, a blank line separates paragraphs, a line starting with a dash is a list item, and stars or underscores mark emphasis. A converter turns this into HTML elements: headings, paragraphs, lists, emphasis, links and code blocks.\n\nThe original description of Markdown was informal and left many cases open. How much indentation turns a line into a code block? Does a list need a blank line before it? What happens when emphasis markers overlap? Different tools answered differently, so the same document could render as a list in one and as a paragraph in another. CommonMark was created to fix this: it is a precise specification with hundreds of numbered examples showing input and exact expected HTML, and most modern converters follow it.\n\nA correct converter works in two phases, like a parser. First it finds the blocks: it reads the document line by line and decides which lines form headings, paragraphs, lists, quotes and code blocks, including blocks nested inside others. Then it processes the inline content of each block, finding emphasis, links and inline code. Doing it with a series of search-and-replace patterns breaks on nesting and edge cases.\n\nThe resulting HTML is markup, which matters for safety: if authors are not trusted, raw HTML inside Markdown must be filtered.","examples":["A README renders as a neat list on one hosting site and as a single run-on paragraph on another because their converters disagree on a blank line.","Documentation tools, forums and chat apps all convert Markdown to HTML, usually following CommonMark or a close variant.","A comment system that allowed raw HTML inside Markdown let users inject scripts into other people''s pages."],"misconceptions":["Markdown has one agreed definition. The original was informal; CommonMark is the precise version most tools follow.","Converting Markdown is a few search-and-replace rules. Nesting and edge cases need a real two-phase parse.","Markdown output is always safe. Markdown allows raw HTML, which must be filtered for untrusted authors."],"elsewhere":["Code hosting sites, wikis and note-taking apps render Markdown.","Documentation generators turn Markdown or similar markup into whole websites.","Chat and forum software convert user Markdown to HTML and sanitise it."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('output-escaping', 'Escaping text inserted into HTML', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Text inserted into HTML must have its special characters replaced with character references, or the browser reads them as markup, breaking the page or running an attacker''s script.', '{"how_it_works":"HTML gives a few characters special meaning. A less-than sign starts a tag, an ampersand starts a character reference, and quotes end attribute values. When plain text is inserted into HTML without change, any of these characters in the text are read as markup. At best the page layout breaks; at worst, a value containing a script tag is run by every reader''s browser. That second case is called cross-site scripting, and it lets an attacker act as the reader on the site.\n\nEscaping replaces each special character with a character reference that displays the same character without its markup meaning: the less-than sign becomes ampersand-l-t-semicolon, the ampersand becomes ampersand-a-m-p-semicolon, and quotes become their own references. The browser shows the original text, and nothing is interpreted.\n\nThe right escaping depends on where the value lands. Text between tags needs the less-than sign and ampersand escaped. A value inside an attribute also needs quotes escaped, and the attribute must be quoted. Values placed inside scripts, styles or addresses need different rules again, which is why the safest designs avoid putting untrusted text there at all.\n\nThe other half of the rule is knowing what is already HTML. Rendered Markdown is markup on purpose and must not be escaped, or readers see tags as text. A design that keeps track of which values are text and which are markup, and escapes text at the moment it is inserted, prevents both mistakes.","examples":["A page titled with an ampersand and a less-than sign broke a site''s layout because the title went into the page without escaping.","A guest author put a script tag in a post title, and every visitor''s browser ran it.","Escaping the rendered Markdown body by mistake made readers see angle-bracket tags instead of formatted text."],"misconceptions":["Only user input needs escaping. Any text from outside the template, including your own content files, does.","One escaping function fits everywhere. Text, attributes, scripts and addresses each need their own rules.","Escaping everything is safe. Escaping a value that is meant to be HTML breaks it."],"elsewhere":["Web frameworks escape template values by default for exactly this reason.","SQL uses parameters instead of pasting values into queries, the same idea for a different language.","Shell commands and log files need their own escaping when untrusted text is inserted."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('template-substitution', 'Template substitution', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A template is a document with placeholders that are filled with values. Correct substitution reads the template once, inserts each value exactly once, and escapes values according to where they land.', '{"how_it_works":"A template separates what a page looks like from what it says. The theme provides an HTML page with placeholders, often written in double braces, for things such as the title and the content. The build replaces each placeholder with a value for the page being generated.\n\nThe safe way to substitute is a single pass. Read the template from start to end, copying text to the output, and each time a placeholder appears, write its value and continue after the placeholder. Inserted values are never scanned again. Substituting one placeholder after another with repeated replace operations instead re-scans text that was already inserted: if a page''s own text contains the name of another placeholder, it gets replaced too, and a page can end up containing its own body twice, or worse, text an author did not intend.\n\nTwo more decisions make templates robust. The first is what to do with a placeholder the template uses but no value exists for: silently writing nothing hides typos in themes, so many engines report it or show a visible marker. The second is escaping: a value that is plain text, such as a title, must be escaped for HTML when it is inserted, while a value that is already HTML, such as rendered Markdown, must be inserted as it is. Template engines usually escape by default and need an explicit mark for trusted HTML.","examples":["A blog post that quoted the theme''s own placeholder syntax came out with its body pasted in a second time.","Email templates with a misspelled placeholder sent customers messages with a blank where their name should be.","Template engines used by web frameworks escape inserted text by default to prevent script injection."],"misconceptions":["Replacing each placeholder in turn is the same as substituting once. Repeated replacing re-scans inserted text.","Missing values should quietly become empty. That hides typos in templates.","Every value should be inserted the same way. Text needs escaping, while trusted HTML must not be escaped."],"elsewhere":["Web frameworks render HTML pages from templates on every request.","Email, invoice and report generators fill templates with data.","Configuration tools and deployment systems fill templated configuration files with values."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('build-pipeline', 'Build pipeline', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'A build turns sources into output through a fixed sequence of stages. A good build is repeatable and complete: its output depends only on the current sources, with nothing stale left behind.', '{"how_it_works":"A build pipeline is the sequence of steps that turns sources into output. For a static site it is: find every content file, read it, convert its Markdown to HTML, fill the template, and write the result to the output folder, alongside copies of static files such as images.\n\nTwo properties make a build trustworthy. It should be repeatable: running it twice on the same sources gives the same output, so the result never depends on what happened before. And it should be complete: the output reflects exactly the current sources. A build that only writes files and never removes them fails the second test, because when a page is renamed or deleted, its old output stays and is published. The simple fix is a clean build that clears the output folder first. The faster fix, used by large sites, is to track which output came from which source and remove the outputs whose sources are gone.\n\nSplitting the pipeline into clear stages with simple inputs and outputs helps in three ways. Each stage can be tested on its own. A failure points at one stage. And incremental builds become possible: if nothing a page depends on has changed, its stage can be skipped. Incremental builds need dependency tracking, since a page depends not only on its own file but also on the template, and a template change must rebuild every page.","examples":["A renamed page stayed online under its old address for months because the build never removed old output.","Make and similar build tools rebuild only targets whose sources changed, using timestamps and declared dependencies.","A site that rebuilt only changed pages showed old navigation everywhere after the template changed, because pages were not marked as depending on it."],"misconceptions":["If the build succeeded, the output is correct. Stale files from earlier builds can remain.","A clean build is wasteful. It is the simplest way to be sure the output matches the sources; incremental builds are an optimisation.","A page depends only on its own source file. It also depends on templates and shared data."],"elsewhere":["Compilers and bundlers run multi-stage build pipelines.","Continuous integration runs builds from a clean checkout to be sure they are repeatable.","Data pipelines transform raw data into reports through similar stages."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('static-file-serving', 'Serving static files over HTTP', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'A static server turns a URL path into a file. It maps / to index.html, sends the file with the right Content-Type, and answers 404 when nothing matches. The browser trusts the Content-Type header, not the file extension.', '{"how_it_works":"A static file server maps the path in a request to a file under a root folder and sends its bytes back. By convention a path ending in a slash maps to an index file, usually index.html. If nothing matches, the server answers 404 Not Found.\n\nTwo headers matter most. Content-Type tells the client what the bytes are, as a media type such as text/html, image/png or application/javascript, sometimes with a charset parameter such as text/html; charset=utf-8. Browsers decide how to treat a response from this header, not from the file name, so a stylesheet sent as text/plain is ignored. Content-Length gives the body''s size in bytes, which lets the client know where the body ends.\n\nFiles should be read and sent as bytes, never decoded and re-encoded as text, because an image is not text and even a text file''s byte count is what Content-Length needs. Real servers add more: caching headers so browsers do not refetch unchanged files, range requests for large media, and a sensible default when the extension is unknown, usually application/octet-stream, which makes browsers download rather than display.","examples":["A site''s CSS has no effect because the server sends it with Content-Type text/plain, and the browser refuses to apply it.","A server that reads images as text corrupts every PNG it serves.","Visiting a folder path such as /docs/ returns /docs/index.html, so the address can stay short."],"misconceptions":["People think browsers trust the file extension. They trust the Content-Type header.","People think Content-Length is the number of characters. It is the number of bytes in the body.","People think a missing file should crash the handler or return 500. It is the client asking for something that does not exist, so it is 404."],"elsewhere":["CDNs and object storage services such as S3 are static file servers at scale, with the same Content-Type rules.","Front-end build tools produce folders of static files meant to be served exactly this way.","Download links that open as gibberish in the browser are usually a wrong Content-Type."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('file-watching', 'Watching files for changes', 'streams-io', 'foundation', null, null, 'Development tools learn about file changes from operating-system notifications or by polling modification times. Raw events arrive in bursts and miss new folders unless the watcher handles both.', '{"how_it_works":"A tool that rebuilds when files change needs to know when they change. There are two ways. Polling checks the files'' modification times every so often: simple, portable and reliable, but slower to react and wasteful on large trees. Notification asks the operating system to report changes as they happen: Linux has inotify, macOS has FSEvents and Windows has ReadDirectoryChangesW. Notifications are fast and cheap, but each has its own rules.\n\nThree practical problems appear with notifications. First, one save is rarely one event. Many editors save safely by writing a temporary file and renaming it over the original, and they may also update metadata, so a single save produces a burst of create, modify and rename events. Reacting to each event rebuilds several times. The fix is to debounce: wait until no events have arrived for a short quiet period, such as a tenth of a second, then rebuild once. Second, watches are often per folder. A new subfolder may not be watched until the tool adds it, so new pages there are missed unless the watcher notices new folders or falls back to polling. Third, the tool''s own output must be excluded, or writing the build output triggers another build, endlessly.\n\nNotifications can also be lost when many changes happen at once, so robust tools occasionally rescan to catch up.","examples":["A development server rebuilt a site three times for every save because the editor wrote a temporary file, renamed it and touched the metadata.","A watcher that wrote its output inside a watched folder rebuilt itself in an endless loop.","Test runners re-run tests on save using the same watching techniques."],"misconceptions":["One save produces one change event. It usually produces several.","Watching a folder covers everything inside it forever. New subfolders may need to be added explicitly.","Notifications are never lost. Under heavy load they can be, so tools rescan from time to time."],"elsewhere":["Build tools, test runners and bundlers in watch mode rely on file notifications.","File synchronisation services watch folders to upload changes.","Editors notice when another program changes a file they have open."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('live-reload-push', 'Pushing a reload to the browser', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'HTTP lets only the browser start a request, so to push a reload the page first opens a long-lived connection, such as server-sent events, and the server sends a message on it after each rebuild.', '{"how_it_works":"Ordinary HTTP is request and response: the browser asks and the server answers. A server cannot reach out to a browser on its own, so to tell an open page that the site has changed, the page must first open a connection that stays open.\n\nServer-sent events are the simplest way. The page runs a small script that opens a connection to a special address on the development server. The server keeps the response open and writes a short text message down it whenever it wants, and the browser delivers each message to the script. After a rebuild, the server sends a reload message and the script reloads the page. If the connection drops, for example because the server restarted, the browser reconnects automatically. WebSockets offer a two-way channel and work too, but are more than a reload signal needs.\n\nThe reload script belongs only to development. A good design adds it while serving, for instance by inserting a script tag into each HTML page as the development server sends it, and never writes it into the files the build produces. A published site that carried the script would keep trying to connect to a server that does not exist, filling readers'' browsers with errors.\n\nTwo details help: send the reload only after the build has finished writing, and tell the browser not to cache the pages during development, or it may reload an old copy.","examples":["Front-end development servers reload or patch the page in the browser a moment after a file is saved.","A site accidentally published with its live-reload script made every visitor''s browser retry a connection to a missing development server.","Dashboards use server-sent events to push new numbers to open pages without the page asking repeatedly."],"misconceptions":["The server can simply tell the browser to reload. HTTP needs the browser to open the connection first.","A WebSocket is required. Server-sent events are simpler and enough for a one-way reload signal.","Adding the script to the template is fine. It must be added only by the development server."],"elsewhere":["Live dashboards, notifications and progress updates use server-sent events or WebSockets.","Chat applications keep long-lived connections to receive messages as they arrive.","Streaming responses from AI services are often delivered as server-sent events."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('delimited-fields', 'Fields in delimited text', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Tab- and comma-separated files hold one record per line, split into fields at a delimiter. Fields are numbered from 1, two delimiters in a row mean an empty field, and a line with fewer fields than asked for is normal, not an error.', '{"how_it_works":"A delimited file is the simplest table format there is: each line is a record, and one chosen character, the delimiter, separates the fields inside it. Tab is the traditional choice for Unix tools because it rarely appears inside data; the comma is the choice of spreadsheets. To read field n you split the line at every delimiter and take the nth piece, counting from 1.\n\nThree details decide whether a tool is right or quietly wrong. Two delimiters in a row are an empty field, not a single separator, so splitting a,,c gives three fields, the middle one empty; tools that collapse runs of whitespace, such as awk by default, behave differently, and mixing the two mental models is a classic bug. A line may have fewer fields than the one you ask for, and the convention for cut is to print an empty result for it rather than fail, while a line with no delimiter at all is printed whole unless you ask otherwise. And the line ending is not part of the last field: a file from Windows ends each line with a carriage return before the newline, and if you only strip the newline, the carriage return becomes the last character of the last field.\n\nThe delimiter is a contract between whoever wrote the file and whoever reads it. Nothing in the file says which character was used, so the reader must be told, and a value that happens to contain the delimiter breaks the contract unless the format adds quoting, which plain tab-separated text does not.","examples":["A spreadsheet exported as tab-separated text has empty cells; a script that splits on runs of whitespace shifts every later column one place left and reports prices as quantities.","Joining two exports by customer id fails for every row whose id came from a Windows machine, because the id field silently ends in a carriage return.","A log line format with a pipe delimiter breaks the day a user types a pipe into a free-text field."],"misconceptions":["Two delimiters in a row are one separator. They are an empty field in between.","Asking for field 5 of a line with three fields is an error. The usual convention is an empty result.","The newline at the end of a line belongs to the last field. It ends the record, and a carriage return before it needs handling too."],"elsewhere":["Database bulk loaders, log shippers and spreadsheet imports all depend on the same field and delimiter rules.","Command-line tools such as cut, awk, sort -t and join all take a delimiter and number fields from 1.","Protocols with header lines, such as HTTP, split each line at a delimiter in the same way."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('unix-pipelines', 'Composing tools with pipes', 'streams-io', 'foundation', null, null, 'A pipe connects one program''s standard output to the next program''s standard input, so small tools each do one job and run at the same time. Data flows through a small buffer, a slow reader pauses the writer, and a reader that exits early stops the writer.', '{"how_it_works":"A pipeline such as cut, then sort, then uniq, then wc starts every program at the same time and joins each one''s standard output to the next one''s standard input through a pipe, a small buffer inside the operating system. Each tool only knows that it reads lines and writes lines, which is what lets small single-purpose tools combine into something none of them does alone.\n\nBecause the buffer is small, a pipeline streams. When the reader is slow the buffer fills and the writer is paused until there is room, so memory stays bounded however much data flows; this automatic pause is called backpressure. When the reader exits early, as head does after printing ten lines, the operating system tells the writer the next time it writes: by default it receives the signal SIGPIPE and stops, or it gets an error it should treat as a quiet reason to finish. That is why cut on a huge file, piped into head, returns at once instead of reading the whole file.\n\nOrder matters, because each tool sees only what the previous one produced. uniq removes only adjacent duplicate lines, so uniq before sort removes only neighbours, while sort then uniq removes all duplicates. A tool that wants to play well in pipelines writes results to standard output, messages to standard error, flushes when it is done, and does not ask the terminal questions.","examples":["Counting distinct artists with cut and uniq gives the wrong number until sort is put before uniq.","A script piping a 10 GB log into head finishes instantly, because the producer is stopped once head exits.","A tool that prints progress messages to standard output corrupts the data the next tool in the pipeline reads."],"misconceptions":["Each stage finishes before the next starts. They all run at once, connected by small buffers.","A pipe holds the whole output. It holds a few kilobytes, and the writer waits when it is full.","An early exit by the reader is an error to report. For the writer it is usually a reason to stop quietly."],"elsewhere":["Shell scripts, build systems and data pipelines are built from the same composition idea.","Streaming frameworks and reactive libraries reinvent backpressure for network services.","Container logging and log shippers rely on programs writing plain lines to standard output."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('regex-matching', 'Regular expressions as automata', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A regular expression describes a set of strings with literals, classes, repetition and anchors. Underneath it is a small state machine: matching walks the text while tracking which states are possible, which is how grep can search huge files quickly.', '{"how_it_works":"A regular expression is a tiny language for describing a set of strings. Letters match themselves, a dot matches any one character, a class such as a set of digits matches one character from the set, a star repeats the thing before it zero or more times, and anchors match positions rather than characters: the caret the start of a line and the dollar sign the end. A line matches when some part of it is in the set, so the pattern J matches any line containing a J anywhere.\n\nUnderneath, every such pattern can be turned into a finite automaton, a graph of states joined by transitions labelled with characters. Thompson''s construction builds a nondeterministic automaton with roughly one state per pattern symbol; matching then reads the text one character at a time while keeping the set of states the automaton could be in. Because that set is never larger than the number of states, the work is proportional to the length of the text times the size of the pattern, with no surprises, and it needs to see each character only once, which suits streaming input.\n\nThere are several dialects. POSIX defines basic and extended regular expressions, in which a plus or a question mark may or may not be special; Perl-style engines add shorthand classes such as backslash d for a digit and backslash w for a word character, plus features like backreferences that are no longer regular at all. Which characters count as digits or word characters can also depend on the locale and on Unicode mode, so the same pattern can match differently in two tools.","examples":["The same pattern that finds error codes in one tool matches nothing in another, because one reads a backslash d as a digit class and the other as the letter d.","A log search tool scans gigabytes per second by compiling the pattern into an automaton once and streaming the file through it.","Input validation that forgets the anchors accepts any string that merely contains a valid value."],"misconceptions":["A pattern must match the whole line. Without anchors it matches anywhere in the line.","All regex engines speak the same language. POSIX basic, POSIX extended and Perl-style dialects differ in syntax and features.","Regular expressions are slow by nature. Automaton-based matching runs in time linear in the input."],"elsewhere":["Lexers in compilers and the JSON parser recognise tokens with the same automata.","Web application firewalls, editors, and search in IDEs and databases are built on regex engines.","Network intrusion detection systems compile thousands of patterns into one automaton."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('recursive-file-walk', 'Walking a directory tree', 'streams-io', 'foundation', null, null, 'To visit every file under a folder, list its entries, handle the files, and descend into each subfolder the same way. The order entries come back in is not guaranteed, symbolic links can form loops, and some folders cannot be read, so a walker needs a rule for each.', '{"how_it_works":"A folder holds files and other folders, which hold more, so the file system is a tree. Visiting every file means a traversal: list a folder, handle each file, and do the same for each subfolder. It can be written with recursion, where a function calls itself for each subfolder, or with an explicit list of folders still to visit. The explicit list avoids running out of call stack on very deep trees.\n\nReal file systems are not quite trees. A symbolic link can point to a folder higher up, so a walker that follows links can loop forever. Walkers usually skip links, or remember which folders they have visited. A hard link makes one file appear under two names, so two paths that look different can be the very same file on disk, not a copy of it.\n\nA walk over thousands of folders will meet entries it cannot read, because of permissions, files deleted while the walk runs, or names that are not valid text. A robust walker reports the problem and moves on, so one bad entry does not stop the whole scan.\n\nThe order in which a folder lists its entries is not promised. If the output must be stable, sort it.","examples":["A backup tool that followed a link from a folder to its own parent filled a disk copying the same tree again and again.","A duplicate finder reported two hard links to one file as duplicates, and deleting one would have freed no space.","A scan of a home folder stopped halfway at a protected system folder, until it was changed to report and skip errors."],"misconceptions":["The file system is a tree. Links make cycles and shared files possible.","Every entry can be read. Permissions and files that vanish mid-walk are normal.","Folders list files in name order. The order depends on the file system; sort it if you need it."],"elsewhere":["Build tools walk source folders to find files that changed.","Search tools such as find and grep with a recursive option use the same traversal.","Garbage collectors walk a graph of objects with the same cycle problem, solved by marking visited nodes."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('case-folding', 'Comparing text without case', 'text-encodings', 'foundation', null, null, 'Ignoring case means mapping both strings to a common form before comparing. In ASCII that is a simple letter swap, but in Unicode some mappings change length or depend on language, so case-insensitive matching is a choice of rules, not a single operation.', '{"how_it_works":"To compare text without regard to case, both sides are mapped to one common form and then compared. For English letters this is easy: each capital has exactly one lowercase partner. Unicode defines case folding for this purpose, a mapping designed for comparison rather than display.\n\nOutside ASCII the rules get interesting. Simple case folding maps one character to one character, which keeps lengths and positions unchanged and is what most search tools use. Full case folding allows one character to become several: the German sharp s ß folds to ss, so under full folding straße matches STRASSE, while under simple folding it does not. GNU grep -i uses the simple form, so on this project''s reference straße does not match STRASSE. Some rules depend on language: in Turkish and Azerbaijani the capital of i is İ with a dot, and the lowercase of I is a dotless ı, so a program that lowercases with the wrong locale can break identifiers and keywords.\n\nCase folding also interacts with normalisation: é can be one code point or e plus a combining accent, and folding does not join them, so robust comparison normalises first and then folds. For a search tool the practical questions are which folding it uses, whether classes such as a word character keep their meaning under -i, and whether its behaviour matches the reference tool''s under the same locale.","examples":["A login that lowercases user names with a Turkish locale turns the admin account TITLE into a different string from title.","A case-insensitive search for straße finds STRASSE in one tool and not in another, because one uses full case folding and the other simple.","Two accounts called André can exist side by side when one name was normalised and folded and the other was not."],"misconceptions":["Ignoring case is just lowercasing both sides. Lowercasing is for display; case folding is designed for comparison, and some mappings change length.","Case rules are the same in every language. Turkish dotted and dotless i are the famous exception.","Case-insensitive matching keeps all lengths and positions. Full folding can turn one character into two."],"elsewhere":["User names, email addresses and file names on case-insensitive file systems all need a folding rule.","Database collations decide how case-insensitive indexes compare text.","Search engines fold case and normalise text before indexing."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('lexical-analysis', 'Lexing text into tokens', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A lexer reads characters and groups them into tokens, such as a brace, a string, a number or a keyword, and skips the whitespace between them. The parser then works with meaningful pieces instead of single characters.', '{"how_it_works":"Text arrives as a flat run of characters, but the rules of a format are written about larger pieces: a string, a number, a keyword, a punctuation mark. A lexer, also called a scanner or tokenizer, walks the characters once from left to right and groups them into those pieces, called tokens. Each token has a kind, such as left brace, string, number or the keyword true, and usually its text and its position in the input.\n\nThe lexer decides where one token ends and the next begins. Some tokens are a single character, like a comma or a colon. Others are longer and need a small loop: a string runs from its opening double quote to the matching closing one, and a number keeps going while the characters still fit the number rules. Whitespace between tokens is skipped, which is why the parser never has to think about spaces or newlines.\n\nSplitting the work this way keeps each part simple. The lexer knows how characters form words, and the parser knows how words form sentences. It is also the natural place to catch characters that cannot start any token, such as a stray at-sign, and to record the line and column of every token so later errors can point at the right place.","examples":["In a JSON text holding an opening square bracket, the number 42, a comma, a space, the word true and a closing square bracket, the lexer hands over five tokens and drops the space.","A string that contains spaces, commas or braces is still one token, because the lexer only stops at the closing double quote.","Compilers, interpreters, syntax highlighters in editors and template engines all start with a lexer before anything else looks at the text."],"misconceptions":["People think splitting on spaces is enough. Strings can hold spaces, and tokens such as a comma and a closing bracket can touch with no space between them.","People think the lexer checks whether the text is valid. It only checks that each piece is a valid token; the order of the pieces is the parser''s job.","People think the lexer must finish before the parser starts. Many parsers ask the lexer for one token at a time."],"elsewhere":["Programming language compilers and interpreters.","Syntax highlighting and code formatting in editors.","Command-line shells, which split a typed line into words while respecting quotes."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('recursive-descent-parsing', 'Recursive descent parsing', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A recursive descent parser has one routine per rule of the grammar, and a rule that contains other rules calls their routines, so nesting in the input becomes nesting of calls. It looks at the next token to decide which rule applies.', '{"how_it_works":"Recursive descent is the most direct way to turn a grammar into a parser. Each rule of the grammar becomes one routine. The routine for value looks at the next token and decides which rule applies: a left brace means object, a left square bracket means array, a string token means string, and so on. The routine for object expects a left brace, then repeatedly expects a string, a colon and a value, separated by commas, and finally a right brace. Whenever the object routine needs a value, it calls the value routine, which may call the object routine again.\n\nThat is where the name comes from: the parser descends through the grammar from the top rule, and it is recursive because rules contain themselves. The nesting in the input becomes nesting of routine calls, and the call stack remembers where the parser is in every enclosing object and array.\n\nLooking at only the next token to choose a rule works for JSON because every kind of value starts with a different token. The parser can therefore decide without guessing or backtracking. At any point it knows exactly which tokens are allowed next, so the first token that does not fit is a precise place to report an error, with what was expected and what was found.","examples":["For an array of two objects, the array routine calls the value routine twice, and each call runs the object routine, which itself calls the value routine for every member.","When the object routine has read a key and expects a colon but finds a comma, it can say exactly that, at the comma''s line and column.","Many hand-written parsers for real programming languages use recursive descent because errors are easy to explain."],"misconceptions":["People think a parser needs a parser generator tool. For a grammar like JSON''s, hand-written recursive descent is short and clear.","People think recursion here is only an implementation detail. It mirrors the grammar, and it brings a real limit: very deep nesting uses a lot of stack.","People think the parser must build a tree to be useful. A validator can walk the same routines and only answer valid or invalid."],"elsewhere":["Compilers and interpreters for programming languages.","Parsers for configuration formats, query languages and expressions in calculators.","Command-line argument parsers that handle nested subcommands."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('json-grammar', 'The JSON grammar', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A JSON value is an object, an array, a string, a number, true, false or null, and objects and arrays can hold any value, nested as deep as you like. The rules are strict: keys are double-quoted strings, and there are no trailing commas or comments.', '{"how_it_works":"A grammar is a small set of rules that says which sequences of tokens are allowed. JSON''s grammar fits on one page. A JSON text is one value, with optional whitespace around it. A value is one of seven things: an object, an array, a string, a number, true, false or null. An object is a pair of curly braces holding zero or more members separated by commas, and each member is a string, a colon and a value. An array is a pair of square brackets holding zero or more values separated by commas.\n\nThe rules are recursive: a value can be an object, whose members hold values, which can be objects again. That is what lets JSON nest to any depth, and it is why a parser for it is naturally recursive too.\n\nJSON is strict on purpose. Keys must be double-quoted strings. There is no comma after the last member or element. There are no comments. Single quotes are not strings. Numbers have no leading zeros, no plus sign and no bare decimal point. Two standards describe the same syntax, ECMA-404 and RFC 8259. RFC 8259 adds advice for interoperability, for example that keys inside one object should be unique and that a JSON text exchanged between systems must be UTF-8. Older versions said the top level had to be an object or an array; RFC 8259 allows any value there.","examples":["An object with a comma after its last member looks harmless but is invalid, and strict parsers reject it.","A single number or a single string on its own is a valid JSON text under RFC 8259, although some old validators reject it.","Configuration files that allow comments are not plain JSON; they use an extension such as JSON5 or JSON with comments."],"misconceptions":["People think JSON is the same as a JavaScript object literal. JavaScript allows unquoted keys, single quotes, trailing commas and comments; JSON allows none of them.","People think duplicate keys are invalid. The grammar allows them, but RFC 8259 says keys should be unique because parsers disagree about which value wins.","People think the top level must be an object or array. That was true of the older RFC 4627, not of RFC 8259."],"elsewhere":["Web API requests and responses.","Configuration files and package manifests.","Grammars for every other format and language, written the same way as rules about tokens."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('conformance-test-suite', 'Conformance test suites', 'process-testing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A conformance suite is a shared set of inputs, each with the verdict a correct implementation must give. Running it shows whether you follow the specification in the corners nobody thinks of.', '{"how_it_works":"Your own tests check the cases you thought of, so they share your blind spots. A conformance suite is a set of inputs written by other people, usually to probe the corners of a specification, each labelled with the verdict a correct implementation must give: must accept, must reject, or implementation-defined. Running your program over the whole suite and comparing its verdicts with the labels finds mistakes you would never have tested, such as a number with a capital E and no digits after it, or a lone surrogate escape.\n\nA good harness runs the suite automatically after every change, derives the expected verdict from each file''s name or label, compares it with your program''s exit status, and lists every mismatch. A rejected valid file and an accepted invalid file are both failures; accepting too much is not safer than rejecting too much.\n\nSuites are not perfect either. The json.org suite is small, and its own checker gets some numbers wrong; one of its fail files is a bare string, which RFC 8259 now allows; another is nested 20 levels deep, which only fails under a depth limit. Larger suites such as JSONTestSuite split tests into must-pass, must-fail and implementation-defined groups. When you deliberately follow a different rule from a test, record the choice and the reason instead of hiding the mismatch.","examples":["The JSONTestSuite study ran dozens of popular JSON parsers over hundreds of edge-case files and found that almost every one disagreed with the specification somewhere.","A team runs a conformance suite in its build so that a refactor of the number lexer cannot quietly start accepting a leading zero.","Web browsers share large conformance suites so that the same page behaves the same everywhere."],"misconceptions":["People think passing their own tests means conforming. Their tests only cover what they already understood.","People think a suite''s verdicts are always right. Suites age and contain mistakes, so a mismatch is a question to settle against the specification.","People think accepting an invalid input is harmless. It means your program and a strict one now disagree about the same data."],"elsewhere":["Browser engines and web platform tests.","Compilers checked against language test suites.","Protocol implementations such as HTTP and TLS tested against shared vectors."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('frequency-map', 'Counting with a map', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'To count how often each symbol occurs, keep a map from symbol to count and add one for every symbol you read. One pass over the input is enough, and the map grows with the number of distinct symbols, not with the size of the input.', null, 'generated'),
  ('priority-queue', 'Priority queues', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'A priority queue hands back the smallest (or largest) item first, whatever order the items were added in. A binary heap does this in logarithmic time per insert or removal, which makes it the usual tool for greedy algorithms that repeatedly take the two cheapest items.', '{"how_it_works":"A priority queue holds items with priorities and always hands back the one with the smallest priority first. Unlike a plain queue, the order of insertion does not matter. The two main operations are insert and remove-the-minimum.\n\nThe usual implementation is a binary heap: a complete binary tree stored in an array, where every parent is no larger than its children. The smallest item is always at the root. Inserting adds the item at the end and swaps it up while it is smaller than its parent. Removing the minimum moves the last item to the root and swaps it down past its smaller child. Both take time proportional to the height of the tree, which grows with the logarithm of the number of items.\n\nThe alternative, keeping a list and searching it, costs time in proportion to the whole list for every removal. Re-sorting the whole list after every insert is worse still. For an algorithm that removes and inserts thousands of times, such as building a Huffman tree or finding shortest paths, that difference decides whether it finishes in milliseconds or minutes.\n\nWhen two items share a priority, a heap does not promise which comes out first. If the order matters, add a tie-breaker, such as an insertion counter.","examples":["Building a Huffman tree removes the two lightest nodes and inserts their parent, over and over, which is exactly the work a heap is fast at.","A hospital triage system sees the most urgent patient next, not the one who arrived first.","Route planners use a priority queue of places ordered by distance so far to find the shortest route."],"misconceptions":["A priority queue keeps everything sorted. A heap only guarantees that the smallest item is on top.","Items with equal priority come out in arrival order. A heap gives no such guarantee without a tie-breaker.","Sorting once is enough. New items keep arriving, and a sorted list is slow to keep sorted."],"elsewhere":["Operating system schedulers pick the next task to run from a priority queue.","Timers in servers and event loops are kept in a heap ordered by when they fire.","Merging many sorted files keeps the next line from each file in a heap."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('huffman-coding', 'Huffman coding', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Huffman coding gives frequent symbols short bit codes and rare symbols long ones. It builds a binary tree by repeatedly joining the two least frequent nodes, and each symbol''s code is the path from the root to its leaf, for example 0 for left and 1 for right.', '{"how_it_works":"Huffman coding builds the best prefix-free code for a given set of symbol frequencies. Best means no other prefix-free code that assigns whole bits to each symbol gives a shorter total.\n\nThe algorithm is greedy. Make a leaf for every symbol, weighted by its count. Repeatedly take the two lightest nodes, join them under a new parent whose weight is their sum, and put the parent back. When one node remains, it is the root. Rare symbols are merged early and end up deep in the tree with long codes, and frequent symbols are merged late and stay near the root with short codes.\n\nTies can be broken in different ways, and each way gives a different but equally short code. So a decoder can only rebuild exactly the same tree if it uses the same tie-breaking rule, or if the encoder sends the code lengths or the tree itself.\n\nSome cases need care. A file with one distinct symbol makes a tree with a single leaf, which still needs a code at least one bit long. An empty file has no symbols at all.\n\nHuffman coding cannot beat the entropy, and because each symbol costs a whole number of bits, it wastes up to almost one bit per symbol when one symbol is very common. Arithmetic coding avoids that, and most real compressors first find repeated strings and then Huffman-code what remains.","examples":["With frequencies 45, 13, 12, 16, 9 and 5, Huffman gives the commonest symbol a 1-bit code and the two rarest 4-bit codes, averaging 2.24 bits instead of 3.","gzip combines repeated-string matching with Huffman codes for the literals and lengths that remain.","A text where one character makes up 99 percent of the file still costs at least 1 bit per character with Huffman, far above its entropy."],"misconceptions":["There is one Huffman code for a text. Ties give several codes of the same total length.","Huffman reaches the entropy exactly. It can be up to almost one bit per symbol above it.","Huffman alone is how zip works. Zip first replaces repeated strings, then uses Huffman codes on the result."],"elsewhere":["JPEG and MP3 use Huffman codes for the final step of their encoders.","HTTP/2 compresses header text with a fixed Huffman code tuned to typical headers.","Fax machines used fixed Huffman-style codes for runs of black and white pixels."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('prefix-codes', 'Prefix-free codes', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'In a prefix-free code no code is the start of another, so a stream of bits can be decoded without separators: read bits until they spell a whole code, emit its symbol, and start again. Codes read from the leaves of a binary tree are prefix-free by construction.', '{"how_it_works":"A variable-length code gives frequent symbols short bit strings and rare symbols long ones. The danger is ambiguity: if a is 0 and b is 01, then the bits 01 could be b, or a followed by the start of something else. A code is prefix-free when no code word is the beginning of another one. Then a decoder can read bits one at a time and stop the moment the bits so far match a code word, because no longer code word starts the same way.\n\nA prefix-free code is exactly a binary tree with the symbols at the leaves. Each left turn adds a 0 and each right turn a 1, and the path from the root to a leaf is that symbol''s code. Because symbols sit only at leaves, no code word can be the start of another.\n\nThe Kraft inequality says which sets of code lengths are possible: the sum of 2 to the power of minus the length, over all code words, must be at most 1. Short codes are expensive, since one code of length 1 uses up half of the whole budget.\n\nBecause the code words are self-delimiting, the stream needs no separators between symbols. The decoder still needs to know where the data ends, because the last byte is usually padded with extra bits.","examples":["Phone numbers work as a prefix code: no valid number is the start of another, which is how the exchange knows when you have finished dialling.","Morse code is not prefix-free, so it needs pauses between letters; without them, dot dash could be A or E followed by T.","UTF-8 is a prefix code over bytes: the first byte of a character says how many bytes follow."],"misconceptions":["Any table of short codes can be decoded. Without the prefix-free property the same bits can mean several different texts.","Every symbol can have a short code. The Kraft inequality limits how many short codes can exist at once.","The decoder knows where the data stops. Padding in the last byte can look like extra symbols unless the length is stored."],"elsewhere":["DEFLATE, used by zip, gzip and PNG, describes its Huffman tables as prefix codes defined by code lengths.","Network protocols use prefix codes for compact headers, such as the Huffman code in HTTP/2 header compression.","Instruction sets with variable-length instructions decode them the same way, one prefix at a time."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('bit-packing', 'Packing flags into bits', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Compact formats pack several small fields into one number: single-bit flags and short numbers at fixed bit positions. Programs read and set them with masks and shifts, so one field can change without disturbing the others.', '{"how_it_works":"A byte has 8 bits and a 16-bit field has 16, and a format can give each bit, or each small group of bits, its own meaning. The DNS header''s flags field is an example. Its top bit says whether the message is a query or a response. The next four bits hold the operation code. Then come single bits for an authoritative answer, a truncated message, recursion desired and recursion available, three reserved bits, and finally four bits for the response code.\n\nTo work with such a field, a program uses masks. A mask is a number with ones only in the positions it cares about. Combining the field with a mask using AND keeps only those bits, which tests a flag or extracts a group. Combining with OR sets bits without touching the rest. Shifting moves a group of bits down to the low end so it can be read as a small number, or up into its place before it is combined. For example, recursion desired is bit 8 counting from zero at the low end, the mask 0x0100, and setting it on an otherwise empty field gives the flags bytes 01 00.\n\nMistakes are silent: setting the wrong bit produces a valid-looking message that means something else, such as a query marked as a response. Writing each mask once, with a name and a comment pointing at the specification, and testing against known bytes catches them.\n\nPacking saves space and bandwidth, which mattered when these formats were designed, and still matters for hardware, network headers and dense storage.","examples":["A teammate sets the top bit of the DNS flags instead of the recursion-desired bit, and servers ignore the query because it now claims to be a response.","File permissions on Unix pack read, write and execute for owner, group and others into nine bits, which is why permissions are written as numbers like 644.","A TCP header packs its SYN, ACK, FIN and other flags into single bits of one byte."],"misconceptions":["Each flag needs its own byte. Flags usually share bits of one field.","Setting a flag means writing a number into the field. Writing replaces the other flags; OR with a mask sets one and keeps the rest.","Bit positions are obvious. Specifications number bits differently, from the top or from the bottom, so check against known bytes."],"elsewhere":["Bloom filters and bitmaps store millions of yes or no facts as single bits.","Image and audio formats pack colour channels and samples into bit fields.","Feature flags and permission systems often store sets of options as bits in one integer."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('file-header-format', 'Self-describing file headers', 'state-storage', 'foundation', null, null, 'A binary file usually starts with a header: a fixed identifier that says what kind of file it is, a version, and the numbers a reader needs before the data, such as sizes or a table. Reading checks the identifier and version first, so a wrong or newer file is rejected instead of misread, and the header says where the data starts.', '{"how_it_works":"A file that another program, or a later version of your own, must read needs to describe itself. A header at the start does that. It usually begins with a magic number, a few fixed bytes such as the letters PNG or CCBF, so a reader can tell at once whether it was handed the right kind of file. Then comes a version number, so the format can change later without old files being misread.\n\nAfter that come the fields the reader needs before the data: counts, sizes, table lengths, and the parameters used to write the data, such as a hash count or a frequency table. Each field has a fixed size and a fixed byte order, so the reader knows exactly where every field starts.\n\nA careful reader checks everything before trusting it. It checks the magic and the version first and refuses anything it does not recognise. It checks that sizes are plausible and that the file is long enough to hold what the header promises. A header that says the data is four gigabytes long should not make the program try to allocate four gigabytes.\n\nThe header is a contract between the writer and every reader. Write it down, so that the format outlives the program that made it.","examples":["Every PNG file starts with the same eight bytes, which also catch a file damaged by a transfer that changed line endings.","A compressed file stores its frequency table in the header, so the decoder can rebuild the same Huffman tree.","A program that skips the version check reads a newer file as garbage, instead of saying the file is too new."],"misconceptions":["The file extension says what the file is. Anyone can rename a file; the magic number is the real evidence.","If the program wrote it, the program can read it. A later version, another platform or a damaged file may not match.","Header values can be trusted. A damaged or hostile file can claim any size, so check before allocating."],"elsewhere":["The file command on Unix identifies files by their magic numbers, not their names.","Network packets carry headers with versions and lengths for the same reasons.","Database files start with a header holding the page size and format version."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('byte-order', 'Byte order', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A number wider than one byte can be stored with its most significant byte first, big-endian, or least significant first, little-endian. Network protocols use big-endian, called network byte order, so programs must write and read multi-byte fields explicitly.', '{"how_it_works":"Memory and files hold bytes, but many values need more than one byte: a 16-bit number spans two bytes and a 32-bit number four. There are two common ways to lay them out. Big-endian puts the most significant byte first, the way we write numbers, so 22 as a 16-bit number is the bytes 00 16 in hexadecimal. Little-endian puts the least significant byte first, so the same number is 16 00.\n\nMost desktop and phone processors are little-endian, while internet protocols such as IP, TCP, UDP and DNS specify big-endian, which is therefore called network byte order. A program that copies a number from memory straight onto the wire sends it in the machine''s order, which is wrong on most machines. The other side then reads a different number: the bytes 16 00 read as big-endian are 5632.\n\nThe safe habit is to build and read every multi-byte field explicitly in the order the format specifies, byte by byte or with the conversion functions every language provides, and never to depend on the machine''s own order. Formats define their order and stick to it: some file formats are little-endian, some big-endian, and a few, like UTF-16 text, mark the order with a byte order mark at the start.\n\nByte order only applies to values wider than a byte. A sequence of single bytes, such as ASCII text, reads the same either way.","examples":["A DNS client writes its query ID 22 in the machine''s order, the server echoes back the bytes it received, and the client reads them as 5632 and rejects the reply as a mismatch.","A file written by a program on one machine and read on another with a different byte order gives nonsense numbers unless the format fixes the order.","Network packet captures show port 53 as the bytes 00 35, because 53 is 0x35 in big-endian order."],"misconceptions":["Byte order affects every byte. It affects only values wider than one byte.","The machine handles it for you. Only if you use the conversion explicitly, or build the bytes yourself.","Big-endian is the norm everywhere. Most processors are little-endian; networks use big-endian."],"elsewhere":["Binary file formats such as images, archives and databases each state their byte order.","UTF-16 text uses a byte order mark to say which order its 16-bit units are in.","Hash and checksum outputs are defined as byte sequences so that every machine gets the same result."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('round-trip-testing', 'Round-trip testing', 'process-testing', 'foundation', null, null, 'When one step undoes another, such as compress and decompress or save and load, feed many inputs through both and check that you get back exactly what you started with, byte for byte. Include awkward inputs, such as an empty file, a single repeated symbol and every possible byte value.', '{"how_it_works":"Many programs come in pairs that undo each other: compress and decompress, encode and decode, save and load, serialise and parse. A round-trip test runs the pair one after the other and checks that the output equals the input exactly, byte for byte.\n\nThe strength of the test is that you do not need to know what the middle step should produce. You do not have to predict the compressed bytes; you only check that decompressing them gives back the original. That lets you test many inputs cheaply, including generated ones.\n\nThe inputs are where the bugs hide. Try an empty input, a single symbol repeated many times, every byte value once, very large inputs, and inputs whose length lands exactly on a boundary such as a multiple of 8 bits. Property-based testing generates hundreds of random inputs automatically and shrinks any failure down to the smallest input that still breaks.\n\nA round trip proves the pair is consistent, not that each half is correct. A compressor that writes the input unchanged and a decompressor that copies it back pass every round trip but compress nothing. So add a few checks on the middle result too, such as that the output is smaller or that a known file decodes to a known text.","examples":["Compressing and decompressing a novel and comparing the result with the original catches a padding bug that only shows when the bit count is not a multiple of 8.","Saving a document and loading it again shows that dates lost their time zone in between.","A JSON library is tested by parsing what it printed and checking the values match."],"misconceptions":["If the round trip passes, both halves are right. Both halves can be wrong in matching ways.","A few hand-picked inputs are enough. Edge cases such as empty or single-symbol input are where the bugs are.","Looking at the output is a test. Only an exact byte comparison catches a single changed bit."],"elsewhere":["Database backups are trusted only after a restore has been tried.","Translation tools are checked by translating back and comparing meaning, the same idea for human language.","Network protocol code is tested by encoding a message and decoding it on the other side."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('hash-functions', 'Hash functions', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'A hash function turns any input into a fixed-size number. The same input always gives the same number, and a good non-cryptographic hash such as FNV-1a spreads similar inputs across the whole range. Different inputs can still collide, so code that uses hashes has to decide what a collision means.', '{"how_it_works":"A hash function turns any input, such as a word or a file, into a fixed-size number. The same input always gives the same number, and a good function spreads different inputs evenly across all possible numbers, so similar inputs such as cat and cats get unrelated results.\n\nHash functions come in two families. Non-cryptographic ones, such as FNV-1a or MurmurHash, are built for speed and even spread. They are right for hash tables and Bloom filters, but an attacker can easily find two inputs with the same hash. Cryptographic ones, such as SHA-256, are slower but designed so that nobody can find a collision or work backwards from the output. MD5 and SHA-1 used to be in this family but are broken for that purpose.\n\nTo pick one of m slots, the hash is reduced to the range 0 to m minus 1, usually with the remainder after dividing by m. A Bloom filter needs k different positions per item. Rather than k separate functions, it is common to compute two hashes and combine them as the first plus i times the second, for i from 0 to k minus 1, which is known to work about as well.\n\nA hash is a fingerprint, not a unique id. Different inputs can share a hash, so the code that uses it must allow for that.","examples":["A hash table puts a key in the slot its hash points to, so a lookup goes straight there instead of searching every entry.","A Bloom filter derives seven bit positions for a word from two FNV-1a hashes instead of running seven separate functions.","A website that used a weak hash for its tables was slowed to a crawl by requests crafted to collide."],"misconceptions":["Equal hashes mean equal inputs. Different inputs can share a hash; only the inputs themselves can prove equality.","Any hash is fine for security. Fast hashes such as FNV-1a are easy to attack on purpose.","A hash of a small input is smaller. The output always has the same fixed size."],"elsewhere":["Git names every file and commit by a cryptographic hash of its content.","Load balancers hash a client id to pick a server, so the same client keeps reaching the same one.","Passwords are stored as slow, salted hashes, never as fast ones."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('bloom-filter', 'Bloom filters', 'data-stores', 'foundation', null, null, 'A Bloom filter answers "is this item in the set?" with a bit array and several hash functions. Adding an item sets the bit chosen by each hash, and a lookup that finds any of those bits clear means definitely not present. All bits set only means probably present, and the bit count and hash count are chosen from the expected number of items and the false positive rate you accept.', '{"how_it_works":"A Bloom filter answers one question, have I seen this item, using far less memory than storing the items. It is an array of m bits, all zero at first, and k hash functions that each map an item to one bit position.\n\nTo add an item, compute its k positions and set those bits to one. To look an item up, compute the same k positions. If any of those bits is zero, the item was definitely never added, because adding it would have set them all. If all of them are one, the item was probably added, but those bits may also have been set by other items.\n\nSo a Bloom filter has no false negatives and some false positives. It never says no to something that is there, and it sometimes says yes to something that is not. You cannot list the items from the bits, and in the basic form you cannot remove one, because clearing its bits could clear bits that other items share.\n\nThe filter does not store the items at all, so its size depends only on how many items it holds and the false positive rate you accept, not on how long the items are. About 9.6 bits per item gives a 1 in 100 false positive rate, whatever the items are.","examples":["A spell checker ships a small filter instead of a whole dictionary; a misspelling is occasionally missed, but a real word is never flagged.","A database checks a Bloom filter before reading a file from disk, and skips the read when the filter says the key is definitely absent.","A web browser once checked a local filter of known bad sites before asking a server, so most safe sites needed no network call."],"misconceptions":["A Bloom filter can say an item is missing when it was added. That never happens; errors only go the other way.","You can delete from a Bloom filter. Clearing bits can remove other items too; counting filters exist for that.","Longer items need a bigger filter. The size depends on the number of items and the error rate, not their length."],"elsewhere":["Databases such as Cassandra and RocksDB keep a Bloom filter per data file to avoid needless disk reads.","Content networks use them to avoid caching pages that are only ever requested once.","Distributed systems use them to compare large sets cheaply before sending the differences."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('tcp-sockets', 'TCP sockets', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'A TCP server binds to an address and port, listens, and accepts connections, each of which becomes its own socket. A connection is a two-way stream of bytes that arrives in order, and a read that returns nothing means the other side closed it.', '{"how_it_works":"A server asks the operating system for a listening socket bound to an address and a port, for example 127.0.0.1 and 8080. The address picks which network interface accepts connections: 127.0.0.1 is only reachable from the same machine, while 0.0.0.0 accepts connections from anywhere. The port picks which program receives them. Ports below 1024 usually need administrator rights.\n\nWhen a client connects, the operating system completes the TCP handshake on its own and places the new connection in a queue called the backlog. Each time the server accepts, it takes one connection off that queue and gets a brand-new socket for it, while the listening socket stays open for the next client. So a busy server holds one listening socket and many connection sockets.\n\nA TCP connection is a two-way stream of bytes. TCP guarantees that the bytes arrive complete and in the order they were sent, retransmitting lost packets, but it does not keep any boundaries between sends. Reading from the socket returns whatever bytes have arrived so far, which can be part of one message or pieces of several. A read that returns no bytes means the other side has closed its sending direction.","examples":["Running a development server on 0.0.0.0 instead of 127.0.0.1 makes it reachable from every machine on the café Wi-Fi.","A second copy of a server fails to start with an ''address already in use'' error, because the first copy still holds the listening socket on that port.","A load test opens connections faster than the server accepts them; once the backlog is full, new clients see their connection attempts time out or get refused."],"misconceptions":["People think each send arrives as one read. In fact TCP is a byte stream, so one send can arrive in several reads and several sends in one.","People think accepting uses the listening socket for the conversation. In fact accept returns a new socket per client, and the listening socket keeps waiting.","People think a read returning nothing means ''no data yet''. On a blocking socket it means the peer closed the connection."],"elsewhere":["Every database driver, message broker client and HTTP client sits on top of TCP sockets.","Container port mappings and firewall rules are about which address and port a listening socket can be reached on.","Connection pools in applications exist because opening a TCP connection costs a handshake and time."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('message-framing', 'Message framing on a byte stream', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'TCP keeps no message boundaries: two sends can arrive in one read, and one send can arrive split over several. A protocol therefore marks where a message ends, with a delimiter such as a newline or a blank line, or with a length sent first, and the reader buffers bytes until a whole message is there.', '{"how_it_works":"Because TCP delivers a stream of bytes with no boundaries, every protocol built on it has to say where one message ends and the next begins. That rule is called framing. There are three common ways to do it.\n\nA delimiter marks the end, such as a newline for line-based protocols or the empty line that ends the head of an HTTP request. A length prefix sends the size first, so the reader knows how many bytes to wait for; HTTP''s Content-Length header does this for a body, and binary protocols often send a fixed-size length field. Closing the connection can also mark the end, but then the connection can carry only one message.\n\nA reader that frames correctly keeps a buffer. It appends each read to the buffer, checks whether a complete message is now there, takes it out if so, and keeps any extra bytes for the next message. It never assumes that one read equals one message. Getting framing wrong produces bugs that only appear on slow networks or under load, when messages start arriving split or glued together.","examples":["A server tested only with curl on localhost works perfectly, then fails for a user on a slow mobile connection whose request arrives in three small pieces.","A chat app that treats each read as one message sometimes shows two messages fused into one line, because both arrived in the same read.","An HTTP client reads a response body until it has exactly Content-Length bytes, then reuses the connection for the next request."],"misconceptions":["People think the receiver sees the same chunks the sender wrote. In fact chunk sizes depend on the network and buffers, not on the sender.","People think a length prefix is only for binary protocols. HTTP uses one too, in the Content-Length header.","People think framing is a problem for the network layer. In fact it is the application''s job, because TCP deliberately does not do it."],"elsewhere":["Redis''s protocol prefixes bulk strings with their length, and WebSocket frames carry a length field.","Log shippers split streams into records by newline, which is why a log line containing a raw newline breaks them.","Video and audio container formats use length-prefixed boxes or chunks for the same reason."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('concurrent-connections', 'Serving many connections at once', 'concurrency', 'foundation', null, null, 'A server that handles one connection at a time makes every other client wait, even for a slow one that sends nothing. Giving each connection its own thread, a thread from a pool, or a slot in an event loop lets clients be served side by side. Any state they share then needs protecting.', '{"how_it_works":"A server that handles one connection at a time makes every other client wait in the backlog, and a single slow client, one that is on a bad network or deliberately sends nothing, stalls everyone. To serve clients side by side there are three main models.\n\nThread per connection gives each accepted connection its own thread. It is simple to write, because each thread can read and wait as if it were alone, but every thread costs memory and scheduling, so thousands of idle connections become expensive. A thread pool keeps a fixed number of worker threads and queues the rest, which caps resource use but means a pool full of slow clients still blocks new ones. An event loop uses one or a few threads and asks the operating system which sockets are ready, handling each only when it can make progress. It scales to many idle connections but requires code that never blocks.\n\nWhichever model you pick, anything shared between connections, such as a counter, a cache or a log file, can now be touched by two of them at the same moment and needs protection. And every model needs limits, because a server that accepts without bound runs out of memory or file descriptors under load instead of slowing down gracefully.","examples":["A team''s internal tool freezes for everyone whenever one person uploads a large file, because it serves one request at a time.","A server with a pool of 10 threads becomes unreachable when 10 clients open connections and send nothing.","The C10k problem described in the early 2000s was about serving ten thousand clients at once, which pushed servers toward event loops."],"misconceptions":["People think more threads always means more throughput. Past a point, memory and switching between threads make the server slower.","People think an event loop makes code faster. It makes waiting cheaper; any slow work done on the loop blocks every connection.","People think concurrency bugs need many users to appear. Two simultaneous requests are enough to corrupt an unprotected shared counter."],"elsewhere":["Web frameworks, database servers and message brokers each choose one of these models, and their configuration options expose its limits.","Async and await in modern languages are a way to write event-loop code that reads like sequential code.","Serverless platforms run one request per instance, moving the concurrency problem to how many instances they start."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('udp-datagrams', 'UDP and datagrams', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'UDP sends separate messages called datagrams, each delivered whole or not at all, with no connection, no ordering and no retransmission. It suits small request and reply exchanges such as DNS, where a lost message is simply asked again.', '{"how_it_works":"UDP is the thin alternative to TCP. Instead of a connection that carries a stream of bytes, a program sends datagrams: self-contained messages, each with the destination address and port attached. The receiver gets each datagram as one unit. A receive call returns exactly one datagram together with the address and port it came from, never half of one and never two glued together. That is the opposite of TCP, where the boundaries between writes disappear.\n\nUDP promises very little. A datagram can be lost, duplicated or arrive after one sent later, and nobody resends it. There is no handshake, so there is also no notion of a connected client: a server simply receives datagrams from many senders on one socket and must reply to the address that came with each one.\n\nSize matters. A datagram has an 8-byte header and must fit in one IP packet, and a receive buffer smaller than the datagram silently loses the rest of it on most systems. Large datagrams may be fragmented by IP, and losing any fragment loses the whole datagram, so protocols over UDP keep messages small. Classic DNS limits a UDP reply to 512 bytes and marks a reply that did not fit as truncated, telling the client to ask again over TCP.\n\nBecause nothing is guaranteed, the application adds what it needs: a timeout and a limited number of retries for requests, an ID to match replies to questions, and sometimes its own ordering. In return it gets low latency, no connection setup and the ability to talk to many peers from one socket.","examples":["A DNS lookup is one small datagram out and one back. If the reply is lost, the resolver waits a second or two and asks again, which is cheaper than setting up a TCP connection for every lookup.","Video calls and online games use UDP because a late packet is useless: it is better to skip it than to stall everything while it is resent.","A logging agent that fires metrics over UDP keeps the application fast, at the price of losing a few metrics when the network is busy."],"misconceptions":["UDP is unreliable, so it is broken. It is simply minimal; the application decides how much reliability it needs.","A UDP server has a connection per client. It has one socket and a stream of datagrams from many addresses, and must reply to each sender''s address.","Datagrams can be any size. Large ones are fragmented or dropped, and a receive buffer that is too small cuts them short."],"elsewhere":["QUIC, the transport under HTTP/3, runs over UDP and builds its own reliability and encryption on top.","Service discovery and network time use small UDP exchanges for the same reasons DNS does.","Firewalls and NAT routers keep a short-lived mapping for UDP flows, which is why some UDP replies never arrive on restrictive networks."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('graceful-shutdown', 'Graceful shutdown', 'concurrency', 'foundation', null, null, 'To stop a server without losing work, first stop accepting new connections, then let the work already in flight finish, then close what is left. A time limit keeps one stuck client from blocking the exit forever.', '{"how_it_works":"Stopping a server abruptly cuts off every request in flight: a download stops halfway, a form submission may or may not have been saved, and clients see errors. A graceful shutdown stops in a defined order instead.\n\nFirst the server stops accepting new connections, usually by closing its listening socket, so new clients go elsewhere or retry. Then it lets the requests already in progress finish, while telling clients on kept-alive connections not to send more, for example by answering with Connection: close. Finally, after the in-flight work is done, it closes the remaining connections and releases resources such as files and database connections. A time limit bounds the whole process, so one stuck client cannot keep the server alive forever; when it expires, the server closes what is left.\n\nShutdown is usually triggered by a signal from the operating system or a process manager, such as an interrupt from Ctrl+C or a termination request from a deployment system. The server has to catch that signal and start the sequence instead of dying immediately. Process managers wait a set time and then force the process to stop, so the server''s own limit must be shorter.","examples":["During a deployment, users see a burst of errors because old server instances are killed in the middle of requests.","Kubernetes sends a termination signal, waits a grace period, then kills the container, so a server must finish its work inside that period.","nginx can be told to quit gracefully, finishing current requests with old workers while new workers take new connections."],"misconceptions":["People think closing the listening socket ends the open connections. It only stops new ones; existing connections stay open until closed.","People think waiting for every request is safe. Without a time limit, one hung client blocks the shutdown forever.","People think shutdown handling only matters for huge sites. Every deployment or restart of a small service runs through it."],"elsewhere":["Job workers drain their queues the same way: stop taking jobs, finish the current one, then exit.","Databases flush and close cleanly on shutdown to avoid recovery work on the next start.","Load balancers ''drain'' a server by sending it no new traffic before it is removed."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('http-message-format', 'The HTTP message format', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'An HTTP/1.1 request is a request line with the method, path and version, then header lines, then an empty line, then an optional body. A response has the same shape, starting with a status line such as 200 OK. Every line ends with a carriage return and a newline, and the empty line is what ends the headers.', '{"how_it_works":"HTTP/1.1 is text with a strict shape. A request begins with a request line of three parts separated by single spaces: the method, such as GET, the request target, such as /index.html, and the version, HTTP/1.1. Header lines follow, each a name, a colon and a value, such as Host: localhost. An empty line ends the head. A body may follow, and the head says how long it is.\n\nEvery line ends with a carriage return and a line feed, written CRLF. Clients that send only a line feed exist, so robust servers accept both, but servers should always send CRLF. A response has the same shape, except that it starts with a status line: the version, a three-digit status code and a short reason phrase, such as HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found.\n\nThe first digit of the status code is its class: 2 means success, 3 a redirect, 4 a mistake by the client, and 5 a failure on the server. Clients act on the number, not the phrase, so returning 200 with an error page, or 400 when you mean 404, misleads every program that reads the reply. HTTP/1.1 also requires a Host header in each request, which lets one server answer for several site names.","examples":["A monitoring tool reports a site as healthy because its error page is sent with status 200 instead of 500.","Running curl with -i shows the status line and every header, which is the quickest way to see exactly what a server sent.","One machine serves both example.com and example.org, choosing the site from the Host header of each request."],"misconceptions":["People think the reason phrase matters. Clients rely only on the code; the phrase is for people reading it.","People think 400 means ''not found''. 400 means the request itself was malformed; 404 means the target does not exist.","People think headers end at the first blank-looking line. They end at the empty line, a CRLF immediately after the previous CRLF."],"elsewhere":["REST APIs communicate success and failure through these same status codes.","Reverse proxies and load balancers parse and rewrite request lines and headers on every request.","HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 keep the same methods, headers and status codes but send them in a binary framing."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('rest-api-design', 'Designing a resource API over HTTP', 'apis-backends', 'foundation', null, null, 'A REST-style API names resources with URLs and acts on them with HTTP methods, answering with status codes that say what happened. Clear resources, the right method and honest status codes let any client, proxy or monitor understand the API without reading its bodies.', '{"how_it_works":"HTTP already has a vocabulary for working with things on a server. REST, the architectural style described by Roy Fielding, uses it directly. Each thing the API offers, such as a short link, is a resource with its own URL. Methods say what to do with it: GET reads it, POST to a collection creates a new member, PUT replaces it, DELETE removes it. Requests carry everything needed to serve them, so the server does not depend on remembered conversation state.\n\nStatus codes carry the outcome, and they matter more than the body. 200 OK means success with a body. 201 Created says a new resource exists, with a Location header naming it. 400 Bad Request says the client sent something invalid, and a good error body names the problem, such as a missing field. 404 Not Found says the resource does not exist. 409 Conflict says the request clashes with the current state. 5xx codes say the server failed. Clients, retry logic, caches and monitoring all act on these codes, so an API that answers 200 with an error message inside defeats them all.\n\nRepresentations, usually JSON, are what the resource looks like on the wire. Consistent field names, a stable error shape and a clear content type make an API easy to use. Methods also bring promises: GET must not change anything, and PUT and DELETE must be safe to repeat, so clients can retry them.\n\nGood design is mostly about being predictable. A developer who has used one well-designed HTTP API can guess how yours works.","examples":["A mobile client treats every 200 as success, so an API that answers errors with 200 and a message inside makes the app silently show broken data.","A shortening service answers a POST with 201 Created and the new short URL, and answers a body without a url field with 400 and a message naming url.","Monitoring counts 5xx replies to alert on outages, which works only if the API uses them for its own failures."],"misconceptions":["Any URL with JSON is a REST API. Resources, methods and status codes are what make it one.","The status code is a detail; the body tells the story. Clients, proxies and monitors act on the code.","POST is for everything that changes data. PUT and DELETE exist so that replacing and removing can be retried safely."],"elsewhere":["Most public web APIs follow these conventions, which is why generic HTTP tools can explore them.","API gateways route, cache and rate-limit by method and path.","Specifications such as OpenAPI describe resources, methods and status codes so clients can be generated."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('http-redirects', 'HTTP redirects', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'A server can answer with a 3xx status and a Location header that sends the client to another URL. The status says whether the move is permanent or temporary and whether the method is kept, and permanent redirects are cached.', '{"how_it_works":"A redirect is a reply that says the content is elsewhere. The status code is in the 300 range and the Location header holds the new URL, which may be relative to the URL that was requested. A client that follows it sends a new request to that address, possibly on another host and with another scheme.\n\nThe codes differ in two ways. Permanence: 301 Moved Permanently and 308 Permanent Redirect say the old URL is gone for good, so browsers and search engines may remember the new one and stop asking; 302 Found and 307 Temporary Redirect say it is temporary, so clients keep using the original URL. Method: 307 and 308 require the client to repeat the same method and body, while for 301 and 302 clients historically changed a POST into a GET, and 303 See Other asks for a GET explicitly.\n\nCaching is the part that surprises people. A browser may cache a 301 indefinitely and go straight to the new address on later visits, without contacting the original server at all. A 302 is not cached unless the server says so. For a service that wants to see every visit, such as a link shortener counting clicks, that difference is decisive.\n\nFollowing redirects automatically has risks. A chain can loop, so clients limit the number of hops. A redirect can lead to another host, so clients must not forward credentials or cookies meant for the first one, and should be wary of moving from https to http.","examples":["A link shortener switched from 302 to 301 to look more permanent, and its click counts stopped growing, because browsers now skipped it.","A site moves from http to https and answers every plain-http request with a 301 to the same path on https.","A misconfigured server redirects a page to itself, and the browser gives up after about twenty hops with a too many redirects error."],"misconceptions":["All redirects are the same. They differ in permanence, caching and whether the method is kept.","A 301 can be undone instantly. Browsers that cached it keep using the new URL until their cache expires or is cleared.","Following a redirect is always safe. It can loop, change host, or downgrade to plain http."],"elsewhere":["Single sign-on flows bounce the browser between sites with redirects carrying codes in the URL.","Open redirects, where a site redirects to any URL in a parameter, are abused in phishing.","APIs use 303 See Other after a POST so that refreshing the page does not repeat the POST."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('short-id-generation', 'Short IDs, key spaces and collisions', 'apis-backends', 'foundation', null, null, 'Short identifiers are drawn from a limited key space, such as 6 characters of base62. The birthday effect makes collisions likely long before the space is full, so a service must detect them and choose a strategy: rehash, count, or pick at random and retry.', '{"how_it_works":"A short link needs a short, unique key. Base62 uses the 62 characters a to z, A to Z and 0 to 9, which are safe in URLs and easy to type. A key of length n has 62 to the power n possible values: about 56.8 billion for 6 characters and about 3.5 trillion for 7.\n\nThere are several ways to make keys. Hashing the long URL and writing the hash in base62, keeping the first few characters, gives the same key for the same URL every time, which makes creation repeatable. A counter written in base62 gives every new link the next key, which never collides but reveals how many links exist and lets people guess others. A random key hides that, but must be checked against the store.\n\nCollisions come sooner than intuition suggests. By the birthday effect, among randomly chosen keys a repeat becomes likely once the number of keys reaches roughly the square root of the key space. For 6 base62 characters that is a few hundred thousand keys, not billions. A truncated hash behaves the same way. So every scheme except a counter must detect when a key is already taken by a different URL and pick another, for example by adding a counter or a salt to the input and hashing again.\n\nThe store should enforce uniqueness itself, with a unique constraint or an atomic insert-if-absent, so that two requests racing for the same key cannot both win. Longer keys make collisions rarer but links longer, and that trade-off is a product decision.","examples":["A shortener cuts its keys to two characters for a test and sees collisions after a few dozen links, which proves its collision handling works.","A service that issues sequential keys lets anyone walk through every link by counting upwards.","A team assumed 6-character keys would never collide and lost links when two URLs hashed to the same prefix."],"misconceptions":["Collisions only happen when the key space is nearly full. They become likely around the square root of its size.","A good hash never collides. Any hash cut to a few characters collides; the question is how often.","Checking that a key is free, then inserting it, is safe. Two requests can pass the check at once unless the store makes the insert atomic."],"elsewhere":["Git abbreviates commit hashes to the shortest prefix that is still unique in the repository.","Order numbers, invite codes and ticket IDs face the same choice between counters, hashes and random codes.","Unique IDs in distributed systems combine time, machine and sequence to avoid coordination and collisions."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('key-value-store', 'Key-value stores', 'data-stores', 'foundation', null, null, 'A key-value store keeps values under unique keys and offers three basic operations: put, get and delete. It does no joins or searches inside values, which is what makes it fast and simple to scale.', '{"how_it_works":"A key-value store maps each unique key to one value. The core operations are to store a value under a key, read the value for a key, and delete a key. Underneath, an in-memory store usually keeps a hash table: the key is turned into a number by a hash function, the number picks a slot, and the value lives at that slot. Finding a key therefore takes about the same time whether the store holds a thousand keys or ten million, because the work does not depend on how many keys there are.\n\nThe price of that speed is that the store only knows how to find values by their exact key. It cannot answer \"all users older than 30\" or sort values unless you build extra structures for it, such as a second key that holds a sorted list. Designing keys well, for example session:42 or user:7:cart, is how you make a key-value store answer the questions you need.\n\nValues can have types. Redis stores strings, which may hold text, numbers or bytes, and also lists, sets, hashes and sorted sets. Each key holds exactly one type, and a command meant for one type refuses a key of another type with an error rather than guessing what you meant. Commands on a type, such as adding to the head of a list, are implemented with the data structure that suits them, so they stay fast.\n\nKeeping everything in memory makes reads and writes very fast, but memory is limited and lost on a restart, which is why expiry and persistence matter so much for such a store.","examples":["A web app keeps each logged-in session under a key such as session plus a random token, and reads it on every request in well under a millisecond.","A shop keeps each cart as a list under the user''s id, adding items to the tail as they are chosen.","A cache stores the rendered HTML of a page under its URL, so the next request skips the database entirely."],"misconceptions":["People think a key-value store can search inside its values. It can only find values by their exact key unless you maintain extra keys that act as an index.","People think more keys always means slower lookups. With a hash table the time to find one key stays about the same as the store grows.","People think a value''s type can change freely. In Redis a key holds one type, and a list command on a string key is refused."],"elsewhere":["Browser local storage, configuration services and distributed caches all expose the same get, put and delete model.","Many databases use a key-value engine underneath and build tables and indexes on top of it.","Content-addressed storage, such as Git''s object store, uses the hash of the content as the key."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('idempotency', 'Idempotent and safe requests', 'apis-backends', 'foundation', null, null, 'An operation is idempotent when doing it twice has the same effect as doing it once. In HTTP, GET, PUT and DELETE are idempotent and POST is not, which decides which requests can be retried safely after a timeout.', '{"how_it_works":"Networks lose replies. When a client sends a request and hears nothing back, it cannot tell whether the request never arrived or the reply was lost after the work was done. Retrying is only safe if doing the work twice causes no harm. That property is called idempotency: repeating the request leaves the system in the same state as doing it once.\n\nHTTP defines it per method. GET, HEAD and OPTIONS are safe, meaning they should not change anything, and therefore idempotent. PUT replaces a resource with the given representation, so putting the same thing twice ends in the same state. DELETE removes a resource, and deleting it again leaves it just as removed, even if the second reply says it was not found. POST is not idempotent: posting an order twice usually creates two orders.\n\nIdempotency is about the effect on the server, not the reply. A second DELETE may answer 404 instead of 200 and still be idempotent. It is also a promise each service must keep: a GET that updates a counter or a PUT that appends to a list breaks it.\n\nFor operations that must not run twice but are not naturally idempotent, services accept an idempotency key: the client sends a unique value with the request, and the server remembers which keys it has completed and returns the stored result for a repeat. Designing creation so that the same input returns the same result, such as a shortener returning the existing key for a URL it has seen, achieves the same thing.","examples":["A payment request times out on a train. Without an idempotency key, the app does not know whether to retry, and retrying might charge the customer twice.","A URL shortener that returns the existing key when the same long URL is posted again lets clients retry freely.","Automatic retries in HTTP clients and proxies are usually limited to idempotent methods for exactly this reason."],"misconceptions":["Idempotent means the reply is always the same. It means the effect on the server is the same.","POST can be retried like GET. It can create duplicates unless the service adds an idempotency key.","A second DELETE must answer 200 to be idempotent. Answering 404 is fine; the state is the same."],"elsewhere":["Message queues deliver some messages more than once, so consumers are written to be idempotent.","Database migrations and deployment scripts are written so that running them twice does no harm.","Payment and order APIs commonly accept an idempotency key header."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('token-bucket', 'Token bucket rate limiting', 'apis-backends', 'foundation', null, null, 'A token bucket holds up to a fixed number of tokens and refills at a steady rate. Each request takes one token, and a request that finds the bucket empty is rejected. The capacity sets how big a burst can be, and the refill rate sets the long-run average.', '{"how_it_works":"Picture a bucket for each client that can hold a fixed number of tokens, its capacity. Tokens drip into the bucket at a steady rate, and any token that arrives when the bucket is already full is thrown away. Every request must take one token to be served. A request that finds the bucket empty is rejected, or in some designs made to wait.\n\nThe two settings do different jobs. The capacity is the largest burst a client can send at once after being quiet: a bucket of 10 lets ten requests through back to back. The refill rate is the long-run average: once the bucket is empty, a client can send only as fast as tokens arrive. A quiet client builds up credit, but never more than the capacity, so it cannot save up an unlimited burst.\n\nThe bucket does not need a timer. When a request arrives, the limiter can work out how many tokens were earned since the last request from the time that has passed, add them up to the capacity, and then try to take one. So the state per client is just two numbers: the token count and the time of the last update.\n\nThis shape matches real clients well. An app that opens and makes several calls at once, then goes quiet, passes freely, while a script that hammers the API is held to the refill rate. That is why many public APIs use it. Its relative, the leaky bucket, holds requests in a queue and releases them at a fixed rate, smoothing the output instead of allowing bursts.","examples":["A payments API lets each account burst to a few hundred requests while holding the long-run rate to a hundred per second.","A mobile app sends eight calls when it starts. With a capacity of ten they all pass, and the bucket refills while the user reads the screen.","A network router shapes traffic with the same idea, measuring tokens in bytes instead of requests."],"misconceptions":["People think the capacity and the rate are the same limit. The capacity bounds a burst; the rate bounds the average over time.","People think a token bucket needs a background job to add tokens. The tokens earned can be worked out from the elapsed time when a request arrives.","People think a client that was idle for an hour can send an hour''s worth of requests. It can send at most the capacity."],"elsewhere":["Network traffic shaping and quality-of-service in routers.","Client libraries that pace their own outgoing calls to stay under a provider''s limit.","The generic cell rate algorithm, a token bucket stored as a single timestamp, used in some rate-limiting modules."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('fixed-window-counter', 'Fixed window counter', 'apis-backends', 'foundation', null, null, 'A fixed window limiter cuts time into windows of equal length, such as each whole minute, counts each client''s requests in the current window and rejects those over the limit. It is cheap, but the count resets at every boundary, so a client can get up to twice the limit across one.', '{"how_it_works":"A fixed window limiter divides time into windows of equal length that line up with the clock, for example each whole minute. Every request belongs to the window that contains its time, found by rounding the time down to the start of the window. The limiter keeps one counter per client and window, adds one for each accepted request, and rejects requests once the counter reaches the limit. When the next window starts, a new counter starts at zero.\n\nThis is the simplest limiter there is. It needs one small number per client, it is easy to explain to users, and the counters for old windows can simply be thrown away.\n\nIts weakness is the boundary. The rule is \"at most N per window\", not \"at most N in any minute\". A client can send N requests in the last second of one window and N more in the first second of the next, and both windows accept them, so 2N requests arrive within about two seconds. If the limit exists to protect a slow database, that burst is exactly what it was meant to prevent.\n\nThe other algorithms in this project exist largely to fix that seam: the sliding window log counts exactly over any span, and the sliding window counter approximates it cheaply.","examples":["An API that resets its quota on the hour lets a script spend the whole quota at 9:59 and again at 10:00.","A gym that allows three visits per calendar week turns away a fourth visit on Saturday but lets the same member in on Monday morning.","Daily quotas on free plans, which reset at midnight, are fixed windows a day long."],"misconceptions":["People think a fixed window guarantees at most N requests in any window-length span. It only bounds each aligned window.","People think the boundary burst needs bad luck. A client that knows the window length can aim for it on purpose."],"elsewhere":["Monthly billing quotas and data caps.","Metrics systems that count events per minute bucket.","Login attempt counters that reset each hour."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('sliding-window-log', 'Sliding window log', 'apis-backends', 'foundation', null, null, 'A sliding window log keeps the time of every accepted request. For each new request it drops the times older than the window and accepts the request only if fewer than the limit remain. It is exact, but its memory grows with the traffic.', '{"how_it_works":"Instead of counting requests in fixed blocks of time, a sliding window log remembers when each accepted request happened. For each client it keeps a list of timestamps. When a new request arrives, the limiter first removes every timestamp older than one window length, then counts what is left. If the count is below the limit, the request is accepted and its time is added to the list; otherwise it is rejected and nothing is added.\n\nBecause the window always ends at the current moment, there are no boundaries to exploit. No stretch of time one window long can ever contain more accepted requests than the limit, which is exactly the guarantee the fixed window fails to give.\n\nThe cost is memory and work. The list holds one entry per accepted request in the last window, so a limit of 1,000 per hour means up to 1,000 timestamps per client. Under a flood, or for clients with high limits, the lists are at their largest, so the limiter becomes most expensive precisely when the system is under the most pressure.\n\nTwo details are easy to get wrong. Rejected requests must not be added to the log, or a client that keeps retrying stays blocked for ever. And old entries must really be removed from the stored list, not just skipped when counting, or the list grows without end.","examples":["A library lets you borrow three books in any fourteen days. Each time you ask, the librarian crosses out loans older than fourteen days and counts the rest.","A security system allows five failed logins in any ten minutes, counted exactly from the times of the failures.","A team replaces its log with a sliding window counter after the logs for a few busy customers use most of the limiter''s memory."],"misconceptions":["People think logging rejected requests is harmless. It makes a retrying client look busier and can block it permanently.","People think the log''s memory is fixed per client. It grows with the limit and with the traffic."],"elsewhere":["Sorted sets in key-value stores are a common way to keep one such log per client.","Fraud checks that look at every event in the last hour.","Moving averages over the last N seconds in monitoring tools."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('sliding-window-counter', 'Sliding window counter', 'apis-backends', 'foundation', null, null, 'A sliding window counter keeps only two counts, for the current and the previous fixed window, and estimates the last window as the current count plus the previous count weighted by how much of it still overlaps. It is approximate but costs two numbers per client.', '{"how_it_works":"The sliding window counter is a compromise between the cheap fixed window and the exact log. It keeps fixed, clock-aligned windows and their counters, but it uses the previous window''s counter as well as the current one to estimate how many requests arrived in the last full window length.\n\nSuppose windows are one minute and a request arrives 15 seconds into the current window. The last minute then covers the 15 seconds of the current window and the final 45 seconds of the previous one. The limiter adds the current window''s count to three quarters of the previous window''s count, because three quarters of the previous window still overlaps the last minute. If that estimate is below the limit, the request is accepted and the current count goes up by one.\n\nThe estimate assumes the previous window''s requests were spread evenly across it. If they all came at its start, the estimate is a little too high; if they came at its end, a little too low. In practice the error is small: one large provider measured wrong decisions on a tiny fraction of requests across hundreds of millions. In exchange, each client costs two counters no matter how much traffic it sends.\n\nIt also fixes the boundary burst: right after a boundary the previous window still counts almost in full, so a client that spent its limit just before the boundary cannot spend it again just after.","examples":["A content delivery network uses this method to apply limits across millions of sites without storing a timestamp per request.","A design tool''s API team moved from a log to two counters per user and cut the limiter''s memory sharply.","Just after a new window starts, a client that used its whole limit in the previous minute is still rejected, because the previous count is weighted almost fully."],"misconceptions":["People think an approximation means a weak limit. The error is bounded and small, and it never lets through twice the limit like a fixed window can.","People think the weight depends on when the previous requests happened. It depends only on how far into the current window we are."],"elsewhere":["Rolling averages in monitoring that blend two buckets instead of storing every sample.","Approximate counting in large-scale analytics, where exactness is traded for fixed memory."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('distributed-counters', 'Counting across several servers', 'concurrency', 'foundation', null, null, 'When several servers enforce one limit, the count has to live in a shared store and be updated atomically. Counts kept in each server''s memory let a client spread across N servers get N times the limit, and read-then-write updates in the store lose counts.', '{"how_it_works":"A limiter that keeps its counts in its own memory works only while there is one copy of the server. Real services run several copies behind a load balancer, and each copy then enforces the limit on its own. A client whose requests are spread across three copies gets three times the limit, and the exact amount depends on how the load balancer happens to route.\n\nThe usual fix is to move the counts into a shared store, often an in-memory key-value store, that every copy talks to. Now every server sees the same count for a client. But the store brings back the race from a single server, across the network: if each server reads the count, decides, and writes the new value, two servers can both read 59 against a limit of 60 and both accept. The update has to be one atomic step in the store, such as an increment that returns the new value, after which the server compares the result with the limit. More complex algorithms run as a single script inside the store so that all their steps happen together.\n\nKeys must say whose count they are and for which window, and they need an expiry so old windows disappear on their own.\n\nThe store is now on the path of every request, so its speed and availability matter. Teams must decide what happens if it is down: let requests through, which keeps the service working but unprotected, or reject them, which protects the backend but fails users. Very large systems sometimes keep approximate counts locally and reconcile them, trading exactness for less traffic to the store.","examples":["A payments company runs its limiter against a cluster of in-memory stores shared by all its API servers.","Three web servers each allow 100 requests per minute; a client spread across them really gets 300 until the counts move to a shared store.","When the shared store went down, a team''s limiter let all traffic through by design, so the API stayed up during the outage."],"misconceptions":["People think a shared store alone fixes the problem. Updates must still be atomic, or counts are lost.","People think the limiter cannot affect availability. It now depends on the store, so its failure mode must be chosen on purpose."],"elsewhere":["View counters, likes and inventory counts in large web services.","Distributed locks and leases built on the same shared stores.","Approximate counting structures used when exact global counts are too expensive."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('lexicographic-order', 'Ordering text', 'text-encodings', 'foundation', null, null, 'Sorting text compares two strings character by character and decides at the first difference, with a prefix first. Comparing raw bytes puts every capital before every lowercase letter; ordering for people needs locale collation rules instead.', '{"how_it_works":"Lexicographic order compares two strings the way a dictionary does: look at the first position where they differ and order by the characters there; if one string runs out first, the shorter one, being a prefix, comes first. So apple comes before apricot, and app before apple.\n\nThe real question is how two characters are compared. In byte order, the C locale''s rule, characters are compared by their numeric value. In ASCII every capital letter, 65 to 90, comes before every lowercase letter, 97 to 122, so Zebra sorts before apple, and in UTF-8 byte order matches code point order, so all accented letters come after z. Byte order is fast, stable across machines and exactly what tools such as sort with LC_ALL set to C, or a binary search over sorted keys, need.\n\nPeople expect something else. Collation, defined for Unicode by the Unicode Collation Algorithm and tailored per language, compares base letters first, then accents, then case, so a, A, b and B come out together; it can ignore punctuation, and it differs between languages: Swedish puts ä after z, German treats it near a. That is why sort gives a, A, b, B under an English UTF-8 locale but A, B, a, b under the C locale. Numbers are a further trap: text order puts 10 before 9, so tools offer a separate numeric comparison.","examples":["A report sorted on one server lists Zoe before adam, and on another lists adam first, because the two machines have different locales.","Release files named 1.10 and 1.9 sort with 1.10 first in text order, so a script picks the wrong latest version.","A binary search over keys sorted with a locale collation misses keys, because the search compares bytes."],"misconceptions":["There is one correct alphabetical order. It depends on the language and the collation rules.","Byte order is alphabetical. It puts all capitals before all lowercase letters.","Numbers sort correctly as text. Text order compares digit by digit, so 10 comes before 9."],"elsewhere":["Database indexes and ORDER BY results depend on the column collation.","File managers use natural sort to put file2 before file10.","Merge and join tools need both inputs sorted with the same comparison, or they silently miss matches."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('sorting-algorithms', 'Sorting algorithms', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'Merge sort, quicksort, heapsort and radix sort all put items in order but differ in worst-case time, memory and stability. Any sort that only compares items needs about n log n comparisons; radix sort avoids comparisons by looking at keys digit by digit.', '{"how_it_works":"Every comparison sort, one that learns about the order only by asking whether one item is less than another, needs about n log n comparisons in the worst case, because it must distinguish all n factorial possible orders. The well-known algorithms reach that bound in different ways and with different costs.\n\nMerge sort splits the input in half, sorts each half and merges the two sorted halves by repeatedly taking the smaller front item. It always takes n log n time, is stable, and merges sequentially, which suits data on disk, but it needs extra memory for the merge. Quicksort picks a pivot, moves smaller items before it and larger after it, and sorts the two parts; it works in place and is usually the fastest in practice, but a bad pivot, such as always the first item on input that is already sorted, splits off one item at a time and takes n squared time. Picking a random pivot or the median of three avoids that on real inputs. Heapsort builds a heap and repeatedly removes the largest item; it is in place and n log n in the worst case, but not stable and less cache friendly.\n\nRadix sort is not a comparison sort: it distributes items into buckets by one digit or byte of the key at a time, so for fixed-length keys it runs in time proportional to n times the key length. Real libraries often combine methods; Timsort, used by several languages, finds runs that are already ordered and merges them, which is very fast on nearly sorted data.","examples":["A service sorts records that usually arrive almost sorted; a naive quicksort with the first item as pivot becomes quadratic and times out.","Sorting log lines on disk uses merging because it reads and writes sequentially, which disks and networks handle well.","Sorting millions of fixed-length IDs with radix sort beats any comparison sort because it never compares two IDs."],"misconceptions":["Quicksort is always n log n. Its worst case is n squared without a careful pivot.","Faster than n log n is impossible. It is impossible only for sorts that compare items.","The algorithm with the best big O is always fastest. Memory access patterns and constant factors often decide."],"elsewhere":["Database ORDER BY, index builds and external sorting use merge-based methods.","Language standard libraries choose hybrid algorithms such as Timsort or introsort.","Graphics and GPU code use radix sort for its predictable, comparison-free work."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('external-sorting', 'Sorting more than fits in memory', 'streams-io', 'foundation', null, null, 'When the input is bigger than memory, sort it in chunks that fit, write each sorted chunk to a temporary file, then merge the chunks by repeatedly taking the smallest front line. Only one line per chunk needs to be in memory during the merge.', '{"how_it_works":"An in-memory sort needs the whole input in memory. When the input is larger than that, external sorting splits the work into two phases. In the first, read as much as fits, sort it in memory and write it out as a sorted run in a temporary file; repeat until the input is used up. In the second, merge the runs: keep one current line from each run, output the smallest, replace it with the next line from the same run, and repeat. A small priority queue makes choosing the smallest of many runs cheap.\n\nThe merge only needs one line per run in memory, and every file is read and written sequentially, which is what disks, SSDs and networks handle best. If there are too many runs to open at once, merge them in groups and then merge the results; each pass reads and writes the whole data once, so the number of passes, which depends on memory size and the number of runs, decides the cost.\n\nThis is not a niche trick. GNU sort does it automatically, with a buffer size option and temporary files, which is why it can sort files many times larger than memory. Databases use the same method for ORDER BY and index creation on big tables, and frameworks such as MapReduce sort their intermediate data this way. The pieces, sorted runs and a k-way merge, also appear in log-structured storage engines, which merge sorted files in the background.","examples":["Sorting a 50 GB log on a machine with 8 GB of memory works with sort but crashes a script that reads all lines into a list first.","A database builds an index on a billion-row table by writing sorted runs to disk and merging them.","A search engine merges sorted lists of document IDs for each word, never holding all of them at once."],"misconceptions":["Sorting needs all the data in memory. Runs on disk and a merge need only one line per run.","More memory only makes it faster. It reduces the number of runs and merge passes, which is where the time goes.","Merging is the slow part because it compares a lot. It is mostly sequential I/O, the cheapest kind."],"elsewhere":["Databases, MapReduce and Spark shuffles all sort data larger than memory this way.","Log-structured key-value stores such as LevelDB merge sorted files in the background.","Version control and backup tools merge sorted file lists to find changes."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('time-space-complexity', 'How cost grows with input', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'Big O notation describes how the time or memory an algorithm needs grows as the input grows, ignoring constant factors. An order n squared algorithm that is fine for a hundred lines can be hopeless for a million, so the question to ask is what happens when the input is ten times bigger.', '{"how_it_works":"Complexity describes how the cost of a program grows as its input grows, ignoring the constant details of one machine. Big O notation names the growth: linear, written O(n), means twice the input takes about twice the time. Quadratic, O(n squared), means twice the input takes four times as long. Logarithmic, O(log n), barely grows at all.\n\nThe same idea applies to memory. A program that keeps one buffer uses constant memory, whatever the input. A program that keeps every item uses memory in proportion to the input, and will eventually run out.\n\nGrowth matters more than speed on small tests. Comparing every pair of 100 files is 4,950 comparisons and feels instant. Comparing every pair of 100,000 files is almost 5 billion. A method that groups files first does work roughly in proportion to the number of files, and stays fast.\n\nTo reason about it, ask what happens when the input is ten times bigger: which loop runs ten times more, which runs a hundred times more, and what is kept in memory. Then test with an input much larger than your test data, because the slow part often hides until then.","examples":["A duplicate check that compared every pair of files worked on a test folder and ran for days on a real disk.","A report that loaded every row into memory crashed once the table passed a few million rows.","Looking up a name in a sorted list of a million by halving takes about 20 steps instead of a million."],"misconceptions":["Fast on my test data means fast. Quadratic code is fast until the input grows.","Big O is the exact running time. It describes growth and hides constants that still matter.","Memory is only a concern for big servers. Any program that keeps all its input fails on large enough input."],"elsewhere":["Database indexes turn linear scans into logarithmic lookups.","Streaming tools such as wc keep constant memory for any input size.","Interview questions about algorithms are mostly about this kind of growth."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('longest-common-subsequence', 'Longest common subsequence', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'A subsequence keeps items in order but may skip some. The longest common subsequence of two sequences is what stays the same between them, and everything else is what a diff reports as removed or added.', '{"how_it_works":"A subsequence of a sequence keeps some of its items in their original order, skipping others: ACE is a subsequence of ABCDE, but EA is not. A common subsequence of two sequences appears in both, and the longest common subsequence, the LCS, is the longest such one. For ABCD and AC it is AC; for ABC and XYZ it is empty.\n\nThe LCS is the heart of diff. Treat each file as a sequence of lines: the LCS is the largest set of lines that both versions share in the same order, so those lines are unchanged, every other line of the old file was removed, and every other line of the new file was added. Maximising what stays the same minimises what has to be reported as changed.\n\nTwo properties matter in practice. First, the LCS is not unique: two different subsequences can have the same maximal length, and different tools pick different ones, so two correct diffs of the same files can look different. Tests must therefore check that the result is a common subsequence of the right length, not one exact answer. Second, the straightforward method fills a table with one cell per pair of items, which costs time and memory proportional to the product of the lengths; Hirschberg''s method gets the same answer in linear memory, and diff programs use faster algorithms that exploit the files being similar.","examples":["A diff of two drafts reports the moved paragraph as one deletion and one insertion, because the LCS cannot keep both orders.","Two diff tools produce different but equally short outputs for the same files, because they choose different LCS ties.","Comparing two DNA sequences uses the same idea to find what was conserved between species."],"misconceptions":["The LCS is unique. Several can share the maximal length.","A subsequence must be contiguous. That is a substring; a subsequence may skip items.","A longer LCS always means a nicer diff. Humans prefer diffs that keep blocks together, which some algorithms optimise for."],"elsewhere":["Version control, code review tools and merge tools all start from a common subsequence.","Spell checkers and bioinformatics use related edit distance problems.","Collaborative editors compute changes between document versions the same way."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('dynamic-programming', 'Dynamic programming', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'Dynamic programming solves a problem by solving its overlapping smaller parts once each, storing their answers in a table, and building the full answer from them. It turns an exponential search into a polynomial one, at the cost of memory for the table.', '{"how_it_works":"Some problems can be broken into smaller versions of the same problem that overlap heavily. The longest common subsequence of two strings depends on the LCS of their prefixes: if the last characters match, it is the LCS of both prefixes without them plus that character; otherwise it is the longer of the two answers you get by dropping the last character of one string or the other. Computing that recursion directly recomputes the same prefixes over and over, and the work grows exponentially.\n\nDynamic programming stores each sub-answer the first time it is computed. Either fill a table in an order where every cell''s inputs are already known, bottom up, or keep the recursion but remember results, which is called memoisation. For the LCS the table has one row per prefix of the first string and one column per prefix of the second, so filling it takes time and memory proportional to the product of the lengths, instead of exponential time. The answer is in the last cell, and the subsequence itself is read back by walking from that cell towards the start, following the choices that produced each value.\n\nThe method needs two things: optimal answers built from optimal answers to subproblems, and subproblems that repeat. Its cost is the table: when only the final value is needed, two rows are enough, and clever divide-and-conquer methods such as Hirschberg''s recover the full answer in linear memory.","examples":["A naive recursive LCS of two 30-character strings takes seconds; the table version is instant.","Spell checkers compute edit distances to suggest corrections with the same table technique.","Route planners and text layout engines that break paragraphs into lines are dynamic programs."],"misconceptions":["Dynamic programming is a kind of fast recursion. It is storing answers to overlapping subproblems so each is solved once.","The table is free. It costs memory proportional to the number of subproblems.","Every recursive problem benefits. It helps only when subproblems repeat."],"elsewhere":["Edit distance, sequence alignment in genetics and shortest paths use dynamic programming.","Compilers use it for instruction selection and register allocation.","Caching function results in any program applies the same memoisation idea."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('edit-scripts', 'Describing changes as an edit script', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A diff describes how to turn the old version into the new one as removals and additions around the lines that stay the same. A shortest edit script is the flip side of a longest common subsequence, and the output format decides who, or what, can read it.', '{"how_it_works":"An edit script is a list of operations that turns one sequence into another: delete these lines of the old file, insert those lines of the new one, and keep everything else. Finding the shortest edit script and finding the longest common subsequence are the same problem seen from two sides: the lines kept are the LCS, and every other line is a deletion or an insertion, so the script length equals the total lines of both files minus twice the LCS length.\n\nThe script must say where each change happens. The classic normal diff format prints a command such as 3c3 or 5d4 giving line numbers in both files, then the removed lines marked with a less-than sign and the added lines with a greater-than sign. A change is simply a deletion and an insertion at the same place. The output is ordered from the top of the file down, and line numbers refer to the original files, so a reader, or a program such as patch, can apply the script.\n\nChoices remain even for the shortest script. When the LCS has ties, the deletions and insertions can be placed differently and still be minimal, so tools add rules, such as preferring to keep blank lines and braces as context, to make diffs easier for people to read. And diff also reports whether files differ through its exit status, 0 for identical, 1 for different and 2 for trouble, so scripts can act on it without parsing the text.","examples":["A code review shows a function as fully deleted and rewritten because the tool paired the wrong closing braces; a smarter alignment shows two changed lines.","A backup script runs diff and skips the copy when the exit status says the files are identical.","A patch file mailed to a project is an edit script that maintainers apply to their own copy."],"misconceptions":["A diff lists the lines that changed. It lists operations, with positions, that transform one file into the other.","There is one correct diff. Several minimal edit scripts can exist, and tools break ties differently.","The text output is the only result. The exit status tells scripts whether anything differed."],"elsewhere":["Patches, code review tools and database migration tools all express changes as edit scripts.","Collaborative editors send edit operations rather than whole documents.","Synchronisation tools such as rsync transfer differences instead of full files."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('unit-tests-first', 'Writing tests first', 'process-testing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Writing a small failing test before the code forces you to decide what the code should do, including edge cases, before deciding how. Each test stays as a guard, and one suite that every implementation must pass makes it safe to swap algorithms.', '{"how_it_works":"Test-driven development works in a short loop: write a test for the next small piece of behaviour, run it and watch it fail, write just enough code to make it pass, then tidy the code while the tests stay green. Watching the test fail first proves that it can fail, so it really checks something.\n\nThe benefit is mostly about thinking. A test is a precise question: given these inputs, what exactly should come out? Writing it first makes you decide edge cases before you are attached to an implementation: an empty input, a single item, all items equal, input already in order, input in reverse, no common lines at all. Each test then stays as a guard, so a later change that breaks old behaviour is caught at once.\n\nTests should describe behaviour, not one implementation. When an answer can legitimately vary, such as two longest common subsequences of the same length, the test should check the properties that must hold, its length and that it is a subsequence of both inputs, rather than one exact string. And a single suite that every implementation must pass, for example every sorting algorithm you offer, lets you add or replace an algorithm with confidence. Tests that run the real tool and compare outputs complement these: they find the cases you did not think to write.","examples":["A diff tool rewritten for speed passes the old suite on the first run, so the rewrite ships the same day.","A test written first for the empty input catches that a sort routine crashes on zero lines.","A test that demanded one exact longest common subsequence failed for a correct algorithm that found a different one of the same length."],"misconceptions":["Tests are written after the code to prove it works. Writing them first shapes what the code must do.","A passing test proves the code is right. It proves only the cases it checks.","Tests must check exact outputs. When several answers are correct, check the properties instead."],"elsewhere":["Continuous integration runs the same suites on every change.","Refactoring large codebases is only safe with tests guarding behaviour.","Library authors publish test suites, like conformance suites, that every implementation must pass."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('lexer-states', 'Scanning with context states', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A scanner that keeps a state, such as code, string or comment, reads each character in context, so the same character can be a comment marker in code and ordinary text inside a string.', '{"how_it_works":"Many characters in source code mean different things depending on where they appear. Two slashes start a comment in code but are just characters inside a string or a web address. A quote ends a string, unless it is escaped, or it sits inside a comment. The only reliable way to decide is to read the file from the start and remember what you are inside.\n\nA context-state scanner, a small state machine, does exactly that. It has a handful of states: in code, in a single-quoted string, in a double-quoted string, in a line comment and in a block comment, plus states such as just after a backslash. In each state only a few characters matter. In code, a quote moves to a string state and a comment marker moves to a comment state. In a string, only the closing quote and the escape character matter. In a line comment, only the end of the line matters, and in a block comment, only its closing marker. Everything else is copied or dropped according to the state.\n\nBecause the state carries over from one character to the next, and from one line to the next, the scanner handles block comments that span lines and strings that contain comment markers without special cases. Each language has its own states and transitions: Python has triple-quoted strings, C has character literals and preprocessor lines, Go has raw strings, and some languages allow block comments to nest, which needs a counter or a stack instead of a single state.","examples":["A find-and-replace on two slashes cuts a web address stored in a string in half, while a state-aware scanner leaves it alone.","Syntax highlighters in editors keep the same kind of state, which is why one missing quote can turn the rest of a file the colour of a string.","Minifiers for JavaScript and CSS use a scanner with states to remove comments and whitespace without damaging strings."],"misconceptions":["Each line can be processed on its own. Block comments and multi-line strings carry state across lines.","A comment marker is always a comment. Inside a string or another comment it is ordinary text.","All languages share the same comment rules. Each defines its own markers, string forms and escapes."],"elsewhere":["Every compiler''s lexer tracks context in the same way before any parsing happens.","Editors, linters and code formatters scan with states to understand where comments and strings are.","CSV and configuration file readers keep a quoted-or-not state to handle separators inside quoted fields."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('meaningful-comments', 'Comments that tools read', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Some comments are instructions for tools rather than notes for people: documentation comments, build constraints, code-generation and linter directives. Removing them changes how a program is built, checked or documented.', '{"how_it_works":"Most comments are for human readers, and the compiler ignores them. But many toolchains read certain comments as instructions, because a comment is a convenient place to put information without changing the language itself. Such a comment is often called a directive or a pragma.\n\nSeveral kinds are common. Documentation comments are placed directly above a declaration and are collected by tools into reference documentation; in Go, a doc comment is the group of comment lines immediately before a top-level declaration with no blank line between. Build constraints decide which files are compiled for which platform or configuration; in Go these are go:build lines. Code-generation directives, such as go:generate, tell a tool which command to run to produce source files. Linter directives, such as noqa in Python or nolint in Go, switch off a specific warning on one line.\n\nTools recognise these comments by two things: position, such as directly above a declaration or at the top of a file, and an exact form, such as a fixed word with no space after the comment marker. A comment remover, formatter or minifier that ignores this can break builds, silence nothing or lose documentation. The safe approach is to find comments with a proper scanner, then examine each comment''s text against the exact directive forms, and keep those that match.","examples":["Removing a build constraint makes a file meant only for Windows compile on Linux, and the build fails.","Stripping noqa comments brings back hundreds of linter warnings that the team had deliberately silenced line by line.","Documentation sites generated from code lose their descriptions when doc comments are stripped."],"misconceptions":["Comments never affect a program. Directives in comments change how it is built, generated and checked.","Every comment above a function is documentation. Position rules are strict: a blank line can separate a comment from its declaration.","Directives can be found by searching the whole line. They must be matched in comment text found by a real scanner, or a string could be mistaken for one."],"elsewhere":["License headers and code-coverage exclusions are other comments that tools read.","Type checkers in dynamic languages read type hints and ignore markers written as comments.","Shebang lines and editor mode lines at the top of scripts are read by the operating system and editors."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('operator-precedence', 'Operator precedence and associativity', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Precedence decides which of two different operators is applied first; associativity decides the order for equal operators side by side. Together they give every flat expression exactly one meaning.', '{"how_it_works":"An expression written in one line, such as 1 + 2 * 3, is ambiguous until two rules are fixed. Precedence ranks operators, so times and divide outrank plus and minus and the expression means 1 + (2 * 3), which is 7. Brackets exist to override the ranking when you want the other meaning.\n\nAssociativity settles ties between operators of the same rank. Most arithmetic operators are left-associative: 10 - 4 - 3 means (10 - 4) - 3, which is 3, and 8 / 4 / 2 means (8 / 4) / 2, which is 1. Working right to left gives 9 and 4, which is wrong by the usual convention. Raising to a power is normally right-associative, so a power of a power is worked from the top down, because the left-to-right reading would just multiply the exponents.\n\nUnary minus, the minus in -3, is a different operator from subtraction even though it uses the same character. A parser tells them apart by position: a minus at the start, or right after another operator or an opening bracket, is unary.\n\nEvery parser that handles infix expressions encodes these two tables somewhere: as levels of rules in a grammar, as numbers in a precedence-climbing or Pratt parser, or as comparisons in the shunting yard algorithm.","examples":["Spreadsheets and programming languages disagree on whether minus binds tighter than raising to a power, so -2 squared is 4 in some spreadsheets and -4 in many languages.","A shell script that does arithmetic on prices gives a wrong total when a formula relies on left-to-right order that the script author assumed but the tool did not follow.","Query languages give AND higher precedence than OR, so a filter written without brackets can match far more rows than intended."],"misconceptions":["Operators are always worked left to right. Precedence comes first, and only operators of equal rank are worked in the associativity direction.","Associativity does not matter for real programs. It changes the result of subtraction, division and powers.","The minus sign is one operator. Unary minus and subtraction are different, and a parser has to tell them apart by position."],"elsewhere":["Boolean conditions in every language: AND binds tighter than OR, which surprises people reading conditions without brackets.","Template and query languages, such as SQL WHERE clauses, define their own precedence tables.","Any expression parser, from spreadsheets to configuration languages, needs a precedence and associativity table."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('shunting-yard', 'The shunting yard algorithm', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Dijkstra''s shunting yard algorithm turns an infix expression into postfix order in one pass, using a stack for operators and brackets, so evaluation no longer needs precedence rules.', '{"how_it_works":"Infix notation puts operators between their operands and relies on precedence and brackets. Postfix notation, also called reverse Polish notation, puts each operator after its operands, so 1 + 2 * 3 becomes 1 2 3 times plus. Postfix needs no brackets and no precedence: the order of the list already says what to do.\n\nThe shunting yard algorithm, published by Edsger Dijkstra, converts infix to postfix in one left-to-right pass with an output list and an operator stack. Each number goes straight to the output. When an operator arrives, operators already on the stack that should be applied first, those with higher precedence, or equal precedence when the new operator is left-associative, are moved to the output. Then the new operator is pushed. An opening bracket is pushed as a marker. A closing bracket moves operators to the output until the marker appears, and the marker is discarded. When the input ends, the remaining operators are moved to the output.\n\nThe name comes from a railway yard: cars, the operators, wait in a siding, the stack, until the rules say they may join the outgoing train. The algorithm also catches some errors on the way, such as a closing bracket with no partner or brackets left on the stack at the end.","examples":["Calculator apps and spreadsheet formula engines convert what the user typed into an internal postfix or tree form before evaluating it.","Older handheld calculators from some makers asked users to enter postfix directly, which removed the need for brackets and an equals key.","Compilers for simple languages use the same idea, or the closely related precedence climbing, to turn expressions into instructions."],"misconceptions":["Postfix is just infix written backwards. The operands keep their order; only the operators move to after their operands.","The algorithm needs to look ahead in the input. It decides each move from the current token and the top of the stack.","Brackets end up in the postfix output. They only steer the conversion and are thrown away."],"elsewhere":["Stack-based virtual machines run postfix-like instruction sequences, so compilers produce this order for expressions.","Query planners and rule engines turn infix filter expressions into evaluation trees using the same precedence logic.","Pratt parsers and precedence climbing are alternatives built on the same precedence and associativity tables."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('stack-evaluation', 'Evaluating postfix with a stack', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'A postfix expression is evaluated left to right with one stack: numbers are pushed, and each operator pops its operands, computes and pushes the result. Leftover or missing operands reveal malformed input.', '{"how_it_works":"Once an expression is in postfix order, evaluating it needs only a stack of values. Read the list from left to right. Push every number. When an operator appears, pop the values it needs, two for a binary operator, apply it, and push the result. When the list ends, exactly one value should remain, and that is the answer.\n\nThe order of popping matters. The value on top of the stack is the one pushed last, which is the right-hand operand. So for 10 3 minus, the evaluator pops 3, then 10, and computes 10 - 3. Reversing them silently gives the wrong sign for subtraction and the wrong quotient for division, while addition and multiplication hide the bug because they do not care about order.\n\nThe stack also checks the expression. An operator that finds fewer values than it needs means a number was missing, as in a trailing plus. More than one value left at the end means an operator was missing, as in two numbers side by side. Both are reported as invalid input rather than allowed to crash the program.\n\nThis is how stack machines work: the Java virtual machine, WebAssembly and many interpreters evaluate expressions by pushing operands and applying instructions that pop and push.","examples":["A bug that reverses operands passes every test that only uses plus and times, and is found only when someone subtracts or divides.","Stack-based virtual machines, such as the ones behind Java and WebAssembly, run instruction streams in exactly this push and pop pattern.","Printers that understand PostScript evaluate a postfix page-description language with an operand stack."],"misconceptions":["The first value popped is the left operand. It is the right one, because it was pushed last.","If evaluation finishes without crashing, the expression was valid. A leftover value on the stack means an operator was missing.","Evaluation needs precedence rules again. The postfix order already encodes them."],"elsewhere":["Virtual machines and bytecode interpreters evaluate expressions on an operand stack.","Undo histories, call stacks and parsers all rely on the same last-in, first-out discipline.","Reverse Polish calculators let users drive the stack directly."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('floating-point-numbers', 'Integer and floating-point numbers', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Integers are exact within their range, but floating-point numbers store fractions in binary, so many decimals such as 0.1 are approximate and very large integers lose precision. Formats and tools have to decide how they read and print numbers.', '{"how_it_works":"Most programs store fractional numbers in IEEE 754 double precision: 64 bits split into a sign, an exponent and a fraction, which gives about 15 to 17 significant decimal digits. The fraction is binary, so a decimal like 0.1, which is a repeating fraction in base two, cannot be stored exactly. It is stored as the nearest representable value, and small errors show up when such values are added, which is why 0.1 plus 0.2 prints as a value slightly above 0.3.\n\nIntegers have their own limit inside a double. Every whole number up to 2 to the power 53, about nine thousand million million, is exact. Beyond that, the gaps between representable values are larger than one, so a long ID such as a 19-digit database key can silently change its last digits when read into a double.\n\nA text format only writes digits, so the format itself has no precision limit, but every reader converts those digits into some machine type. JSON''s number grammar is strict about spelling: an optional minus sign, an integer part with no leading zero unless it is zero itself, an optional fraction after a dot with at least one digit, and an optional exponent with at least one digit. There is no plus sign, no hexadecimal, and no NaN or Infinity. RFC 8259 warns that numbers outside double precision are not interoperable and lets implementations set limits on range and precision.","examples":["An API sends a 19-digit order ID as a JSON number, a JavaScript client reads it as a double, and the last digits change, so the client looks up the wrong order. Many APIs send such IDs as strings for this reason.","A bill adds 0.1 and 0.2 and prints 0.30000000000000004; money is usually stored as whole cents or in a decimal type instead.","The numbers minus zero, 1E400 and 0.0000001 are all valid JSON, but parsers disagree on what they become."],"misconceptions":["People think a valid JSON number always round-trips. It does only if both sides read it into a type with enough range and precision.","People think 0.1 is stored exactly. It is stored as the nearest binary fraction.","People think 01, .5, 1. and plus 1 are fine JSON numbers. All four are invalid under the grammar."],"elsewhere":["Money and accounting code, which avoids binary floating point for amounts.","Databases with decimal column types.","Calculators and spreadsheets choosing how many digits to show."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('interpreter-loop', 'The interpreter loop', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'An interpreter runs a program by repeating one loop: fetch the next instruction, decode what it means, execute it and move on. Everything it costs is paid again on every instruction executed.', '{"how_it_works":"An interpreter runs a program directly, without first turning it into machine code. At its heart is one loop, often called the fetch, decode, execute cycle, the same cycle a processor runs in hardware. A program counter says where in the program the interpreter is. Each turn of the loop fetches the instruction there, decodes it to decide what it means, executes it by changing the program''s state, and advances the counter, or sets it somewhere else for a jump.\n\nThe state an interpreter changes depends on the language. For Brainfuck it is a tape of cells and a data pointer; for a stack language it is a stack; for a language with variables it is an environment that maps names to values.\n\nThe loop''s overhead is paid for every instruction executed, not every instruction written. A loop body that runs a million times is fetched and decoded a million times. If the interpreter works on raw source text, it also skips comments and searches for jump targets over and over. That is why a direct interpreter is simple and easy to trust but slow, and why faster interpreters move work out of the loop: they translate the source once into a compact form such as bytecode, work out jump targets in advance, and combine common sequences into single instructions.\n\nThe opposite approach, compiling ahead of time to machine code, removes the decoding from run time entirely, at the cost of a translation step before the program can start.","examples":["Early versions of many scripting languages walked the source or a syntax tree directly, and later versions gained large speed-ups by compiling to bytecode first.","A Brainfuck interpreter that reads the text character by character spends much of its time skipping comments and searching for brackets rather than doing the program''s work.","Emulators for old game consoles run a fetch, decode, execute loop over the original machine''s instructions."],"misconceptions":["Interpreters are slow because the language is slow. Much of the cost is the loop''s overhead, which better designs reduce.","Interpreting and compiling are opposites with nothing between. Most real systems compile to bytecode and then interpret that.","The cost of an instruction is paid once. It is paid every time the instruction is executed."],"elsewhere":["Language runtimes for Python, Ruby and Lua run a bytecode interpreter loop.","Processors run the same fetch, decode, execute cycle in hardware.","Rule engines and template engines interpret their own small languages with the same structure."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('bracket-matching', 'Matching brackets with a stack', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Nested brackets are matched with a stack: each opening bracket is pushed and each closing bracket pops its partner. One pass finds every unmatched or wrongly paired bracket and records which pairs belong together.', '{"how_it_works":"Brackets nest: the most recently opened bracket must be the first one closed. A stack captures exactly that rule. Walk the text once. Push each opening bracket, with its position. When a closing bracket appears, pop the top of the stack: if the stack is empty, the closing bracket has no partner; if the popped bracket is a different kind, such as a round one closed by a square one, the pair is wrong. When the text ends, anything left on the stack was opened and never closed.\n\nCounting is not enough. A counter that adds one for each opening bracket and subtracts one for each closing bracket accepts a closing bracket of the wrong kind, and only notices order problems if it checks that the count never goes negative. The stack handles both, and because it stores positions, error messages can point at the exact bracket.\n\nThe same pass can record the partner of every bracket. Interpreters and editors use this to jump from a bracket to its match without searching: an interpreter that pairs every loop bracket before running never has to scan the program to find where a loop ends, which turns a slow search on every iteration into a single lookup.\n\nIn formal terms, balanced brackets form a language that no regular expression can recognise, because recognising it needs unbounded memory of what is still open. That is why the stack, or recursion, appears.","examples":["Code editors highlight the partner of the bracket under the cursor, using pairs worked out by the same stack walk.","An interpreter for a language with loop brackets that searches for the matching bracket on every pass becomes dramatically slower on programs with long loops.","Configuration and data formats such as JSON reject a document with a missing closing brace, and a good error points at the opening brace that was never closed."],"misconceptions":["Counting opening and closing brackets is enough. It misses wrong kinds and some wrong orders.","A regular expression can check balanced brackets. Arbitrary nesting needs a stack or recursion.","Unmatched brackets can only be found while running the program. One pass before running finds them all."],"elsewhere":["Parsers for every programming language and data format check nesting this way.","Editors and formatters use bracket pairs for matching, folding and indentation.","HTML and XML validators match opening and closing tags with the same stack discipline."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('bytecode-compilation', 'Compiling to bytecode', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'Compiling source to bytecode translates the program once into a compact list of instructions with their operands worked out, so the run-time loop only executes and never reads text.', '{"how_it_works":"Bytecode is a compact, regular list of instructions designed to be run by a virtual machine rather than a physical processor. A bytecode compiler reads the source once and translates it: comments and whitespace disappear, each operation becomes an instruction, and anything that can be worked out in advance is attached as an operand. For Brainfuck that means one instruction per command, with each loop bracket carrying the position of its partner.\n\nThe virtual machine that runs bytecode is an interpreter loop, but a much cheaper one. It never skips text, never searches for a matching bracket and never re-parses anything. It fetches an instruction, uses its kind to choose an action, and acts, often with the operand already in hand.\n\nThe split between compiling and running has a further benefit. The instruction list is data that the compiler can inspect and improve before it runs: merging repeated instructions, replacing common patterns with dedicated ones, and removing work that has no effect. This is where optimisation lives in most language implementations. Some go further and compile hot bytecode to machine code while the program runs, which is called just-in-time compilation.\n\nBytecode can also be saved, so later runs skip the translation step, and it can be made independent of the processor, so the same compiled program runs anywhere the virtual machine does.","examples":["Java compiles source to bytecode once, and the same class files run on any machine with a Java virtual machine.","Python compiles each module to bytecode and caches it, so the next run starts faster.","A Brainfuck program compiled to bytecode with jump targets resolved runs much faster than the same program interpreted from its text."],"misconceptions":["Bytecode is machine code. It is run by a virtual machine, not directly by the processor.","Compiling to bytecode means the program is no longer interpreted. The virtual machine interprets the bytecode, just far more cheaply.","Optimisation needs a full compiler. Even a simple instruction list can be improved before it runs."],"elsewhere":["Language runtimes such as the Java virtual machine, Python, Lua and WebAssembly engines run bytecode.","Database engines compile queries into an internal program of operations before executing them.","Regular-expression engines often compile a pattern into a small instruction program for a matching machine."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('peephole-optimization', 'Peephole optimisation', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'A peephole optimiser looks at short runs of instructions and replaces them with fewer or cheaper ones that have exactly the same effect, such as merging repeats or turning a clearing loop into one instruction.', '{"how_it_works":"Peephole optimisation looks at the instruction list through a small window, a few instructions at a time, and replaces patterns with cheaper equivalents. It needs no deep analysis of the whole program; it relies on local facts that are always true.\n\nTwo kinds of rewrite cover most of the gain for simple languages. Merging replaces a run of identical instructions with one instruction and a count: ten pluses become add ten, and five moves right become move five. Pattern replacement recognises a short sequence whose overall effect is known and emits a dedicated instruction instead: a loop whose body only subtracts one from the current cell always ends with that cell at zero, so the whole loop becomes set to zero. Larger patterns, such as a loop that moves a value from one cell to another, can become a single copy instruction.\n\nEvery rewrite has to be safe: it must have exactly the same effect for every possible state, not just the states seen in tests. A loop that adds one ends at zero only if cells wrap, so that rewrite depends on the memory model. Rewrites also move instructions, so jump targets must be worked out again after the list changes. The reliable way to check is to run the same programs with and without optimisation and compare the output byte for byte, and to measure the time before and after, because some rewrites cost more than they save.","examples":["Merging repeated commands and clearing loops makes typical Brainfuck programs run several times faster without changing their output.","Compilers replace multiplication by a power of two with a shift, and remove an instruction that loads a value that is already loaded.","A rewrite that assumed wrapping cells silently broke a program on an interpreter that used larger cells."],"misconceptions":["An optimisation that passes the tests is safe. It must be correct for every possible state, not just the tested ones.","Optimising only makes code faster. A wrong rewrite changes behaviour, so the output must be compared as well as the time.","Jump targets survive rewriting. Changing the list moves instructions, so jumps must be resolved again."],"elsewhere":["Production compilers run many peephole passes over their intermediate code and machine code.","Query optimisers rewrite query plans with equivalence rules in the same spirit.","Build tools and minifiers rewrite code into shorter equivalent forms."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('benchmarking', 'Measuring performance', 'process-testing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Measure before and after a change on the same workload and machine, run it several times, and compare like with like, such as the same request count, concurrency and pipelining. Report latency percentiles as well as throughput.', '{"how_it_works":"A benchmark runs a fixed workload against a system and measures the result, so that two systems, or two versions of one system, can be compared. It is only meaningful if everything except the thing being compared stays the same: the machine, the workload, the number of concurrent clients, the number of requests, the size of the data, and settings such as pipelining. Changing two things at once leaves you unable to say which one made the difference.\n\nTwo kinds of number matter. Throughput is how many requests complete per second. Latency is how long a single request takes from being sent to its reply arriving. They are related but not the same: a server can complete many requests per second while some of them wait a long time, and a single average hides that.\n\nThat is why latency is reported as percentiles. The p50, or median, is the time that half of the requests beat. The p99 is the time that 99 out of 100 requests beat, so it describes the slow tail. Users notice the tail: a page that makes ten requests is slowed by the slowest of them, so a bad p99 is felt far more often than its 1 percent suggests.\n\nResults vary from run to run because of other programs, caches warming up and the operating system''s scheduling, so a benchmark is repeated several times and the spread is noted. Running the load generator on the same machine as the server also competes for the processor, which is fine for comparing two servers on that machine but not for measuring absolute capacity.","examples":["Comparing your server with the real Redis using the same redis-benchmark options on the same laptop shows how far apart they are on SET and GET.","A change that raises average throughput by 10 percent but doubles p99 latency makes the slowest pages noticeably worse.","Running a benchmark once while a backup runs in the background gives numbers that cannot be compared with anything."],"misconceptions":["People think one run is enough. Results vary between runs, so repeat them and look at the spread.","People think the average latency describes what users feel. The slow tail, shown by p99, is what users notice.","People think comparing numbers from different machines or settings is fair. Only like-for-like comparisons say anything about the systems."],"elsewhere":["Web teams track p95 and p99 page load times rather than averages.","Database and storage vendors publish benchmarks whose settings must be read carefully before comparing them.","Performance regression tests in a build pipeline compare each change against the previous version on the same machine."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('candidate-filtering', 'Cheap checks before expensive ones', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'When comparing many items, rule out most pairs with a cheap test first and save the expensive test for what is left. Files of different sizes cannot be duplicates, so group by size before hashing, and only compare bytes for files whose hashes match.', '{"how_it_works":"When a check is expensive, run cheap checks first to rule most candidates out. Each stage throws away the items that cannot possibly match, so the expensive stage only runs on the few that remain.\n\nFinding duplicate files is the classic case. Two files can only be identical if they have the same size, and the size is free to read from the file system. Grouping by size removes every file with a unique size without reading a single byte. Within each group of equal sizes, hashing the first few kilobytes removes most of the rest cheaply. Only files that still match get a full hash, and only files with equal full hashes get a final byte-by-byte comparison.\n\nThe order matters: each stage must be cheaper than the next and must never throw away a true match. Size and partial hashes are safe because identical files always agree on them. A stage that could reject a real duplicate would make the whole method wrong, however fast it is.\n\nComparing every file with every other file costs time in proportion to the square of the number of files: 10,000 files make almost 50 million pairs. Grouping turns that into work in proportion to the number of files, plus small groups.","examples":["Of 200,000 photos, only a few hundred share a size with another photo, so only those few hundred are ever hashed.","A login system checks that a username exists before running a slow password hash.","Spam filters run cheap blocklist checks before expensive content analysis."],"misconceptions":["Hashing everything is simplest, so it is best. Reading every byte of every file is the slow part that filtering avoids.","Any cheap check will do. A cheap check that can reject a real match makes the result wrong.","A match on the cheap check is a match. It only means the item survives to the next stage."],"elsewhere":["Database query planners apply the most selective, cheapest conditions first.","Bloom filters are a cheap first check before a disk read.","Image search compares small thumbnails before comparing full images."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('content-digests', 'Content digests', 'data-stores', 'foundation', null, null, 'A digest such as MD5 or SHA-256 reduces a file of any size to a short fingerprint, computed by streaming the file through it. Equal files always have equal digests, and different files almost never do, but MD5 collisions can be made on purpose, so a byte comparison is the final proof.', '{"how_it_works":"A content digest is a fixed-size fingerprint of a file''s bytes, made by a hash function. If two files have different digests, they are certainly different. If they have the same digest, they are almost certainly the same, and how almost depends on the hash.\n\nCryptographic digests such as SHA-256 make an accidental match vanishingly unlikely, and no one knows how to make one on purpose. MD5 is still fast and fine for spotting accidental duplicates, but since 2004 people have been able to build two different files with the same MD5 on purpose. So MD5 must not be trusted when someone might benefit from fooling it, for example when deciding which uploaded file to delete or which download is genuine.\n\nA digest is computed by feeding the file to the hash in chunks, so a file of any size needs only a small buffer. The cost is reading every byte, which is why digests are used only after cheaper filters.\n\nWhen a wrong answer is costly, such as before deleting a file, confirm equal digests with a direct byte-by-byte comparison. It costs one more read of both files, and it turns almost certainly into certainly.","examples":["Download pages publish a SHA-256 digest so you can check the file arrived unchanged.","Researchers produced two different certificates with the same MD5 and used the collision to forge a trusted one.","A duplicate finder hashes only files that share a size, then compares matching files byte by byte before offering to delete one."],"misconceptions":["Equal digests prove equal files. They make it very likely, and with a broken hash an attacker can fake it.","MD5 is useless. It still detects accidental changes well; it fails only against deliberate attacks.","Hashing a big file needs it all in memory. The file can be fed to the hash in small chunks."],"elsewhere":["Git and other version control systems store content under its digest, so identical files are stored once.","Backup and sync tools compare digests to decide what to copy.","Package managers check the digest of every downloaded package before installing it."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('cache-invalidation', 'Knowing when a cache is stale', 'state-storage', 'foundation', null, null, 'A cache saves work by remembering results, but it must know when a result no longer holds. Store what the result depended on, such as a file''s size and modification time, next to it, and recompute when either has changed.', '{"how_it_works":"A cache keeps the result of an expensive computation so it does not have to be done again. The hard part is knowing when the stored result is no longer true. A cache that serves stale answers is worse than no cache, because it is wrong quickly.\n\nThe usual approach is to store, next to each result, the facts it depends on, and to check them before reusing it. For a cached file digest, those facts are the file''s path, size and modification time. If any of them changed, compute the digest again. The check is cheap because the file system already knows those facts.\n\nThe check can still be fooled. A program can change a file and set its modification time back. Some file systems record time only to the second, so a change within the same second can be missed. Copying a file can keep or reset the time, depending on the tool. When a stale answer would be dangerous, such as before deleting a file, verify directly instead of trusting the cache.\n\nOther strategies are expiry after a fixed time, and telling the cache explicitly when the source changes. Each trades freshness against cost.","examples":["A build tool rebuilds only files whose modification time is newer than their output, and occasionally misses a change made within the same second.","A duplicate finder caches digests by path, size and modification time, and rehashes a file that was edited.","A website showed an old price for an hour because its cache expired only after a fixed time."],"misconceptions":["A cache only makes things faster. A stale cache makes them wrong.","Modification time always changes when content changes. Tools can preserve or reset it.","Caching the result alone is enough. You also need to store what the result depended on."],"elsewhere":["Web browsers revalidate cached pages using headers such as ETag and Last-Modified.","Content delivery networks purge cached copies when a site publishes changes.","Processors keep their caches consistent across cores with invalidation protocols."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('safe-destructive-actions', 'Confirming destructive actions', 'process-testing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A command that deletes or overwrites should show exactly what it will do, ask before doing it, and treat anything other than a clear yes as no. Keeping at least one copy of every duplicate, and logging what was removed, makes a mistake recoverable.', '{"how_it_works":"Some actions cannot be undone: deleting a file, dropping a table, overwriting a backup. A program that performs them must make the safe outcome the default and the dangerous outcome deliberate.\n\nIn practice: show exactly what will be destroyed before doing it, by full path or name rather than a vague description. Ask for an explicit answer, and treat anything other than a clear yes, including an empty line, a typo or the end of input, as no. Never pick the target automatically when a mistake would lose data the user wanted. Offer a dry run that only reports what would happen.\n\nCheck the facts just before acting, not only at the start. A file can change between the scan and the delete, so confirm it is still identical to the copy being kept. Prefer reversible steps when possible, such as moving to a trash folder or replacing a duplicate with a link.\n\nWhen the program is used in scripts, there is no person to ask. Destructive actions then need an explicit option, and the program should refuse rather than guess.","examples":["A duplicate finder lists each set with numbers and deletes only when the user types a listed number; pressing Enter keeps everything.","A cleanup script ran with an empty variable as the folder name and deleted from the wrong place, because nothing checked the target.","Database tools ask you to type the table name before dropping it, rather than just yes."],"misconceptions":["A confirmation prompt is enough. A prompt that accepts Enter as yes protects nothing.","The scan result is still true when the user answers. Files can change while the user thinks.","Power users do not need safeguards. Mistakes in bulk operations happen to everyone."],"elsewhere":["Cloud consoles require typing a resource name before deleting it.","Version control keeps deleted work recoverable for a while, so mistakes can be undone.","The Command Line Interface Guidelines recommend confirmation and a force option for dangerous commands."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('url-parsing', 'Parsing and validating URLs', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'A URL has a defined structure: scheme, optional user information, host, optional port, path, query and fragment. Parsing it correctly, applying defaults and percent-encoding, and validating untrusted URLs prevents both broken requests and classic tricks.', '{"how_it_works":"A URL such as http://user@example.com:8080/docs/a%20b?lang=en#intro has parts with fixed roles. The scheme, http, says which protocol to speak and implies a default port, 80 for http and 443 for https. After the two slashes comes the authority: optional user information ending in an at sign, then the host, then an optional colon and port. The path starts at the next slash. A question mark starts the query and a hash starts the fragment.\n\nThe fragment is never sent to the server; it is used by the client, for example to scroll to a heading. Everything else is used to build the request: the host and port to connect to, and the path and query to put in the request line. A path with no characters becomes a single slash.\n\nOnly certain characters may appear as they are. Others, such as spaces, are percent-encoded as a percent sign followed by two hexadecimal digits, so a space becomes %20. Decoding happens once, at the right time; decoding twice, or decoding before splitting, lets an encoded slash or dot change the meaning of a path.\n\nUntrusted URLs need care. In http://trusted.com@evil.example/ the trusted-looking part is user information and the real host is evil.example. Schemes such as javascript: or data: are not web addresses at all. Hosts are case-insensitive and can be written in many ways, so a service that compares or stores URLs should parse and normalise them first, and should accept only the schemes it really supports.\n\nTwo standards describe URLs: RFC 3986 for the general syntax and the WHATWG URL Standard for how browsers parse them. They differ in edge cases, which is one reason to use a well-tested parser when the input comes from strangers.","examples":["A link shortener that stores URLs as raw text gives http://Example.com/x and http://example.com/x two different keys, although they name the same page.","A phishing email shows a link that starts with the name of a real bank, followed by an at sign and the attacker''s domain.","A client that forgets to encode a space in a query sends a request line with three spaces, and the server rejects it as malformed."],"misconceptions":["The fragment is part of the request. It stays in the client.","Anything that starts with a scheme is a web address. javascript: and data: URLs run code or carry content directly.","Splitting on slashes is enough. User information, ports, IPv6 hosts in brackets and percent-encoding all break naive splitting."],"elsewhere":["Every HTTP client, browser and proxy parses URLs, and differences between parsers have caused real security bugs.","Server-side request forgery attacks abuse services that fetch user-supplied URLs without validating the host.","Database connection strings and many configuration files reuse the URL syntax."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('dns-message-format', 'The DNS message format', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'Every DNS query and reply is one binary message: a 12-byte header with an ID, flags and four counts, then the question, answer, authority and additional sections. Records have variable length, so a decoder walks them in order.', '{"how_it_works":"DNS uses the same message layout for questions and answers. It starts with a fixed 12-byte header: a 16-bit ID chosen by the client so it can match the reply to its query, a 16-bit flags field, and four 16-bit counts giving the number of entries in each of the four sections that follow.\n\nThe question section holds the name being asked about, encoded as labels, followed by a type and a class. Type 1 asks for an A record, an IPv4 address, and class 1 means internet. The answer section holds resource records that answer the question. The authority section holds records pointing at the servers responsible for the name, which is how referrals work. The additional section holds helpful extras, such as the addresses of those servers.\n\nEach resource record has a name, a type, a class, a time to live in seconds, a data length, and then that many bytes of data. For an A record the data is four bytes, shown as four numbers joined by dots. Because names and data vary in length, there is no fixed offset for the second record: a decoder must read each record completely to find where the next begins.\n\nNames are written as a series of labels, each preceded by a byte giving its length, and ended by a zero byte, so dns.google.com becomes 3 dns 6 google 3 com 0. Labels can be at most 63 bytes and a whole name at most 255. Every number in the message is big-endian.","examples":["A resolver that ignores the counts and assumes the answer starts right after the header misreads the question as an answer.","Tools such as dig print the five parts of a reply, the header and four sections, in exactly this order.","A reply with zero answers and records in the authority section is not an error: it is a referral to other servers."],"misconceptions":["Records have fixed sizes, so you can jump to the answers. You must walk each record, because names and data vary in length.","The question is only in the query. Replies repeat it, and decoders must skip it.","An empty answer section means the name does not exist. It can mean a referral; the response code says whether the name exists."],"elsewhere":["Many binary protocols use the same pattern of a fixed header with counts followed by variable-length records.","DNS over HTTPS and DNS over TLS carry these same binary messages inside other protocols.","Email delivery depends on MX records and spam checks on TXT records, all carried in this format."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('dns-name-compression', 'DNS name compression', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'To save space, a DNS name can end with a two-byte pointer to an earlier place in the message where the rest of the name is written. Decoders must follow pointers and also guard against pointers that loop.', '{"how_it_works":"A DNS reply often repeats the same name many times: in the question, in every answer and in authority records. Writing it out in full each time would waste most of a 512-byte message, so DNS compresses names with pointers.\n\nA name is normally a sequence of labels, each starting with a length byte between 0 and 63, ending with a zero byte. Because 63 needs only six bits, the top two bits of a length byte are free. When both are set, the byte is not a length but the start of a pointer: those two bits together with the next 14 bits form an offset, counted from the start of the message, where the rest of the name continues. The pair c0 0c, for example, points at offset 12, which is where the question''s name starts, right after the 12-byte header.\n\nA name may be written partly in full and finish with a pointer, such as a new label followed by a pointer to an existing domain. A decoder reads labels, and when it meets a pointer it jumps to the offset and keeps reading there. The name ends at a zero byte, wherever that is. After a pointer, the decoder must remember to continue reading the record from just after the two pointer bytes, not from where the jump ended.\n\nPointers come from the network, so they are untrusted. A pointer that points at itself or at a later pointer that points back creates a loop, and a naive decoder spins forever. Decoders defend themselves by checking that every offset lies inside the message, by limiting the number of jumps, and by capping the decoded name at 255 bytes.","examples":["The sample reply in the challenge uses c0 0c for every answer name, so each answer spends two bytes on its name instead of sixteen.","A fuzzing tool sends a reply whose pointer points at itself, and a resolver without a jump limit uses all its CPU.","A decoder that keeps reading the record from the jump target instead of after the pointer misreads the type and TTL of every compressed answer."],"misconceptions":["After following a pointer, carry on reading from there. Return to just after the pointer for the rest of the record.","Pointers only appear at the start of a name. A name can start with labels and end with a pointer.","Pointers from a server can be trusted. Anything off the network must be bounds-checked and loop-checked."],"elsewhere":["Compression by back-references to earlier data is the core of formats such as gzip.","Parsers for any untrusted binary format need the same bounds and loop checks.","Email and other protocols that carry domain names reuse DNS-style label encoding."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('iterative-resolution', 'Iterative resolution from the root', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'No single server knows every name. A resolver starts at a root server, which refers it to the servers for the top-level domain, which refer it on, until a server responsible for the name answers. Glue records supply the addresses of the servers it is referred to.', '{"how_it_works":"DNS is a tree split into zones, each run by its own servers. The root servers know which servers handle each top-level domain such as com. The com servers know which servers handle google.com. Those servers hold the actual records for names under google.com. Resolving a name means walking down this tree.\n\nA resolver that starts from scratch sends its query to a root server, with the recursion-desired flag cleared. The root does not know the answer, so it replies with a referral: no answers, but NS records in the authority section naming the servers for com, and, in the additional section, their addresses. Those addresses are called glue; without them the resolver would first have to resolve the name server''s own name. The resolver repeats the query to one of the com servers, gets another referral to the google.com servers, asks one of those, and finally receives an answer with the address.\n\nThings go wrong in interesting ways. A referral may name a server with no glue, so the resolver must resolve that server''s name first, starting again from the root. A server may be down, so the resolver tries another. A misconfiguration can send it round in circles, so it caps the number of queries. The response code says when a name truly does not exist.\n\nMost programs never do this themselves. They send one query with recursion desired to a recursive resolver, such as the one their network provides or a public one, and that resolver does the whole walk, caching every step so the next lookup is faster.","examples":["dig with the trace option shows the same walk: root, then com, then the domain''s own servers.","When a top-level domain''s servers had an outage, every name under it failed to resolve for resolvers whose caches had expired.","A domain whose name servers live inside the domain itself depends on glue records, or no one could ever find them."],"misconceptions":["Root servers know every name. They only know who runs each top-level domain.","An answer with no records is a failure. It is usually a referral to the next servers down.","Your computer walks the tree for every lookup. A recursive resolver normally does it and caches the results."],"elsewhere":["Recursive resolvers at internet providers and public services run this algorithm millions of times a second, mostly from cache.","Domain registrars set the NS records and glue in the parent zone when you register a domain.","DNSSEC adds signatures that let a resolver check each step of the walk."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('resp-protocol', 'The Redis protocol (RESP)', 'apis-backends', 'foundation', null, null, 'RESP marks each value with its first byte, such as plus for a simple string, colon for an integer, dollar for a bulk string and star for an array, and ends each line with a carriage return and newline. Bulk strings state their length, so they can hold any bytes.', '{"how_it_works":"RESP is the format a Redis client and server use to exchange values over a connection. Every value begins with one byte that names its type: a plus sign for a simple string, a minus sign for an error, a colon for an integer, a dollar sign for a bulk string and a star for an array. Each header line ends with a carriage return and a newline.\n\nSimple strings and errors are short pieces of text that run to the end of their line, so they cannot contain a line break. Bulk strings are different: the header gives the length in bytes, then exactly that many bytes follow, then a final line ending. Because the reader counts bytes instead of looking for a line break, a bulk string can carry anything, including line breaks, zero bytes or an image. That property is called binary safety.\n\nAn array header gives the number of elements, and each element is itself a full RESP value, so arrays can nest. Clients send every command as an array of bulk strings: the command name first, then its arguments. A length of minus one marks a null bulk string or null array, which is how a server says \"no value\", for example when GET asks for a missing key.\n\nThe design mixes two framing ideas. Delimiters, the line endings, are easy to read and debug by eye. Length prefixes make large and binary values safe and let the reader know in advance how much to wait for. A parser has to handle both, and must also cope with a value that has only partly arrived.","examples":["Typing PING in redis-cli sends an array of one bulk string holding the four letters, and the server replies with the simple string PONG.","Storing a JPEG thumbnail with SET works because the image bytes travel inside a bulk string whose length is stated up front.","GET on a missing key returns a bulk string with length minus one, which redis-cli shows as (nil)."],"misconceptions":["People think RESP is a line-based text protocol. Headers are lines, but bulk strings are read by length and may contain any bytes, including line breaks.","People think a simple string and a bulk string are interchangeable. A simple string cannot hold a line break and has no length, so values from users must go in bulk strings.","People think an empty bulk string and a null bulk string are the same. Length zero is an empty value; length minus one means there is no value at all."],"elsewhere":["HTTP/1.1 uses the same two ideas: headers end with line endings, and the body length comes from a Content-Length header.","Many binary formats and network protocols use type, length and value records so a reader can skip fields it does not understand.","Message queues and database wire protocols prefix each message with its length so a reader on a byte stream knows where one ends."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('key-expiry', 'Expiring keys', 'data-stores', 'foundation', null, null, 'A key with a time to live disappears after a set time. Storing the absolute moment it expires, rather than the time left, keeps the four expiry options consistent, and expired keys are removed both when read and by a light background sweep.', '{"how_it_works":"Expiry lets a key remove itself after a while, which is how caches and sessions stay fresh and how memory is reclaimed without anyone deleting keys by hand. The time can be given in different ways: a number of seconds or milliseconds from now, or an absolute moment as a Unix time. The simplest design converts all of them, as soon as the command arrives, into one absolute expiry moment stored next to the key. Then every check is the same comparison: has the current time passed the stored moment?\n\nThere are two complementary ways to enforce it. Lazy expiry checks the moment whenever a key is accessed, and treats an expired key as missing, deleting it on the spot. This guarantees no client ever reads an expired value, at almost no cost. But a key that is never read again would sit in memory forever.\n\nActive expiry fixes that with a background task. Scanning every key would cost too much with millions of keys, so Redis instead tests a small random sample of keys that have an expiry, deletes the expired ones, and repeats straight away if a large share of the sample had expired. The work per round stays small and steady, and memory is reclaimed over time.\n\nClocks matter. Using the wall clock means a clock change can make keys expire early or late, while timing durations with a clock that only moves forward avoids jumps. Copies of the data on other machines also need the same expiry, which is why an absolute moment travels better than a remaining duration.","examples":["A login session is stored with a 30-minute expiry, so a user who walks away is logged out without any clean-up job.","A cache of exchange rates expires every minute, so the next request after that fetches fresh rates.","A one-time password is stored with a five-minute expiry and is useless after that even if it leaks."],"misconceptions":["People think an expired key is deleted at exactly the moment it expires. It is hidden from readers at once, but its memory may be reclaimed a little later by the background sweep.","People think the server must scan all keys to expire them. Sampling a few keys repeatedly keeps the cost small however many keys there are.","People think storing the remaining seconds is enough. The remaining time changes every second, while an absolute moment stays correct and is easy to compare."],"elsewhere":["DNS records and HTTP caches carry a time to live that says how long an answer may be reused.","Network packets have a hop limit, also called time to live, so a packet caught in a loop is dropped.","Job queues and locks often use expiry so a crashed worker does not hold work or a lock forever."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('snapshot-persistence', 'Saving a snapshot to disk', 'state-storage', 'foundation', null, null, 'An in-memory store can survive a restart by writing its whole state to a file and reading it back at startup. Writing to a temporary file and renaming it over the old one means a crash mid-write leaves the previous snapshot intact, and anything changed since the last snapshot is lost on a crash.', '{"how_it_works":"An in-memory store loses everything when the process stops. A snapshot is the simplest cure: write the whole state to a file at a moment in time, and read that file back when the server starts. Redis calls its snapshot an RDB file and can take one on a schedule or when asked with SAVE.\n\nWhat a snapshot gives you is the state at the moment it was taken, no more. Every change made after the last snapshot is lost if the server crashes, so the interval between snapshots is the amount of data you accept losing. Snapshots are compact and fast to load, which makes them good for backups and restarts, but poor when every write matters.\n\nHow the file is written matters as much as when. If the server overwrites the old file in place and crashes halfway, it leaves a file that is part old and part new, and possibly unreadable, so both snapshots are gone. The safe pattern is to write the complete new snapshot to a temporary file in the same folder and then rename it over the old one. On most systems a rename within one file system replaces the old name in a single step, so any reader sees either the whole old snapshot or the whole new one.\n\nWriting a snapshot of a busy store also takes time. A server must either pause, which is what SAVE does, or keep serving while it writes, which Redis does by writing from a separate copy of the process so the snapshot stays consistent while new commands are served.","examples":["A cache server restarts after an upgrade and is warm again within seconds because it reloads last night''s snapshot instead of starting empty.","A program that overwrites its settings file in place and crashes halfway leaves a half-written file that will not load on the next start.","A team keeps hourly snapshots as backups and accepts losing up to an hour of cache data in a crash."],"misconceptions":["People think a snapshot keeps every change. It keeps the state at one moment; everything after it is lost in a crash.","People think overwriting the old file is fine because writes are fast. A crash during the write can leave a corrupt file and no good snapshot at all.","People think a completed write is on disk. It may still be in the operating system''s memory until it is flushed."],"elsewhere":["Games save progress to a temporary file and rename it, so a crash while saving does not destroy the old save.","Virtual machines and file systems take snapshots to roll back to a known state.","Databases combine a periodic snapshot with a log of later changes, so recovery loads the snapshot and replays the log."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('append-only-log', 'Append-only log storage', 'state-storage', 'foundation', null, null, 'An append-only store never changes a record in place: every write, including an update, adds a new record at the end of the active file. Appending is fast and a crash can only damage the last record. The latest record for a key wins, and old ones are cleaned up later.', '{"how_it_works":"An append-only log stores data by only ever adding records to the end of a file. An update does not change the old value in place; it appends a new record with the new value. The latest record for a key wins.\n\nThis makes writes fast and simple. Appending to the end of a file is sequential writing, the fastest thing a disk can do, and it never needs to find and rewrite a block in the middle. It also makes crashes easier to survive: a crash can only damage the last record being written, never data written earlier, because earlier bytes are never touched again.\n\nThe cost is space and reads. Every update leaves the old value behind as dead data, so the file keeps growing, and something must eventually reclaim the space by compaction. Finding a key''s latest value in a long log would mean scanning it, so log stores keep an index that points straight at the latest record.\n\nTo keep files manageable, the active file is closed when it reaches a size limit and a new one is started. Closed files are never written again, which makes them easy to back up, copy and merge.","examples":["Bitcask writes every set and delete as a new record at the end of the active file and closes it at a size limit.","Database write-ahead logs record every change before it is applied, so the change can be replayed after a crash.","Event-sourced systems store every event that happened, and rebuild the current state by replaying them."],"misconceptions":["An update overwrites the old value. In a log it is a new record; the old one stays until compaction.","A log grows forever. Compaction rewrites it with only the live values.","Logs are slow to read. With an index pointing at the latest record, a read is one seek."],"elsewhere":["Kafka stores messages as append-only logs on disk.","Log-structured merge trees in databases such as RocksDB and Cassandra build on the same idea.","Log-structured file systems write all changes sequentially for the same speed advantage."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('checksums', 'Checksums for stored records', 'state-storage', 'foundation', null, null, 'A checksum such as CRC-32, stored with each record, is computed again when the record is read. If the two differ the record was cut short or corrupted, for example by a crash mid-write, and it is skipped instead of being trusted.', '{"how_it_works":"A checksum is a small number computed from a block of data and stored with it. When the data is read back, the reader computes the number again and compares. If they differ, the data was damaged, by a bad disk sector, a torn write during a crash, or a bug.\n\nCRC-32 is the common choice for stored records and network frames. It is fast, needs only 4 bytes, and is designed to catch the typical damage: any single flipped bit, any burst of flipped bits up to 32 long, and nearly all other random changes. It is not a security measure; anyone who changes data on purpose can fix up the CRC too.\n\nWhat the checksum covers matters. In a log record it should cover every byte after it: the timestamp, the sizes, the key and the value. If the size fields are not covered, a damaged size can make the reader jump to the wrong place and misread everything after.\n\nA checksum only detects damage. The program decides what to do: refuse the record, stop at the first bad record in a log, or fall back to an older copy. For a record cut short by a crash at the end of a log, the usual answer is to drop it, because the write never finished.","examples":["After a power cut, a log store finds that its last record''s CRC does not match, drops that half-written record and keeps everything before it.","Ethernet frames and zip files both carry a CRC-32 to catch corrupted bytes.","A disk returned slowly rotting data for months, and only checksummed file systems noticed."],"misconceptions":["A checksum prevents corruption. It only detects it; the program must decide what to do.","A CRC protects against tampering. Anyone can recompute it; use a cryptographic signature for that.","Covering the value is enough. Damaged length fields can make the reader misread everything after them."],"elsewhere":["File systems such as ZFS and Btrfs checksum every block.","TCP and IP headers carry simple checksums against transmission errors.","Database pages store a checksum that is checked whenever the page is read."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('hash-index', 'An in-memory index into log files', 'data-stores', 'foundation', null, null, 'Keeping every value in memory does not scale, but keeping only where each value lives does. An in-memory hash table maps each key to the file, the position and the size of its latest value, so a read is one lookup and one disk seek.', '{"how_it_works":"A log store keeps every value in files on disk, in the order it was written. To read a key without scanning, it keeps an in-memory hash table, the index, that maps each key to the location of its latest record: which file, at what position, and how many bytes. In Bitcask this table is called the keydir.\n\nA read is then one lookup in memory and one read from disk: open the file the index names, seek to the position and read that many bytes. A write appends the record to the active file and then points the key''s entry at the new record. The old record stays in its file but is no longer reachable.\n\nThe index holds every key, so all the keys must fit in memory, though the values do not have to. That suits workloads with a moderate number of keys and large or numerous values. It does not suit range queries such as all keys between a and c, because a hash table has no order.\n\nThe index lives only in memory, so it must be rebuilt when the program starts, by reading the data files or small hint files written for that purpose.","examples":["A store with 10 million short keys keeps its index in a few hundred megabytes while the values fill hundreds of gigabytes on disk.","After an update, the index points at the new record, so the old value is never returned even though it is still in an older file.","A sorted index would let a store answer give me all keys starting with user, which a hash index cannot."],"misconceptions":["The index stores the values. It stores where the values are.","Values must fit in memory. Only the keys and their locations must.","A hash index can answer range queries. It has no order between keys."],"elsewhere":["Many caches and session stores use the same in-memory index over data held elsewhere.","Database storage engines keep page maps that work the same way.","Search engines keep an index from words to the documents that contain them."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('tombstones', 'Deleting with tombstones', 'data-stores', 'foundation', null, null, 'In an append-only store a delete cannot remove the old record, so it appends a tombstone, a record that says the key is gone. Reads and rebuilds treat the tombstone as the latest word on that key, and compaction drops both the tombstone and the old values.', '{"how_it_works":"In an append-only store a delete cannot remove the old record, because nothing is ever changed in place. Instead the store appends a special record, a tombstone, that says this key is deleted. Readers that find a tombstone as the latest record treat the key as missing.\n\nThe tombstone has to stay until every older record for the key is gone. If compaction removed the tombstone but left an older value in another file, the next restart would rebuild the index from that older value and the deleted key would come back to life. That is the classic tombstone bug.\n\nSo compaction removes a key''s old values and its tombstone together, only when it is sure no older copy of the key remains anywhere. Until then tombstones take space, and a store with many deletes can be slowed down by them.\n\nIn systems spread over many machines the problem grows: a machine that was offline when the delete happened may still hold the old value, so tombstones are kept for a grace period long enough for every machine to hear about the delete.","examples":["After set a 1, then del a, the log holds two records for a: the value and the tombstone.","A store that merged away tombstones too early saw deleted keys reappear after a restart.","Cassandra keeps tombstones for a configured grace period so offline replicas cannot resurrect deleted rows."],"misconceptions":["Deleting a key frees its space. Space is freed only when compaction removes the records.","A tombstone can be dropped as soon as it is written. It must outlive every older record of the key.","Deleted means gone from disk. The old value stays on disk until compaction, which matters for privacy."],"elsewhere":["Distributed databases replicate tombstones so every copy learns about the delete.","Sync services keep deletion markers so a delete on one device reaches the others.","Privacy rules about erasing data have to account for values still on disk before compaction."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('log-compaction', 'Compacting a log', 'data-stores', 'foundation', null, null, 'An append-only log keeps growing with stale values and tombstones. Compaction, which Bitcask calls merging, rewrites the closed files keeping only the latest live value of each key, can write a small hint file listing where each key now lives, and then deletes the old files.', '{"how_it_works":"An append-only log grows with every update and delete, while most of its records become dead: values that were later overwritten, and keys that were deleted. Compaction, called merging in Bitcask, reclaims that space by writing new files that hold only the live data.\n\nIt reads the closed files, keeps only the latest live record for each key, writes those records to new files, and points the index at the new copies. Only after the new files and the index are safely in place does it delete the old files. Doing it in that order means a crash at any moment leaves either the old files or the new ones complete, never neither.\n\nCompaction never touches the active file, which is still being written. It runs in the background while reads and writes continue, so it must coordinate with them: a key written after compaction read the old files must not be overwritten by the older value compaction copies.\n\nAlong with the new data files, Bitcask writes hint files: the key, position and size of every record, without the values. On startup, reading the hint files rebuilds the index far faster than reading all the data.","examples":["A store where every key was updated a hundred times shrank to about a hundredth of its size after merging.","Kafka''s compacted topics keep only the latest message for each key, so new consumers can catch up quickly.","A merge that deleted old files before syncing the new ones lost data when the power failed between the two steps."],"misconceptions":["Compaction can run on the active file. The active file is still being written; only closed files are merged.","The order of steps does not matter. Delete old files only after the new ones and the index are safe.","Compaction is just cleanup. It also makes startup and reads faster by shortening what must be scanned."],"elsewhere":["Log-structured merge trees in RocksDB and Cassandra compact sorted files in levels.","Garbage collectors that copy live objects and drop the rest follow the same pattern.","Version control systems pack loose objects into compact files in the background."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('crash-recovery', 'Recovering after a crash', 'state-storage', 'foundation', null, null, 'After a crash a store rebuilds its in-memory state from what is on disk, by replaying the log from the start or by reading smaller hint files. Records that fail their checksum, usually a half-written last one, are dropped, so the store comes back with every write that fully reached the disk.', '{"how_it_works":"A storage program can stop at any instant: a crash, a kill, a power cut. Crash recovery is what it does on the next start to get back to a correct state from whatever is on disk.\n\nAn append-only log makes this simpler than most designs. Data written before the crash is never touched again, so only the record being written at the moment of the crash can be damaged. On startup the store reads the files and rebuilds its index, checking every record''s checksum. A record that is cut short or fails its checksum at the end of the active file is a write that never finished, so it is dropped, and the store continues with everything before it.\n\nA damaged record in the middle of a file is different: it points to disk damage rather than a crash, and the safe response is to report it rather than silently skip it.\n\nRecovery is only as good as what reached the disk. A write the program believed finished may still have been sitting in the operating system''s cache when the power went. Whether the store syncs after every write, every few writes or never decides how much recent data a power cut can lose.","examples":["After a power cut, a log store dropped a half-written record at the end of the active file and served every earlier value correctly.","A store that trusted the size field of a torn record tried to read past the end of the file and refused to start.","Databases replay their write-ahead log on startup to redo changes that were logged but not yet applied."],"misconceptions":["If the program said done, the data is on disk. It may still be in a cache until the file is synced.","Recovery means repairing every damaged record. A torn last record is simply dropped; there is nothing to repair.","Crashes are rare enough to ignore. Power cuts, kills and full disks are everyday events at scale."],"elsewhere":["SQLite uses a journal or write-ahead log so a transaction is either fully applied or not at all after a crash.","Journaling file systems recover quickly by replaying their journal.","Message queues recover delivered-but-unacknowledged messages after a restart."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('text-encoding-utf8', 'Unicode code points and UTF-8', 'text-encodings', 'foundation', null, null, 'Unicode gives every character a number, its code point. UTF-8 stores each code point in one to four bytes, with plain ASCII in one byte, so the byte count and the character count of the same text often differ.', '{"how_it_works":"Text in a file is only bytes. To turn bytes into letters, the program that wrote them and the program that\nreads them must agree on an encoding: a rule that maps each character to bytes and back.\n\nUnicode splits this into two questions. First, which character is it? Unicode gives every character a number\ncalled its code point, written like U+0041 for the capital letter A or U+1F600 for the grinning face emoji, with\nroom for about 1.1 million of them. Second, how is that number stored? That is the job of an encoding such as\nUTF-8, UTF-16 or UTF-32.\n\nUTF-8 uses a variable number of bytes. Code points up to U+007F, which is plain ASCII, take one byte, exactly as\nin ASCII. Code points up to U+07FF take two bytes (accented Latin letters, Greek, Cyrillic, Arabic, Hebrew), up\nto U+FFFF take three (most Chinese, Japanese and Hindi text, and the euro sign), and the rest take four, which\nincludes most emoji. The first byte of a character says how many bytes it has: its leading bits are 0 for one\nbyte, or 110, 1110 or 11110 for two, three or four. Every following byte starts with the bits 10, so a reader\ndropped anywhere in a file finds the start of the next character by skipping bytes that start with 10.\n\nWhy this design? Every ASCII file is already valid UTF-8, so old files and tools kept working. No multi-byte\ncharacter contains a byte that looks like an ASCII letter, a slash, a newline or a zero, so tools that only care\nabout those bytes, such as counting lines by newline, work on UTF-8 without decoding it. And UTF-8 has no byte\norder question, because it is a sequence of single bytes.\n\nSome programs write the three bytes EF BB BF at the very start of a UTF-8 file. This byte order mark only says\n\"this is UTF-8\", and many tools do not expect it.","examples":["The word café is 4 characters but 5 bytes in UTF-8, because é (U+00E9) needs two bytes. A column limited to 4 bytes cannot hold it, and a tool that counts bytes as letters reports 5.","For years the MySQL encoding called utf8 stored at most three bytes per character, so saving an emoji in a username failed or cut the text short. The fix was a separate encoding, utf8mb4, which allows four.","A web address cannot contain é directly, so browsers send its two UTF-8 bytes in percent form: café becomes caf%C3%A9."],"misconceptions":["UTF-8 means 8 bits per character. It means the code unit is 8 bits, and a character takes one to four units.","Unicode is an encoding. Unicode is the list of characters and their numbers; UTF-8, UTF-16 and UTF-32 are ways to store those numbers.","A file''s size in bytes is its number of characters. That holds only for plain ASCII text.","A UTF-8 file must start with a byte order mark. It needs none, and the mark can break tools that read the first line or column name."],"elsewhere":["JSON exchanged between systems must be UTF-8, and so is almost every web page and API.","Databases limit text in bytes or in characters depending on the type, which matters as soon as users type other scripts or emoji.","Go and Rust strings hold UTF-8 bytes, so their length is a byte count, not a character count."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('utf16-code-units', 'UTF-16, UTF-32 and code units', 'text-encodings', 'foundation', null, null, 'UTF-16 stores code points in 16-bit units: one unit for most characters and a surrogate pair of two units for the rest, including most emoji. Java, JavaScript, C# and Windows use it, so their string length counts units, not characters.', '{"how_it_works":"UTF-16 stores text in units of 16 bits. A code point up to U+FFFF, the Basic Multilingual Plane that holds almost\nevery living script, fits in one unit. A code point above U+FFFF, such as U+1F600, does not fit, so it is split\nacross two units called a surrogate pair. The first comes from the high surrogate range D800 to DBFF and the\nsecond from the low surrogate range DC00 to DFFF. Those ranges are never used for real characters, so a reader\nalways recognises a pair.\n\nThis matters because Java, JavaScript, C#, Objective-C and the Windows text APIs store strings as UTF-16, and\ntheir length counts code units. A string holding one grinning face has length 2 there. Cutting such a string at\na fixed length can split a pair and leave half an emoji, which shows up as a box or a question mark. Counting or\nslicing by code point needs separate methods that each language provides.\n\nBecause a unit is two bytes, UTF-16 also needs a byte order: UTF-16LE puts the low byte first and UTF-16BE the\nhigh byte first. A file may begin with the mark FF FE or FE FF to say which.\n\nUTF-32 stores every code point in four bytes. It is simple to index, since the nth code point starts at byte 4n,\nbut it uses four times the space of ASCII text, so it is rare in files.\n\nBefore Unicode, single-byte encodings such as Latin-1 and Windows-1252 mapped 256 values to the letters of one\ngroup of languages. Reading UTF-8 bytes as Windows-1252 turns café into cafÃ©, the garbled text called mojibake.","examples":["A social app limits posts by JavaScript string length. A post of 140 emoji measures 280, so users meet a limit that seems to shrink.","A notification cuts a message at 100 units to fit the screen. If unit 100 is the first half of a surrogate pair, the phone shows a broken symbol at the end.","Excel''s Unicode Text export writes UTF-16LE with the mark FF FE, so a script expecting UTF-8 sees a zero byte after every English letter."],"misconceptions":["UTF-16 uses exactly two bytes per character. Characters above U+FFFF take four bytes, two units.","String length in Java or JavaScript counts characters. It counts UTF-16 units, so most emoji count as two.","UTF-32 gives one unit per visible character. It gives one per code point, and a visible character can be several."],"elsewhere":["JSON writes an escaped character above U+FFFF as two escapes, one per surrogate, so a parser must join them.","Windows file names are UTF-16, so tools that move files between Windows and Linux convert the names.","Any API with a length limit needs to say whether it means bytes, UTF-16 units, code points or what users see."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('grapheme-clusters', 'What users see as one character', 'text-encodings', 'foundation', null, null, 'A grapheme cluster is what a reader sees as one character, and it can be several code points: a letter and a combining accent, an emoji and a skin tone, or emoji joined into a family. The same visible text can also be stored as different code points, which normalisation makes comparable.', '{"how_it_works":"Code points are not the characters a person sees. Unicode builds many visible characters out of several code\npoints, and calls the visible unit a grapheme cluster.\n\nCombining marks attach to the character before them: e followed by U+0301, the combining acute accent, shows as\né. Emoji use the same idea. The thumbs up U+1F44D followed by the skin tone modifier U+1F3FD shows as one thumb\nbut is two code points. A family emoji of a man, a woman and a girl is three person emoji joined by two invisible\nzero width joiners, U+200D, so it is five code points and eighteen UTF-8 bytes for one picture. A flag is two\nregional indicator letters: the Indian flag is the letters I and N.\n\nBecause of combining marks, the same visible text can be stored in more than one way. é can be the single code\npoint U+00E9, called precomposed, or e plus U+0301, called decomposed. They look identical but compare as\ndifferent strings and have different lengths and bytes. Normalisation converts text to one agreed form: NFC\nprefers precomposed characters and NFD takes them apart. Compare and store text only after normalising it the\nsame way.\n\nCounting what users see needs the segmentation rules of Unicode Annex 29, which libraries implement. Most tools,\nincluding wc with -m, count code points instead, so a family emoji counts as five characters there.","examples":["Two accounts both called José can exist side by side if one phone sent the precomposed é and another system sent e plus an accent, unless the sign-up form normalises names.","A form that limits display names to 10 characters by counting code points rejects a short name with a family emoji in it, although the user sees far fewer than 10 characters.","macOS used to store file names in decomposed form, so a file named café there could fail to match the same name typed on Linux."],"misconceptions":["One code point is one character. A visible character can be many code points.","Strings that look the same are equal. They are equal only when their code points match, which may need normalising first.","Reversing or cutting text one code point at a time is safe. It can separate an accent from its letter or break up an emoji."],"elsewhere":["Text editors move the cursor and delete by grapheme cluster, so one backspace removes a whole family emoji.","Search and databases need normalisation and collation rules so that café finds café however it was typed.","Password checks should normalise first, or the same password typed on another keyboard can fail."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('io-buffering', 'Buffered I/O and system calls', 'streams-io', 'foundation', null, null, 'Every read from the operating system is a system call with a fixed cost, so reading one byte at a time is slow even though the data is the same. Reading into a buffer of some kilobytes and working from memory spreads that cost over many bytes.', '{"how_it_works":"Programs do not read files by themselves. They ask the operating system through a system call, and each call\nhas a fixed cost: switching into the kernel, checking the request, copying the data, switching back. That cost\nis small, but paid once per byte it dominates everything else.\n\nA buffer fixes this. The program asks for many bytes at once, perhaps 64 KB, then works through them in memory,\nwhere each byte costs almost nothing. One system call now serves tens of thousands of bytes. Languages offer\nbuffered readers and writers that keep such a buffer behind the scenes, so code can ask for one byte or one line\nat a time without paying a system call for each.\n\nThe operating system buffers too. Recently read parts of files stay in memory in the page cache, and the system\nreads ahead when it sees a file being read in order. That is why counting a file the second time is often much\nfaster than the first.\n\nBigger is not always better. Once the buffer holds some tens of kilobytes, the time goes into waiting for the\ndisk or copying memory, not into calls, and a bigger buffer only uses more memory. Measuring is the honest way to\nchoose a size.","examples":["Counting a 1 GB file one byte per system call makes about a billion calls and can take minutes. Reading 64 KB at a time makes about sixteen thousand calls and takes seconds.","Writing a log without a buffer slows a busy server down, so logging libraries buffer and write out the buffer on a timer or when it fills.","Running wc twice on the same large file is faster the second time, because the file now sits in the page cache."],"misconceptions":["A buffered reader holds the whole file. It holds one buffer''s worth at a time.","When code asks for one byte, the disk reads one byte. Disks and the operating system work in blocks of kilobytes.","Buffered output is written the moment the code writes it. It waits in the buffer until the buffer fills or is flushed, so a crash can lose the last part."],"elsewhere":["Sockets, database drivers and file uploads all buffer for the same reason.","Forgetting to flush a buffered writer before a program exits is a classic cause of missing last lines in output files.","Tracing tools such as strace on Linux count system calls, which exposes unbuffered code."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('malformed-input', 'Handling malformed input', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Real input breaks its format: invalid UTF-8 bytes, a truncated last record, a value out of range. A tool must decide whether to reject it, replace the bad part or skip it, and say clearly where the problem was.', '{"how_it_works":"Every format has rules, and real input breaks them: a file cut off in the middle of a write, a byte that cannot\nstart any UTF-8 character, a log line written by a buggy program. A tool cannot pretend this never happens, so it\nneeds a policy.\n\nThere are three honest choices. Reject: stop and report an error with the position of the bad input, which is\nright when wrong output would be worse than none, such as when loading configuration. Replace: put a marker in\nplace of the bad part and carry on, which is what text decoders usually do, substituting U+FFFD, the replacement\ncharacter, for each invalid sequence. Skip: drop the bad part quietly, which keeps the tool running but hides\nproblems, so it should at least be counted or logged.\n\nIn UTF-8 the invalid cases are a continuation byte with no lead byte before it, a lead byte without enough\ncontinuation bytes after it, a sequence longer than its code point needs, called overlong, and the code points\nreserved for UTF-16 surrogates. Overlong forms matter for security: an old attack wrote a slash as two bytes to\nslip past filters that only looked for the one-byte slash.\n\nWhatever the policy, write it down, apply it the same way at a chunk boundary as in the middle of a chunk, and\ntest it, ideally against a reference tool.","examples":["A log file holds a few bytes of binary data from a crashed process. A character counter that stops at the first invalid byte cannot report anything about the rest of a 20 GB file.","Browsers show U+FFFD, often drawn as a black diamond with a question mark, wherever a page''s bytes are not valid in its declared encoding.","A JSON parser must reject invalid UTF-8 in a string instead of guessing, because two systems that guess differently would read different data."],"misconceptions":["Input from files is well formed. Truncated writes, wrong encodings and binary junk happen every day.","Replacing bad bytes loses nothing important. It changes the data, so the tool should say that it happened.","Being lenient is always kinder. A lenient reader and a strict one can disagree about the same input, and attackers use that."],"elsewhere":["Parsers for JSON, HTTP, DNS and file formats all need a policy for malformed input.","Data pipelines often send bad records to a separate queue instead of failing or dropping them.","Security reviews look for places where two components treat malformed input differently."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('locale-settings', 'Locales and the environment', 'text-encodings', 'foundation', null, null, 'A locale is a set of environment settings, such as LANG and LC_ALL, that tells programs which encoding, character classes and sorting rules to use. The same command can give different answers under two locales, so tests must set it.', '{"how_it_works":"Many Unix tools do not decide by themselves how to read text. They ask the C library, which follows the locale:\nenvironment variables such as LANG, LC_CTYPE and LC_ALL, where LC_ALL overrides the others. A locale name such as\nen_US.UTF-8 or C.UTF-8 says which language conventions to follow and which encoding to decode with.\n\nThe plain C locale, also called POSIX, treats every byte as one character and only ASCII letters as letters. In\nthat locale wc -m counts bytes, so it gives the same number as wc -c, and é counts as two characters. In a UTF-8\nlocale wc -m decodes UTF-8 and counts code points. The locale also decides which characters are whitespace for\nwc -w, which characters grep calls letters, and how sort orders lines.\n\nSo a result can change without any change to the file or the program: another terminal, a scheduled job with an\nalmost empty environment, a container image built without locale data, or a CI runner. Git Bash on Windows, for\nexample, sets a UTF-8 locale in its terminal window but not always for scripts, and its wc counts an emoji above\nU+FFFF as two characters even in UTF-8.\n\nTo get reproducible results, set the locale explicitly whenever a test or script runs a tool, and record which\nlocale an expected value was taken with.","examples":["The challenge''s test file gives 339292 for wc -m in a UTF-8 locale and 342190, its byte count, in the C locale, on the same machine.","A nightly job that sorts customer names produces a different order from the same command typed in a terminal, because the scheduler runs it with the C locale.","A container image without locale data falls back to C, so a tool that handled accented names on a laptop treats them as bytes in production."],"misconceptions":["A program behaves the same wherever it runs. Its environment, including the locale, is part of its input.","The locale only changes language and date formats. It also decides decoding, character classes and sort order.","A UTF-8 file is always read as UTF-8. The tool uses the locale''s encoding, whatever the file holds."],"elsewhere":["sort, grep, awk and many language runtimes read the locale.","Databases have a collation setting that plays the same role for sorting and comparing text.","Number formats differ by locale, such as a comma or a point before decimals, which breaks parsing numbers written on another machine."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('http-keep-alive', 'Persistent HTTP connections (keep-alive)', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'In HTTP/1.1 a connection stays open after a response unless either side asks to close it, so several requests can reuse one TCP connection. That saves handshakes, but the server must frame each message exactly and close idle connections itself.', '{"how_it_works":"Opening a TCP connection costs a handshake, and for HTTPS a longer TLS handshake on top, before the first byte of a request can be sent. Early HTTP opened a new connection for every request, which made pages with dozens of images slow. HTTP/1.1 made connections persistent by default: after a response, the connection stays open and the client can send the next request on it. Either side can end this by sending the header Connection: close, after which the connection closes once the current message is done.\n\nPersistence only works if both sides know exactly where each message ends. That is why a server keeping connections open must send a correct Content-Length, or use chunked transfer encoding, on every response: without it, the client can only find the end of the body when the connection closes, which defeats reuse. On the server side, after writing a response, the code goes back to reading the next request head from the same socket, and any bytes already buffered past the previous request belong to the next one.\n\nOpen idle connections cost the server memory and, in some models, a thread each, so servers close connections that stay idle longer than a short timeout and often cap how many requests one connection may carry.","examples":["A browser loading a page with 30 images reuses a handful of connections instead of opening 30.","curl -v with two URLs on the same host prints that it is re-using the existing connection for the second request.","A server that omits Content-Length on kept-alive connections makes clients hang, waiting for a body end that never comes."],"misconceptions":["People think keep-alive is something the client must ask for in HTTP/1.1. It is the default; Connection: close is what has to be asked for.","People think the Keep-Alive header keeps connections open. It only hints at timeouts; persistence comes from the protocol version and the Connection header.","People think persistent connections let several requests be in flight at once. In HTTP/1.1 they are answered in order, one after another; HTTP/2 added true multiplexing."],"elsewhere":["Database and HTTP client libraries keep pools of persistent connections for the same reason.","Load balancers keep long-lived connections to back-end servers and reuse them for many client requests.","HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 push the idea further by carrying many concurrent requests over one connection."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('slow-client-timeouts', 'Timeouts and slow clients', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'A connection that sends slowly, or not at all, holds server resources while doing nothing. Read and idle timeouts, plus limits on how many connections and how large a request may be, keep a few slow or hostile clients from exhausting a server.', '{"how_it_works":"A server spends resources on every open connection: memory for buffers, a file descriptor, and in some models a whole thread. A client that opens a connection and then sends its request one byte every few seconds, or never sends anything, keeps those resources busy without doing useful work. If enough such clients appear, the server has no capacity left for real users. This is the idea behind the Slowloris attack, which needs only one machine to take down an unprotected server.\n\nThe defence is to put a time limit on every wait. A read timeout closes a connection when no bytes arrive for a set period. A header timeout bounds the total time allowed to receive the whole request head, so dribbling one byte at a time does not reset the clock forever. An idle timeout closes kept-alive connections that have finished a response and are not sending another request. When the server gives up waiting for a request, it may answer 408 Request Timeout before closing.\n\nTimeouts work together with limits: a maximum number of connections, a maximum size for the request head and body, and a cap per client address. Choosing values is a trade-off, because timeouts that are too short cut off real users on slow networks, and ones that are too long leave the server open to exhaustion.","examples":["A small company''s site goes down because one script holds hundreds of connections open, each sending a header byte every ten seconds.","Telnetting to a server and typing nothing shows whether it ever closes an idle connection.","Reverse proxies such as nginx are often placed in front of application servers partly because they enforce these timeouts well."],"misconceptions":["People think attacks need huge bandwidth. Slow attacks use almost none; they exhaust connections, not the network.","People think a timeout per read is enough. A client can send one byte just before each read timeout; a deadline for the whole request is also needed.","People think the operating system protects the server. TCP happily keeps quiet connections open for a very long time."],"elsewhere":["Database drivers, HTTP clients and message consumers all need timeouts, or one slow dependency freezes the caller.","Load balancers and API gateways expose idle and request timeouts as core settings.","Rate limiters and connection caps per IP address are the related defences against floods of fast requests."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('nesting-depth-limits', 'Nesting depth and stack limits', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Every level of nesting in the input costs a level of the parser''s call stack or of an explicit stack. Deeply nested input can exhaust it and crash the program, so robust parsers set a depth limit or use an explicit stack.', '{"how_it_works":"A recursive descent parser enters one routine call for every level of nesting. An array inside an array inside an array is three calls deep, and each call keeps a small frame on the call stack until it finishes. The call stack has a fixed size, often a few megabytes, decided by the operating system or the language runtime. A file containing a hundred thousand opening square brackets in a row is only a hundred kilobytes, yet it asks the parser to go a hundred thousand calls deep. Long before the end, the stack runs out and the program crashes with a stack overflow.\n\nThis is a real security problem, not a curiosity. Anyone who can send JSON to a service can send deeply nested JSON, and a crash on every request is an easy denial of service. That weakness is catalogued as uncontrolled recursion.\n\nThere are two standard defences. The first is a depth limit: the parser counts how deep it is and rejects the input with a clear error once it passes a chosen maximum, such as a few hundred or a thousand levels. The second is to replace recursion with an explicit stack kept in ordinary memory, which grows much further and can be capped by size. RFC 8259 explicitly allows implementations to set limits on nesting depth, so either choice is conforming as long as it is deliberate and documented.","examples":["A service accepts JSON from users and crashes on a request body made of tens of thousands of opening brackets, taking the worker down every time.","The json.org test suite includes a file nested 20 levels deep that its own checker rejects as too deep, while RFC 8259 parsers may accept it: the depth limit is an implementation choice.","XML and YAML parsers face the same problem with deeply nested elements."],"misconceptions":["People think file size limits protect them. A tiny file can still be extremely deep.","People think the stack is as large as memory. It is a small, fixed region, far smaller than the heap.","People think any depth limit breaks the standard. RFC 8259 permits limits on depth."],"elsewhere":["Any recursive algorithm over untrusted input, such as walking a directory tree or evaluating nested expressions.","Web servers and API gateways that set limits on request depth and size.","Compilers that limit how deeply expressions or blocks may nest."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('string-escapes', 'Escapes in strings', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Inside a quoted string, an escape such as a backslash followed by n or a double quote stands for a character that could not be written plainly. JSON''s backslash-u escape names a UTF-16 code unit, so a character beyond U+FFFF is written as two escapes, a surrogate pair.', '{"how_it_works":"A string in a text format needs a way to hold the characters that would otherwise end it or be invisible. JSON strings sit between double quotes, so a double quote inside the string is written as a backslash followed by a double quote, and a backslash itself is written as two backslashes. Control characters, the code points from U+0000 to U+001F such as newline and tab, must not appear raw inside a JSON string at all; they have to be written as escapes such as backslash n or backslash t.\n\nThe most general escape is a backslash, the letter u and exactly four hexadecimal digits, which names one UTF-16 code unit. That covers every character up to U+FFFF directly. A character above that, such as most emoji, needs two such escapes in a row: a high surrogate followed by a low surrogate. A parser that builds real text has to recognise the pair and join the two halves into one code point. A lone high or low surrogate with no partner is not a real character, and RFC 8259 notes that parsers behave unpredictably when they meet one.\n\nA lexer for strings therefore runs a small state machine: inside the string, a backslash switches to an escape state that accepts only the allowed letters, and a u starts a run of four hex digits. Anything else, such as a backslash followed by x, or a raw tab, makes the string invalid.","examples":["The grinning face emoji is written in an escaped JSON string as two backslash-u escapes, D83D followed by DE00, and a correct parser turns them into one character.","A log line containing a raw tab inside a JSON string is rejected by strict parsers because control characters must be escaped.","Two services disagree about a username because one parser replaces a lone surrogate escape with U+FFFD and the other keeps it, so the same JSON means two different strings."],"misconceptions":["People think backslash-u can write any code point. It writes one 16-bit unit, so characters above U+FFFF need a surrogate pair.","People think any backslash sequence is allowed, as in many programming languages. JSON allows only eight escape letters plus the u form.","People think non-ASCII characters must be escaped. JSON allows them raw, in UTF-8; escaping is optional for them."],"elsewhere":["String literals in programming languages, URLs with percent-encoding, and HTML entities all solve the same problem in different ways.","Logging pipelines that escape newlines so one event stays on one line.","Security bugs where an escape is decoded twice or not at all."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('parse-error-reporting', 'Reporting parse errors', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A good parser stops at the first token the grammar does not allow and says where it is, by line and column, what it expected and what it found, so a person can fix the input quickly.', '{"how_it_works":"Rejecting invalid input is only half the job; the other half is telling a person how to fix it. Because a recursive descent parser always knows which tokens are allowed next, the first token that does not fit is a precise place to stop. A useful message has three parts: where the problem is, what the parser expected at that point, and what it actually found. For example, after the key name inside an object the parser expected a colon and found a comma.\n\nWhere means a position a person can use. Byte offsets are easy for programs, but people think in lines and columns, so the lexer records the line and column where each token starts. Counting columns has its own subtlety: a column can be counted in bytes, in code points or in what a person sees as characters, and they differ for non-ASCII text. Editors and tools should agree on which one they report.\n\nThe position that is reported should be where the rule broke, not where the program noticed. A missing closing bracket is only discovered at the end of the input, so a helpful parser also says which opening bracket was never closed. Messages should quote a little of the input but never echo huge or sensitive values, since error messages often end up in logs.","examples":["Invalid JSON at line 12, column 7: expected a colon after the key, found a comma.","A configuration loader that only says invalid JSON makes a person bisect a 2,000-line file by hand.","An API that returns the exact position of a syntax error in a request body lets the client developer fix it in one try."],"misconceptions":["People think the error position is where the program failed. It should be where the input first broke the rules.","People think one message per error is always possible. After the first error the parser''s view of the input is uncertain, which is why simple parsers stop at the first error.","People think columns are obviously counted in characters. Tools differ between bytes, code points and visible characters."],"elsewhere":["Compiler and interpreter error messages.","Form validation that points at the wrong field.","Linters and schema validators for configuration files."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('strict-vs-lenient-parsing', 'Strict and lenient parsing', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'A strict parser accepts exactly what the specification allows. A lenient one also accepts common mistakes such as trailing commas or comments. Leniency feels friendly, but when two systems parse the same input differently it causes interoperability and security bugs.', '{"how_it_works":"Every parser makes choices at the edges of the specification. Some choices are about mistakes: should a trailing comma, a comment, single quotes or an unquoted key be rejected or quietly accepted? Others are about places the specification leaves open: when an object has the same key twice, should the first value win, the last one, or should it be an error? How large a number, how long a string, how deep a nesting will be accepted?\n\nThe old advice known as the robustness principle said be conservative in what you send and liberal in what you accept. Experience has shown the cost of the liberal half. When one component accepts input that another rejects, or reads it differently, the two systems no longer agree on what the data means. An attacker can then craft one document that a security check reads one way and the service behind it reads another way. Duplicate keys are the classic case: a gateway checks the first value of a role key while the application uses the last one.\n\nThe practical approach is to be strict by default, to reject what the specification forbids, and to make every remaining choice deliberate and documented: which value wins for duplicate keys, the depth limit, and what happens to numbers beyond the supported precision. Extensions such as comments belong in an explicitly named format, such as JSON5, not silently in a JSON parser.","examples":["A permissions check reads the first role key in a request and the application reads the last one, so a request with role user and then role admin passes the check and runs as admin.","A configuration file with a trailing comma works in one tool and fails in another, and nobody can tell which tool is right without the specification.","The json.org checker accepts some numbers the grammar forbids and rejects some it allows, which shows that even reference tools need testing."],"misconceptions":["People think accepting more input is always kinder. It moves the error to a later, more confusing place, or to a different system.","People think strictness is only pedantry. Disagreement between parsers is a known source of security bugs.","People think the specification answers every question. RFC 8259 leaves duplicate keys, number limits and depth limits to the implementation."],"elsewhere":["HTTP request smuggling, where a proxy and a server disagree about where a request ends.","Email and URL parsing differences between libraries.","File formats such as ZIP, where two readers can see different contents in the same file."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('client-identity', 'Identifying the client to limit', 'apis-backends', 'foundation', null, null, 'A rate limit is only as fair as the key it counts by. An IP address is easy to get but shared by many users behind one network, hidden behind proxies, and forgeable in headers; an API key or user account identifies the caller far better.', '{"how_it_works":"Every limiter counts something per key: per IP address, per API key, per user account, per session or a combination. The key decides who shares a limit, and so whether the limit is fair and whether it can be escaped.\n\nThe IP address is the easiest key because every request carries one, and it is the only choice for anonymous traffic. But it is weak in three ways. First, many people can share one public address: an office, a school, a mobile carrier or a VPN can put thousands of users behind it, so they all share one limit and one heavy user blocks the rest. Second, when the server sits behind a load balancer, CDN or reverse proxy, the address it sees is the proxy''s, so every client collapses into a single bucket unless the real address is recovered. Third, the headers that carry the original address, such as X-Forwarded-For or Forwarded, are just text the client can write, so they can be trusted only when a proxy you control sets them.\n\nWhen the caller is known, a key tied to them is better: an API key, an account or a tenant. It follows the caller across networks and lets limits depend on their plan. Many systems use the IP for unauthenticated endpoints such as login, and the account everywhere else.\n\nThe opposite mistake is a key that is too broad, for example a counter keyed only on the window and not the client, so that all clients share one limit.","examples":["A university''s students all appear from one public address and keep hitting a per-IP limit meant for single users.","A server behind a load balancer limits by the address it sees, which is the load balancer''s, and ends up rate-limiting the whole internet as one client.","An attacker sends a different made-up X-Forwarded-For value on each request to a server that trusts the header, and never hits the limit."],"misconceptions":["People think one IP address means one person. Networks routinely put many people behind one address, and one person can use many.","People think the X-Forwarded-For header is reliable. It is only as trustworthy as the proxy that set it."],"elsewhere":["Fraud detection and abuse prevention face the same question of who a request really comes from.","Analytics that count unique visitors by address undercount offices and overcount mobile users.","Logging and auditing behind proxies need the real client address recovered the same way."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('rate-limit-responses', 'Telling clients they are limited', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'A rejected request should get the HTTP status 429 Too Many Requests, ideally with a Retry-After header saying how long to wait, so programs can tell a limit apart from success or a server failure and back off.', '{"how_it_works":"Programs that call an API make decisions from the status code, not from the words in the body. If a limiter rejects a request with 200 OK and a message such as \"slow down\", every program treats it as success, keeps going at full speed and may even store the message as if it were data. The limit then protects nothing and confuses everyone.\n\nHTTP has a status code for this case: 429 Too Many Requests, defined in RFC 6585. It says the client has sent too many requests in a given time. Like other 4xx codes it puts the cause on the client, which separates it from 503 Service Unavailable, which says the server itself is overloaded or down for everyone. A 429 must not be cached, because it describes a moment, not the resource.\n\nThe response may include a Retry-After header, defined in HTTP''s core semantics, giving either a number of seconds or a date after which the client may try again. Well-behaved clients wait that long instead of retrying at once, which matters because immediate retries make the overload worse. Many APIs also send headers describing the quota itself, such as how many requests remain and when the window resets, so clients can slow down before they are blocked; the IETF is standardising these as RateLimit fields, but they are still a draft.\n\nOn the client side the matching habit is back-off: wait as told, or wait longer after each rejection, often with a little randomness so many clients do not all retry at the same instant.","examples":["A script calling a code-hosting API reads a 429 with Retry-After of 30, sleeps 30 seconds and continues without errors.","An API that returned 200 with an error message for limits found that clients cached the message and showed it to users for hours.","Thousands of phones that all retried at once after an outage were spread out by adding random delays to their back-off."],"misconceptions":["People think the body explains the error well enough. Programs act on the status code and headers.","People think 429 and 503 mean the same. 429 is about one client''s rate; 503 is about the server''s own state."],"elsewhere":["Every major cloud and SaaS API returns 429 and documents its retry behaviour.","HTTP client libraries often have built-in retry with back-off that reads Retry-After.","Message queues and email servers use similar ''try again later'' signals."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('durability-and-fsync', 'Durability, fsync and the append-only log', 'state-storage', 'foundation', null, null, 'A write normally lands in the operating system''s memory, not on disk, so a power cut can lose it. Forcing a flush with fsync makes data durable at a cost in speed, and an append-only log flushed every write or every second bounds what a crash can lose.', '{"how_it_works":"When a program writes to a file, the operating system usually copies the bytes into its page cache in memory and reports success at once. It writes them to the disk later, in the background. This makes writes fast, but if the machine loses power before that background write, the data is gone even though the program was told the write succeeded. A process crash alone does not lose it, because the operating system still holds the cache; a power cut or a kernel crash does.\n\nDurability means that once a write is confirmed, it survives such failures. The system call fsync asks the operating system to push a file''s data to stable storage and waits until the device reports it done. Only after fsync returns is the data safe from a power cut. The cost is time: a flush waits for the disk, which takes from well under a millisecond on a fast drive to many milliseconds on a slow one, so calling it after every write slows a busy server down.\n\nRedis offers a second kind of persistence for this, the append-only file. Every write command is appended to a log as it happens, and on restart the log is replayed to rebuild the store. How often the log is flushed is a setting: after every write, which is the safest and slowest; once a second, which risks about a second of writes; or never, leaving it to the operating system, which is fastest. Because the log grows, it is periodically rewritten into a compact form.\n\nSnapshots and logs trade off differently: a snapshot is compact but loses everything since it was taken, while a log loses at most what was not yet flushed.","examples":["A server replies OK to SAVE, and a power cut a second later leaves an empty or partial file because the bytes were still in memory.","A payment service flushes its log after every write and accepts the slower writes, because losing a confirmed payment is not acceptable.","A cache uses the once-a-second setting and accepts losing up to a second of writes in exchange for speed."],"misconceptions":["People think a successful write means the data is on disk. It usually means the data is in the operating system''s memory.","People think a crash of the program loses unflushed writes. A process crash usually does not, since the operating system still holds them; a power cut does.","People think flushing after every write is free. Each flush waits for the storage device, so it can cut throughput sharply."],"elsewhere":["Databases write a log ahead of changing their data files and flush it at commit, which is why a committed transaction survives a crash.","File systems keep a journal so they can recover a consistent state after a power cut.","Message queues let you choose whether a message is confirmed before or after it is flushed to disk."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('request-pipelining', 'Pipelining requests', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'Waiting for each reply before sending the next request costs a full round trip per request. Pipelining sends many requests at once and reads the replies afterwards, in order, so the round trip is paid once per batch.', '{"how_it_works":"Every request over a network pays a round trip: the time for the request to reach the server and the reply to come back. Inside one data centre that is a fraction of a millisecond, but between continents it can be over a hundred milliseconds. A client that sends one command, waits for the reply, then sends the next spends most of its time waiting, so 1,000 commands cost 1,000 round trips however fast the server is.\n\nPipelining removes that waiting. The client writes many commands to the connection one after another without reading anything, then reads all the replies. The round trip is paid once for the whole batch, and the server also does less work per command, because it handles one large read and one large write instead of many tiny ones. Throughput can rise by several times.\n\nFor this to work the server must do two things. It must parse every complete command that arrives in one read, rather than assuming one read holds one command. And it must send the replies in exactly the order the commands arrived, because the client matches replies to commands by position, not by any id.\n\nPipelining is not a transaction. Commands from other clients can still run between the commands of a batch, and if one command fails the others still run. Very large batches also make the server hold many replies in memory before the client reads them, so clients usually send batches of a sensible size.","examples":["Loading 100,000 keys into a remote store one by one takes minutes; sent in pipelined batches it takes seconds.","redis-benchmark with the pipeline option set to 16 reports several times more requests per second than without it on the same server.","HTTP/1.1 allowed pipelining but replies still had to come back in order, so one slow reply delayed the rest, which later protocols fixed."],"misconceptions":["People think a faster server fixes slow remote commands. When the time goes on round trips, only fewer round trips help.","People think pipelined commands run together atomically. Other clients'' commands can run in between, and each command succeeds or fails on its own.","People think the server needs special support to receive a pipeline. It only needs to parse several commands from one read and answer them in order."],"elsewhere":["Database drivers batch inserts so many rows travel in one round trip.","HTTP/2 multiplexes many requests on one connection, removing the in-order delay of HTTP/1.1 pipelining.","Web pages bundle many small files into fewer requests to cut round trips on slow mobile networks."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('flow-control-backpressure', 'Flow control and backpressure', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'When a receiver reads more slowly than a sender writes, buffers fill and the sender is forced to slow down. TCP does this with its receive window, and programs feel it as writes that block or would block. Handling it, rather than buffering without limit, keeps a server alive.', '{"how_it_works":"Every TCP connection has buffers at both ends. Bytes a program writes go into its send buffer, travel to the other machine, and wait in that machine''s receive buffer until the receiving program reads them. The receiver keeps telling the sender how much room is left in its receive buffer, the receive window, and the sender may only have that much unacknowledged data in flight.\n\nIf the receiving program stops reading, its receive buffer fills and the window shrinks to zero. The sender can no longer transmit, so its own send buffer fills too. From then on the sending program''s write cannot complete: a blocking write waits until space frees up, and a non-blocking write reports that it would block. The sender has been slowed to the receiver''s pace. That pressure flowing backwards from a slow consumer to a fast producer is called backpressure.\n\nBackpressure is a feature, but a server must respect it. A server that keeps reading from one side and queueing what it cannot write in its own memory turns a slow client into unbounded memory growth. A thread blocked on a write to a client that never reads is a thread lost. Good servers stop reading from a source while they cannot write to its destination, cap how much they buffer per connection, and use timeouts so a peer that never drains is eventually disconnected.\n\nThe same idea appears wherever data flows between parts that run at different speeds: pipes between programs, message queues, streams in a program, and protocols that carry flow-control windows of their own.","examples":["An echo server whose client sends a large file but never reads the echoes: after a few hundred kilobytes the server''s writes stop going through, and a naive server either blocks that thread forever or keeps queueing the echoes in memory.","A shell pipeline where the last program is slow: the earlier programs block on writes to the pipe and pause instead of filling memory.","A proxy that downloads from a fast server and sends to a phone on a slow network must pause reading from the server, or it will hold the whole download in memory."],"misconceptions":["A write that returned has delivered the data. It only means the data is in the local send buffer.","Bigger buffers solve slow consumers. They only delay the moment the buffers fill.","Backpressure is an error. It is the normal signal that the other side needs time."],"elsewhere":["Stream libraries in most languages expose backpressure, for example a write that reports the buffer is full and a later signal that it has drained.","Message queues apply it by refusing or delaying producers when consumers fall behind.","HTTP/2 and QUIC carry their own per-stream flow-control windows on top of the connection."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('name-resolution', 'Turning names into addresses', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'Before a program can connect to a host name it must turn the name into IP addresses, through the hosts file and the system''s DNS resolver. A name can have several addresses, IPv6 and IPv4, and how a client tries them affects speed and reliability.', '{"how_it_works":"Sockets connect to IP addresses, not names. When a program is given a host name, it asks the operating system''s resolver to translate it. The resolver checks local sources first, such as the hosts file and a cache, and otherwise sends a DNS query to a recursive resolver, usually run by the network or a public service, which does the full lookup and returns the answer.\n\nThe answer is often a list. A name can have several IPv4 addresses for load spreading and several IPv6 addresses as well. The resolver orders them by rules about which address families work best, and a well-written client tries them in turn: if connecting to the first fails or takes too long, it moves to the next. Modern clients race IPv6 and IPv4 attempts a fraction of a second apart, a technique called Happy Eyeballs, so a broken IPv6 path does not cost the user a long timeout.\n\nResolution takes time and can fail separately from connecting. A name may not exist, the resolver may be unreachable, or the answer may be cached and out of date. Errors should say which step failed, because \"could not resolve host\" and \"connection refused\" point to very different problems.\n\nBecause answers carry a time to live, resolvers cache them. That is what makes the second lookup of a name instant, and also why a changed address can take a while to reach everyone.","examples":["A command-line tool hangs for twenty seconds on a server but not on a laptop because the server gets an IPv6 address first and has no working IPv6 route, so every connection waits for a timeout before trying IPv4.","A developer adds a line to the hosts file to point a production name at a test machine, and forgets to remove it.","An outage at a public DNS service makes many sites unreachable even though their servers are fine."],"misconceptions":["A host name maps to exactly one address. It often maps to several, of both families.","Every lookup goes out to the internet. Most are answered from caches on the machine or at the resolver.","If the name resolves, the server is up. Resolution and connection are separate steps that fail separately."],"elsewhere":["Browsers and HTTP clients keep their own DNS caches and connection pools keyed by host.","Service discovery in clusters works by answering name lookups with the addresses of healthy instances.","Content delivery networks answer the same name with different addresses depending on where the user is."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('chunked-transfer-encoding', 'Chunked transfer encoding', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'HTTP/1.1 can send a body whose total length is not known in advance as a series of chunks, each preceded by its size in hexadecimal and ended by a chunk of size zero. A client must strip the size lines and join the data.', '{"how_it_works":"An HTTP/1.1 reply needs a way to say where its body ends while the connection stays open. Content-Length gives the exact number of bytes, but a server that generates its output on the fly may not know that number when it starts sending. Chunked transfer encoding solves this. The server sends the header Transfer-Encoding: chunked and then the body as a sequence of chunks.\n\nEach chunk starts with a line holding its size in hexadecimal, followed by a carriage return and line feed, then exactly that many bytes of data, then another carriage return and line feed. The body ends with a chunk of size zero, which may be followed by trailer headers and a final empty line. So a chunk line reading 1f8 announces 504 bytes of data.\n\nA client reading such a body loops: read a size line, convert it from hexadecimal, read exactly that many bytes, read the line ending, and stop at size zero. The size lines are framing, not content, so they must never be printed as part of the body. The bytes of one chunk can still arrive across several reads, which is why the reader must count bytes rather than rely on read boundaries.\n\nChunked encoding and Content-Length must not be combined. Servers and proxies that disagree about which one wins have been tricked into reading one request as two, an attack called request smuggling. HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 do not use chunked encoding; they frame data in their own way.","examples":["A search page that streams results as it finds them sends each batch as a chunk, so the browser can start showing results before the search finishes.","A hand-written HTTP client prints stray lines such as 1f8 and 0 around the JSON it fetched, because it printed the chunk sizes along with the data.","A server that compresses its output on the fly cannot know the compressed size in advance, so it uses chunked encoding."],"misconceptions":["The chunk size is in decimal. It is hexadecimal.","One chunk arrives in one read. A chunk can be split across reads, and one read can hold several chunks.","A body is either chunked or has no end. Content-Length and closing the connection are the other two ways to end one."],"elsewhere":["Streaming APIs, including servers that stream model output, use chunked replies over HTTP/1.1.","Proxies must rewrite or pass through the encoding correctly, and bugs here cause request smuggling.","The same length-prefixed framing idea appears in many binary protocols."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('tls-basics', 'TLS: encrypted, authenticated connections', 'networking-http', 'foundation', null, null, 'TLS sits between TCP and HTTP. A handshake agrees on keys and lets the client check the server''s certificate before any request is sent, and then every byte is encrypted and protected against tampering. https is HTTP over TLS, normally on port 443.', '{"how_it_works":"Plain HTTP travels as readable text, so anyone on the path, such as a shared Wi-Fi network or a compromised router, can read the request, the reply and any password or cookie, and can change them. TLS fixes this by adding a layer between the TCP connection and the HTTP messages.\n\nWhen the TCP connection opens, client and server first run a handshake. The client says which host it wants, using an extension called server name indication, because one address can serve many sites. The server sends its certificate, which binds its name to a public key and is signed by a certificate authority. The client checks that the signature chains up to an authority it trusts, that the certificate is still valid, and that the name matches the host it meant to reach. The two sides then agree on fresh secret keys. In TLS 1.3 this takes one round trip before data can flow.\n\nAfter the handshake, every byte of HTTP is encrypted and carries an integrity check, so an eavesdropper sees only sizes and timing, and any change is detected. The request itself is unchanged: the same request line and headers, just sent inside the encrypted channel.\n\nCertificate checking is the part that gives security. Encryption with a server whose identity was not checked only protects you from everyone except the attacker you are talking to. Turning off verification to make an error go away removes the protection entirely.","examples":["A user on café Wi-Fi logs in to a plain-http site, and anyone on the same network can read the password from the traffic.","A tool that speaks plain HTTP on port 80 cannot fetch an https URL: the server on port 443 expects a TLS handshake first, not a request line.","An expired certificate makes browsers show a full-page warning, because the server can no longer prove who it is."],"misconceptions":["TLS hides which site you visit. The server name is usually visible in the handshake, and the address always is.","Encryption alone makes a connection safe. Without checking the certificate, you may be encrypting to an attacker.","https changes the HTTP messages. They are the same messages, carried inside an encrypted channel."],"elsewhere":["Email, databases and message brokers use TLS for their own protocols.","Mutual TLS, where the client also presents a certificate, is common between services inside companies.","Let''s Encrypt issues free certificates automatically, which is why most sites now use https."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('dns-caching-ttl', 'Caching and time to live', 'state-storage', 'foundation', null, null, 'Every DNS record carries a time to live in seconds. Resolvers keep and reuse a record until it expires, which makes lookups fast and also delays changes. Negative answers are cached too.', '{"how_it_works":"Looking a name up from the root every time would be slow and would overload the root and top-level servers. Instead, every resource record carries a time to live, a number of seconds chosen by the owner of the zone. A resolver that receives a record may keep it in its cache and answer later questions from the cache until that many seconds have passed. When it hands out a cached record it reduces the TTL by the time already spent in the cache.\n\nCaching happens at several levels: in the recursive resolver, often in the operating system, and in applications such as browsers. Referral records are cached too, so after one lookup under com a resolver no longer needs the root for other com names. Negative answers, saying a name does not exist, are cached for a time taken from the zone''s settings, so a typo is not looked up again and again.\n\nThe price is that changes spread slowly. If a record has a TTL of a day and its address changes, resolvers that cached it keep handing out the old address until their copy expires. Teams planning a move lower the TTL well in advance, at least one old TTL before the change, make the change, and raise the TTL again afterwards.\n\nA short TTL means fresher answers and more queries; a long one means fewer queries and slower changes. Values from a minute to a day are common.","examples":["A company moves its website to a new server, and for hours some customers keep reaching the old one because their resolvers cached the old address with a long TTL.","A resolver that has just looked up one name under com answers the next com name faster, because it cached the referral to the com servers.","Someone mistypes a domain, then registers it, and finds it does not work for a while because the \"does not exist\" answer was cached."],"misconceptions":["Changing a DNS record takes effect at once. Caches keep the old record until its TTL runs out.","Only positive answers are cached. Answers saying a name does not exist are cached too.","The TTL is set by the resolver. It is set by the zone owner on each record."],"elsewhere":["Every cache, from HTTP to databases, faces the same trade-off between freshness and load.","Content delivery networks use short TTLs so they can move traffic between locations quickly.","Service discovery in clusters often relies on very short DNS TTLs so clients notice when instances come and go."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('information-entropy', 'Information and entropy', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Entropy measures how unpredictable each symbol of a source is, in bits. It sets the lower limit on how small any lossless code can make the data on average, and it is why skewed text compresses and random bytes do not.', '{"how_it_works":"A symbol that is very likely carries little information when it appears, and a rare one carries a lot. Information theory makes this exact: a symbol with probability p carries minus log base 2 of p bits of information. Entropy is the average of that over all symbols, weighted by how often each one appears.\n\nIf every one of 256 byte values is equally likely, the entropy is 8 bits per byte, and no lossless code can beat plain bytes on average. Real text is far from uniform: spaces and the letter e are common, and q and z are rare, so its entropy is well under 8 bits per character. That gap between 8 bits and the entropy is the room a compressor has to work in.\n\nShannon''s source coding theorem says no lossless code can use fewer bits per symbol, on average, than the entropy of the source, and that codes can come close to it. A classic example has six symbols with frequencies 45, 13, 12, 16, 9 and 5 out of 100. A fixed code needs 3 bits for each of them, the entropy is about 2.22 bits, and a Huffman code reaches 2.24.\n\nEntropy depends on the model of the data. Counting single bytes ignores that th is common in English. Compressors that look at pairs, words or repeated strings model the data better, and so find a lower entropy to approach.","examples":["Compressing an already compressed file, such as a JPEG or a zip, barely shrinks it, because its bytes are close to uniformly random.","A log file full of repeated timestamps and field names compresses to a small fraction of its size, because its symbols are highly predictable.","Encrypted data looks random by design, so a backup tool that compresses after encrypting saves almost nothing."],"misconceptions":["A good enough algorithm can compress any file. Counting arguments show that no lossless method can shrink every input; some must grow.","Entropy is a property of the file alone. It depends on the model used to predict the symbols, so a better model finds a lower entropy.","Compression ratio is the same for every text. It depends on how skewed and repetitive that particular text is."],"elsewhere":["Password strength is measured in bits of entropy, the same idea applied to guessing.","Decision trees in machine learning pick splits that reduce entropy the most.","Video and audio codecs spend more bits where the signal is less predictable."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('false-positive-rate', 'Sizing a filter for a false positive rate', 'data-stores', 'foundation', null, null, 'A Bloom filter trades memory for accuracy. Given the number of items and the false positive rate you accept, standard formulas give the number of bits and hash functions, and the rate rises sharply if the filter holds more items than it was sized for.', '{"how_it_works":"Each added item sets up to k bits. As more items are added, more of the m bits are one, and a lookup for an item that was never added is more likely to find all of its k bits already set. That chance is the false positive rate.\n\nFor n items and a target rate p, the best number of bits is about n times the natural log of p, made positive, divided by the square of the natural log of 2. That works out to about 9.6 bits per item for 1 in 100, and about 14.4 bits per item for 1 in 1,000. The best number of hash functions is the bits per item times the natural log of 2, so about 7 for 1 in 100 and about 10 for 1 in 1,000. For 100,000 words at 1 in 100 that is about 958,500 bits, under 120 kilobytes.\n\nMore hash functions are not always better. Too few leave too many items matching by chance, and too many fill the array with ones faster. The formula balances the two.\n\nThe rate holds only if the filter holds about the number of items it was sized for. Put in twice as many and the rate is far worse than twice as bad. Measure the rate you actually get, by looking up many items you know were never added.","examples":["A team sized a filter for 1 million keys, then loaded 5 million, and the filter answered probably present for almost everything.","Halving the false positive rate from 1 in 100 to 1 in 200 costs only about 1.4 more bits per item.","A spell checker tested with random strings measured close to the 1 percent rate its sizing promised."],"misconceptions":["More hash functions always lower the error. Past the best number they raise it.","The false positive rate is fixed once chosen. It depends on how many items have actually been added.","Halving the error rate doubles the size. The size grows with the logarithm of the rate, so it grows much more slowly."],"elsewhere":["Cache filters in databases are sized per data file from the number of keys in that file.","Capacity planning for hash tables uses the same idea of a load factor beyond which performance falls.","Probabilistic counters such as HyperLogLog trade memory for a known error in the same way."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('csv-quoting', 'CSV quoting', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'In CSV a field that contains a comma, a double quote or a line break is wrapped in double quotes, and a quote inside it is doubled. Splitting a CSV line at every comma therefore breaks real data; parsing it needs a small state machine.', '{"how_it_works":"CSV looks like delimited text with commas, but the common form, written down in RFC 4180, adds quoting so that any value can be stored. A field that contains a comma, a double quote or a line break is wrapped in double quotes, and each double quote inside such a field is written twice. So the title Hello, World is stored as \"Hello, World\", and the value He said \"hi\" is stored as \"He said \"\"hi\"\"\".\n\nThat one rule changes how the file must be read. Splitting a line at every comma cuts \"Hello, World\" into two fields, and because a quoted field may contain a line break, one record can span several physical lines, so even reading line by line is wrong for general CSV. A correct reader walks the characters with a little state: outside quotes a comma ends a field, inside quotes a comma is data, and a pair of quotes inside quotes stands for one quote.\n\nReal CSV files also disagree with each other. Some use semicolons because the comma is the decimal separator in their locale, some start with a byte order mark, some quote every field and some none. That is why tools built for plain delimited text, such as cut, are the wrong tool for CSV with quoted fields, and why most languages ship a CSV library: the format is simple to write and easy to read wrongly.","examples":["Cutting the first field of a song list prints \"Hello instead of the title Hello, World, because cut splits inside the quotes.","An address field with a line break in it makes a naive importer see two half-records and reject both.","A file from a European spreadsheet uses semicolons, and every row arrives as one huge field."],"misconceptions":["CSV is just text split at commas. Quoted fields may contain commas, quotes and line breaks.","One line is one record. A quoted field can span several lines.","There is one CSV format. RFC 4180 describes the common one, but delimiters, quoting and headers vary between producers."],"elsewhere":["Spreadsheet exports, bank statements and data science pipelines all hit the same quoting rules.","Injection attacks through CSV files opened in spreadsheets rely on fields that start with an equals sign.","Log formats and other text protocols face the same choice between escaping and quoting."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('regex-backtracking', 'Backtracking versus linear-time matching', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Many regex engines try alternatives one by one and back up when they fail. For some patterns and inputs that takes exponential time, which has taken down real services. Automaton-based engines avoid it by tracking all possibilities at once.', '{"how_it_works":"There are two ways to run a regular expression. A backtracking engine tries one way of matching, and when it fails partway it backs up to the last choice it made and tries the next alternative. This is simple and supports features such as backreferences, and on ordinary patterns it is fast. But a pattern with nested or overlapping repetition, such as a group that repeats something that itself repeats, gives the engine exponentially many ways to split the same text. When the text almost matches but fails at the very end, the engine tries every split before giving up, and a few dozen characters can keep it busy for minutes.\n\nAn automaton-based engine, in the style of Thompson''s construction, never backs up. It reads each character once and keeps the set of states that are still possible, so its time grows only linearly with the text, whatever the pattern. The price is that it cannot support backreferences, which need memory of what was matched earlier. Tools such as GNU grep, RE2 and Rust''s regex crate take this route; many language standard libraries use backtracking.\n\nThis matters for security as well as speed. If an attacker controls the text, and the service uses a backtracking engine with a vulnerable pattern, one short request can pin a processor; this is called regular expression denial of service, or ReDoS. Cloudflare''s global outage on 2 July 2019 was caused by one such pattern in a firewall rule. The defences are to use a linear-time engine for untrusted input, to avoid nested repetition, and to set time limits.","examples":["A web firewall rule with a pattern containing two adjacent wildcard repetitions pinned every CPU core at Cloudflare in July 2019.","An email-validation pattern with nested repetition hangs a sign-up form when an attacker submits a long string that almost matches.","Stack Overflow went down briefly in 2016 when a pattern for trimming whitespace met a line with tens of thousands of spaces."],"misconceptions":["Regex speed depends only on the input size. With a backtracking engine it can depend exponentially on the pattern and input together.","All engines behave the same. Linear-time engines and backtracking engines accept similar syntax but have very different worst cases.","Only huge inputs are dangerous. A few dozen characters are enough for a vulnerable pattern."],"elsewhere":["Request validation, log parsing and search features in web services all face this risk.","Linting tools flag regex patterns with nested quantifiers for the same reason.","Database full-text and pattern search choose engines with predictable worst cases."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('sort-stability', 'Stable sorting', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'A stable sort keeps items that compare equal in the order they arrived. That lets you sort by several keys in passes, and it matters whenever equal-looking items are not really identical.', '{"how_it_works":"A sort is stable when items that compare as equal come out in the same relative order they went in. If two lines have the same sort key but differ elsewhere, a stable sort keeps the first of them first.\n\nStability matters whenever the comparison looks at only part of an item. Sorting people by last name, a stable sort keeps their original order within each last name, so if the list was already sorted by first name, the result is sorted by last name and then first name. This is how multi-key sorting is done in passes: sort by the least important key first, then by each more important key with a stable sort, and the earlier orders survive inside ties. With an unstable sort the order inside ties is arbitrary and can change from run to run or between library versions, which shows up as flaky tests and reports that reorder themselves.\n\nMerge sort and insertion sort are naturally stable; quicksort and heapsort, in their usual in-place forms, are not. Any sort can be made stable by adding the original position as a final tie-breaker. Tools expose this too: sort compares whole lines by default and, when keys tie, falls back to comparing the whole line, which hides stability; with a key option and the stable flag it keeps input order among equal keys instead. A sort that removes duplicates also has to decide which of several equal items it keeps.","examples":["A table sorted by date and then by customer loses its date order inside each customer when the second sort is unstable.","A snapshot test fails at random because two records with the same score swap places between runs.","A leaderboard sorted by score keeps earlier achievers ahead of later ones only because the sort is stable."],"misconceptions":["Equal items are identical, so order among them cannot matter. They are equal only on the key being compared.","All built-in sorts are stable. Many in-place sorts are not, and documentation says which.","Stability is only about duplicates. It is about any items whose keys compare equal."],"elsewhere":["Spreadsheet column sorting and database ORDER BY with several columns rely on stable tie handling.","User interfaces that sort tables by clicked column expect earlier orders to persist inside ties.","Version control and build tools sort file lists stably to keep output reproducible."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('myers-diff', 'The Myers diff algorithm', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'Myers'' algorithm finds a shortest edit script in time proportional to the file size times the number of differences. It is fast when files are similar, which is the usual case, and it is the default in GNU diff and Git.', '{"how_it_works":"The table method for diff costs the product of the two file lengths, which is hopeless for large files even when they differ in only a few lines. Eugene Myers'' 1986 algorithm takes a different view. Picture a grid with the old file along one side and the new file along the other; moving right deletes an old line, moving down inserts a new line, and where the two lines are equal you may move diagonally for free. A shortest edit script is a path from the top-left corner to the bottom-right with the fewest non-diagonal moves.\n\nThe algorithm searches outward by number of edits. With zero edits it follows the diagonal as far as the lines match. With one edit it tries one step right or down and then follows the free diagonal again, keeping only the furthest point reached on each diagonal line. It repeats for two edits, three, and so on, and stops as soon as some path reaches the corner. Because it only explores paths with at most D edits, its time is proportional to the total length times D, the size of the difference. For two versions of a file that differ in a handful of lines, that is close to linear, and a refinement needs only linear memory.\n\nThat is why Myers'' algorithm, or a variation of it, is the default in GNU diff and Git. It is not always the most readable: Git also offers patience and histogram diffs, which first anchor on lines that occur once in each file and often produce diffs that match how people edited the code.","examples":["Diffing two versions of a 100,000-line log that differ in ten lines is instant with Myers and needs gigabytes with the full table.","Git can show the same change with the default algorithm or with the patience option, and the second often lines up functions better.","Diffing two completely different files is the worst case, because the number of differences is as large as the files."],"misconceptions":["Diff always builds a table of every pair of lines. Practical diffs explore only paths with few edits.","A faster diff gives a different, worse answer. Myers finds a shortest edit script, the same length as the table method.","The shortest diff is always the most readable. Alternative algorithms trade a slightly longer diff for better alignment."],"elsewhere":["Git, GNU diff and most code review tools use Myers or its variants.","Text editors and IDEs compute the changed-lines gutter with the same algorithm.","Bioinformatics and plagiarism detection compare long sequences with related edit-graph searches."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('unified-diff-format', 'The unified diff format', 'formats-parsing', 'foundation', null, null, 'Unified diffs group changes into hunks with a header giving line ranges, three lines of unchanged context, and lines marked with a space, a minus or a plus. The context is what lets patch apply a diff to a file that has shifted slightly.', '{"how_it_works":"A unified diff starts with two header lines naming the old and new files, marked with three minus signs and three plus signs. The changes follow as hunks. Each hunk begins with a header such as @@ -12,7 +12,8 @@, meaning the hunk covers seven lines of the old file starting at line 12 and eight lines of the new file starting at line 12. Inside the hunk, a line starting with a space is unchanged context, a line starting with a minus was removed, and a line starting with a plus was added. By default each change carries three lines of context before and after it, and changes close together share one hunk.\n\nThe context is the clever part. A patch tool applying the diff does not trust the line numbers blindly: it looks for the context lines near the expected position, so a patch still applies when the target file has gained or lost a few lines elsewhere. If the context does not match, the hunk is rejected and written to a reject file for a person to resolve. The header counts must agree with the body, or the patch is treated as corrupt.\n\nCompared with the older normal format, which prints line-number commands and marks lines with less-than and greater-than signs, unified diffs are shorter and easier to read, because removals and additions sit side by side inside their surrounding lines. They are the format of patch files, Git output and code review tools. Special markers also matter: a line saying there is no newline at end of file records that the last line had no final newline.","examples":["A security fix mailed as a unified diff still applies to a slightly newer version of the file because its context lines are found a few lines further down.","A patch is rejected because someone edited one of its context lines; the reject file shows exactly which hunk to fix by hand.","A code review tool turns every pull request into unified hunks with context, so reviewers see each change in place."],"misconceptions":["The line numbers are enough to apply a patch. Patch tools rely on context and allow the file to have shifted.","Context lines are only for people. They are what lets the patch tool find the right place.","Unified and normal diffs carry different information. They describe the same edit script in different layouts."],"elsewhere":["Git, GitHub, GitLab and mailing-list patch workflows use the unified format.","Configuration management and infrastructure tools show planned changes as unified diffs.","Database schema migration tools and document comparison tools borrow the hunk layout."]}'::jsonb, 'generated'),
  ('tape-memory-model', 'A tape of cells as memory', 'design-structure', 'foundation', null, null, 'Brainfuck''s memory is a tape of byte cells and a pointer. The choices of cell size, wrapping, tape length and end-of-input behaviour are part of the language, and programs break when interpreters choose differently.', '{"how_it_works":"A memory model says what the program''s memory is and what happens at its limits. Brainfuck''s model is deliberately tiny: a row of cells, traditionally 30,000 of them, each starting at zero, and a data pointer that points at one cell. Commands move the pointer one cell left or right, add or subtract one from the current cell, read a byte of input into it or write it as output, and loop while it is non-zero. It is close to the tape of a Turing machine, which is why such a small language can still compute anything.\n\nBecause the original description left the limits open, interpreters differ, and those differences are part of the model. The first is cell size: most use one byte per cell, where adding one to 255 wraps round to 0 and subtracting one from 0 gives 255, but some use larger cells that do not wrap. Many programs depend on wrapping, for example to clear a cell by adding until it reaches zero. The second is the tape''s edges: moving left of the first cell or past the last can be an error, wrap to the other end, or grow the tape. The third is end of input: reading when no input is left may leave the cell unchanged, set it to 0, or set it to 255.\n\nA portable interpreter picks the most common conventions, thirty thousand wrapping byte cells and an error or growth at the edges, and documents each choice, so users know which programs it will run as their authors intended.","examples":["A program that clears a cell by adding one until it reaches zero runs forever on an interpreter whose cells do not wrap.","A program that reads input until a zero byte appears loops forever on an interpreter that leaves the cell unchanged at the end of input.","Integer overflow in other languages, where a counter wraps from the largest value to the smallest, is the same choice made for every number."],"misconceptions":["There is one correct Brainfuck behaviour. The original left cell size, tape ends and end of input open, so interpreters differ.","Wrapping is a bug to avoid. Many programs rely on it on purpose.","A larger tape is always better. Growing without limit lets a runaway program use all memory, so a limit can be a feature."],"elsewhere":["Every language defines what happens when a number overflows: wrapping, an error or a larger type.","Virtual machines define their memory model, such as how much memory a program may use and what happens past its end.","Turing machines, the theoretical model of computation, use the same idea of a tape and a head."]}'::jsonb, 'generated')
