update challenge set title = 'Build your own echo server', source_url = 'https://codingchallenges.fyi/challenges/challenge-echo', level = 'beginner',
  summary = 'A network server on port 7 that sends back every byte a client sends it, first for one TCP client, then for many at once, then over UDP with a command-line flag, and that shuts down cleanly without dropping echoes that are still on their way.', requirements = array['Listen on port 7 on every local address and log each connection it accepts', 'Read what a client sends and write the same bytes back until the client closes the connection', 'Serve several clients at the same time, so one slow client never holds up another', 'Run over UDP instead of TCP when started with a UDP flag, still on port 7', 'Shut down cleanly, finishing echoes in flight and closing every connection']::text[],
  restate_rubric = '[{"id":"r1","point":"It is a server on port 7 that sends back exactly what each client sends it"},{"id":"r2","point":"Over TCP it serves many clients at the same time and keeps echoing until each one closes its connection"},{"id":"r3","point":"A flag switches it to UDP, and it shuts down cleanly without losing echoes"}]'::jsonb, reflection_prompt = 'In your own words, what surprised you about how data really arrives over TCP and UDP, and what would you change in your server if it had to handle ten thousand clients?',
  primer = '{"what":"An echo server is the smallest useful network server: whatever a client sends, it sends straight back. The server listens on a port, the echo protocol uses port 7, and each client that connects gets its own connection to read from and write to. You will build one for TCP, make it serve many clients at once, add a UDP mode and make it stop cleanly.","why":"Almost every network program, from web servers to databases, is built on the same few socket calls. An echo server lets you learn them, and how TCP and UDP really deliver data, with nothing else in the way.","example":"In one terminal you start the server. In another you connect with netcat to localhost on port 7 and type good morning, then press enter. The server reads those 13 bytes, 12 letters and spaces plus the newline, and writes the same 13 bytes back, so the line appears twice on your screen: once as you typed it and once as the echo.","words":[{"term":"socket","meaning":"The handle a program uses to send and receive network data, much as a file handle is used to read a file."},{"term":"port","meaning":"A number from 0 to 65535 that picks out one service on a machine. The echo protocol uses port 7."},{"term":"listening socket","meaning":"A socket that only waits for clients. Each time it accepts one, it hands back a new socket for that client and keeps waiting."},{"term":"TCP","meaning":"A protocol that gives two programs a connection carrying a stream of bytes, delivered in order, with lost pieces resent."},{"term":"UDP","meaning":"A protocol that sends separate messages with no connection. A message can be lost, and nothing resends it."},{"term":"netcat","meaning":"A command-line tool, nc, that connects to a port and sends what you type, which makes it handy for testing servers."}]}'::jsonb, figure_kind = 'net',
  content_format = 3, reference_tool = 'netcat (nc) as the client, over TCP and over UDP with -u. Flags differ between netcat versions, so check yours.',
  done_when = 'The server echoes every byte back over TCP for several clients at once, survives a client that never reads, echoes datagrams over UDP to the right sender, reports startup failures on standard error with a non-zero exit status, and stops cleanly on Ctrl+C; and your notes explain, in your own words, the difference between a TCP byte stream and UDP datagrams.', open_checks = array['Whether listening on port 7 needs administrator rights on your system; use a high port such as 7007 while developing if it does.', 'Which netcat you have, and which flags it uses for UDP and for closing after the end of input.', 'How large a UDP datagram your system lets you send on the local machine.', 'How long your system keeps a closed connection''s port busy, and whether address reuse changes that.']::text[],
  content_status = 'generated', updated_at = now() where id = (select id from challenge where source = 'coding_challenges' and slug = 'challenge-echo');
delete from project_concept where challenge_id = (select id from challenge where source = 'coding_challenges' and slug = 'challenge-echo');

